1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:16,350
On this episode of<i> Chef & My Fridge,</i>

2
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:19,119
we've invited distinguished guests.

3
00:00:19,186 --> 00:00:22,522
We usually start the show
with updates from our chefs,

4
00:00:22,589 --> 00:00:24,892
but those are not important today.

5
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:26,593
We're not important at all.

6
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:31,064
The national athletes
who brought glory to our nation

7
00:00:31,131 --> 00:00:35,102
in the Aichi-Nagoya 2026 Asian Games
are waiting behind the panels right now.

8
00:00:35,168 --> 00:00:37,471
-We returned yesterday too.
-We did.

9
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:38,605
We were the commentators.

10
00:00:38,672 --> 00:00:39,940
-Let's invite them in now.
-Okay.

11
00:00:40,007 --> 00:00:43,577
Please welcome the fridge owners
of the Asian Games Special.

12
00:00:43,644 --> 00:00:45,879
Come on out!

13
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,916
Today's fridge owners are here.

14
00:00:49,416 --> 00:00:53,520
Please welcome the stars
of the Aichi-Nagoya 2026 Asian Games,

15
00:00:53,587 --> 00:00:56,623
Oh Sang-uk, Huh Mi-mi,
and Biwesa Daniel Kashama.

16
00:00:56,690 --> 00:00:57,858
Please say hello.

17
00:00:58,592 --> 00:00:59,927
He's so tall.

18
00:00:59,993 --> 00:01:01,295
-Hello.
-Whoa.

19
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:03,697
You have all those medals on you,

20
00:01:03,764 --> 00:01:05,265
-making you look more stunning.
-Hello.

21
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:06,566
Look at them.

22
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:09,403
-They're like jewelers.
-Jewelers?

23
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:11,872
First, the man sitting beside me.

24
00:01:11,939 --> 00:01:13,073
He's too good to be true.

25
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:16,543
The man who won two gold medals
at the Aichi-Nagoya Asian Games 2026.

26
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:20,013
The fencing emperor,
please welcome fencer Oh Sang-uk!

27
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,381
-Welcome.
-Hello.

28
00:01:21,448 --> 00:01:23,016
So magnificent.

29
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:24,618
-Please meet our viewers.
-GOAT fencer.

30
00:01:24,685 --> 00:01:26,687
Hello, I'm Oh Sang-uk, a fencer.

31
00:01:26,954 --> 00:01:29,122
WELCOME TO THE SHOW

32
00:01:29,189 --> 00:01:31,592
He's so good-looking too.

33
00:01:31,658 --> 00:01:33,560
Following Jung-hwan,

34
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:35,862
we've found yet another handsome athlete.

35
00:01:35,929 --> 00:01:38,098
-Fencers are better-looking.
-They're better?

36
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:40,167
THE MAN WITH THE SWORD WINS

37
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:41,435
I'll introduce the next guest.

38
00:01:41,501 --> 00:01:45,305
She took over the recent Asian Games
with her golden shoulder throw.

39
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:46,974
She may be named Mi-mi,

40
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,409
but her skills
are not to be taken as a meme.

41
00:01:49,476 --> 00:01:52,713
Please welcome the star
of the Korean judo scene, Huh Mi-mi!

42
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:54,314
-Huh Mi-mi!
-Hello.

43
00:01:54,481 --> 00:01:57,050
WELCOME

44
00:01:57,117 --> 00:01:59,853
Hello, I'm Huh Mi-mi, a judo athlete.

45
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,022
-Hello.
-Hello.

46
00:02:02,089 --> 00:02:03,323
It's great to have you.

47
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:07,260
Next, the most nervous one
among the three today.

48
00:02:07,327 --> 00:02:08,962
His mouth has gone dry.

49
00:02:09,029 --> 00:02:11,365
The bronze medalist
of the men's 400-meter relay

50
00:02:11,431 --> 00:02:13,967
in the recent
Aichi-Nagoya Asian Games 2026,

51
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:15,702
the Usain Bolt of Korea,

52
00:02:15,769 --> 00:02:18,105
please welcome Biwesa Daniel Kashama!

53
00:02:18,171 --> 00:02:19,406
Welcome!

54
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:20,607
A RISING TRACK STAR

55
00:02:21,274 --> 00:02:22,743
-He's so cool.
-Please say hello.

56
00:02:22,809 --> 00:02:25,145
I'm Biwesa Daniel Kashama,
a national track athlete.

57
00:02:25,212 --> 00:02:26,179
BOWING

58
00:02:26,413 --> 00:02:27,681
WELCOME

59
00:02:27,748 --> 00:02:29,650
-Thank you, Kashama.
-Yes.

60
00:02:29,716 --> 00:02:33,053
You've all come
with your medals around your neck.

61
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,154
-They seem heavy.
-Yes.

62
00:02:34,221 --> 00:02:35,956
As you know,

63
00:02:36,023 --> 00:02:38,258
track-and-field is a difficult event.

64
00:02:38,325 --> 00:02:39,192
I got chills.

65
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:41,261
Mi-mi, I heard you returned yesterday.

66
00:02:41,328 --> 00:02:42,896
Yes, I arrived yesterday,

67
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:45,332
but you invited me here, so I ran over.

68
00:02:46,566 --> 00:02:49,202
-I can't believe this is happening.
-Is that so?

69
00:02:49,269 --> 00:02:53,273
I heard Biwesa Daniel
was also surprised to hear from us.

70
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:56,243
When I heard the news,
I thought, "Am I that famous?"

71
00:02:56,476 --> 00:02:57,744
SUDDENLY SELF-AWARE

72
00:02:57,811 --> 00:02:59,079
You are!

73
00:02:59,146 --> 00:03:00,180
You're so worthy.

74
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:03,450
So I came with gratitude
for the kind invitation.

75
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:05,385
"Biwesa Daniel Kashama."

76
00:03:05,452 --> 00:03:07,954
-It must mean something.
-The meaning.

77
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:09,689
My parents named me.

78
00:03:09,756 --> 00:03:13,427
"Biwesa" means someone
who does something amazing,

79
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:15,896
and "Kashama" means lion.

80
00:03:15,962 --> 00:03:17,597
That suits you.

81
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:20,133
-A lion who does amazing things.
-That's so cool.

82
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:23,370
-A lion who does amazing things.
-So cool.

83
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:25,138
So how can we address you?

84
00:03:25,205 --> 00:03:26,907
-Daniel or Niel.
-Niel.

85
00:03:26,973 --> 00:03:28,675
There are too many Daniels, so Niel it is.

86
00:03:28,742 --> 00:03:33,413
Niel's parents are originally from Congo,

87
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,782
but you've never been anywhere near it?

88
00:03:35,849 --> 00:03:36,950
-No.
-No?

89
00:03:37,017 --> 00:03:39,453
I was born in Ansan, Gyeonggi-do.

90
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:41,722
-You were born in Ansan.
-Suddenly relatable.

91
00:03:41,788 --> 00:03:44,124
-Born and raised in Ansan.
-Born and raised.

92
00:03:44,191 --> 00:03:46,326
WE CAN TELL BY YOUR CHOICE OF WORDS

93
00:03:46,393 --> 00:03:47,527
AMAZED

94
00:03:47,594 --> 00:03:48,495
He was born in Ansan,

95
00:03:48,562 --> 00:03:51,731
-and Mi-mi is a former Zainichi Korean.
-Yes.

96
00:03:51,798 --> 00:03:54,301
My father is ethnically Korean,

97
00:03:54,367 --> 00:03:57,838
-and my mother is Japanese.
-I see.

98
00:03:57,904 --> 00:04:02,576
So until I turned 20,
I held citizenship in both countries,

99
00:04:02,642 --> 00:04:04,611
but I chose to come to Korea.

100
00:04:05,412 --> 00:04:08,048
Let's dig deeper

101
00:04:08,115 --> 00:04:11,685
into the amazing stories of
these two athletes in the next episode.

102
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:14,020
An all-time legendary fencer,

103
00:04:14,087 --> 00:04:17,290
Oh Sang-uk, was chosen

104
00:04:17,357 --> 00:04:21,027
as the MVP Korean player
after the Asian Games.

105
00:04:21,394 --> 00:04:22,829
THE IRREFUTABLE MVP

106
00:04:23,196 --> 00:04:24,965
CONGRATULATIONS

107
00:04:25,031 --> 00:04:26,833
I heard the news yesterday.

108
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:28,735
-There was a cash prize.
-A cash prize?

109
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:29,903
-A cash prize?
-Cash?

110
00:04:29,970 --> 00:04:32,172
-I asked, "Cash prize? How much?"
-And the honor?

111
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:34,775
-I was suddenly all ears.
-You even get a cash prize?

112
00:04:34,841 --> 00:04:36,376
And so,

113
00:04:36,443 --> 00:04:38,512
the cash prize was 30 million won.

114
00:04:38,578 --> 00:04:39,880
-Thirty million?
-Wow, thirty million?

115
00:04:39,946 --> 00:04:41,581
Wait, after taxes?

116
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:43,283
-Taxes?
-Do they deduct for taxes?

117
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:45,252
-They have to, 3.8%.
-Did they deduct 3.8%?

118
00:04:45,318 --> 00:04:46,686
It's before tax.

119
00:04:46,753 --> 00:04:48,822
-So the tax gets deducted.
-I didn't know.

120
00:04:48,889 --> 00:04:51,057
So I was in great condition yesterday.

121
00:04:51,792 --> 00:04:54,895
RECOVERED FROM EXHAUSTION

122
00:04:54,961 --> 00:04:57,364
I heard you two were also Sang-uk's fans.

123
00:04:57,430 --> 00:05:00,934
I once saw him at the cafeteria
in the national training center,

124
00:05:01,001 --> 00:05:04,738
-and I felt like I was seeing a celebrity.
-He's a celebrity.

125
00:05:04,804 --> 00:05:07,174
-You'll get to eat with him today.
-Right.

126
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,976
-Yes, sitting across from him.
-That's right.

127
00:05:10,043 --> 00:05:11,778
What about you, Niel?

128
00:05:11,845 --> 00:05:14,915
When he stepped up
as the last fencer for the team event

129
00:05:14,981 --> 00:05:16,349
and turned the game around,

130
00:05:16,416 --> 00:05:19,252
I thought, "Isn't that just too cool?"

131
00:05:19,519 --> 00:05:21,288
YOU OWNED THE GAME, MAN…

132
00:05:21,354 --> 00:05:22,956
I fell for him then and there.

133
00:05:23,023 --> 00:05:24,291
He even makes men swoon.

134
00:05:24,357 --> 00:05:26,059
Yes, even the guys.

135
00:05:26,126 --> 00:05:28,695
The moment you won the gold medal

136
00:05:28,762 --> 00:05:30,263
was very dramatic.

137
00:05:30,330 --> 00:05:33,800
For the team event, the fencers take turns

138
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:37,571
and whichever team
gets to 45 points first wins.

139
00:05:37,637 --> 00:05:40,240
At the end, the score was 44 to 41,

140
00:05:40,307 --> 00:05:42,242
with Japan scoring 44, and us, 41.

141
00:05:42,309 --> 00:05:45,478
Then Sang-uk stepped up

142
00:05:45,545 --> 00:05:47,047
and won four points consecutively.

143
00:05:47,113 --> 00:05:48,882
SEIZED THE THRILLING VICTORY FROM BEHIND

144
00:05:48,949 --> 00:05:49,916
It was insane.

145
00:05:49,983 --> 00:05:54,221
Even scriptwriters couldn't
come up with something like this.

146
00:05:54,287 --> 00:05:57,023
-First of all, there was…
-Pressure?

147
00:05:57,090 --> 00:05:59,092
-…a lot of pressure.
-I see.

148
00:05:59,159 --> 00:06:01,328
The moment we got up to 43 points,

149
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:04,698
I began to think,
"I might be able to turn this around."

150
00:06:05,498 --> 00:06:07,467
During the match point,

151
00:06:07,534 --> 00:06:09,369
the referee suddenly stopped the match.

152
00:06:09,436 --> 00:06:13,974
It's the rule to check the video
when there's only one point left to go.

153
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:15,809
Both teams boasted about their victories.

154
00:06:15,876 --> 00:06:18,612
Was there a time when you feigned that
although you knew you lost?

155
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:21,581
-That's…
-I mean, to show your response.

156
00:06:21,648 --> 00:06:23,550
-The spirit.
-Yes, the spirit is a part of it.

157
00:06:23,617 --> 00:06:26,820
It happens in soccer too,
telling the referee that you didn't do it.

158
00:06:26,886 --> 00:06:29,489
Everyone does that at 44 to 44.

159
00:06:29,556 --> 00:06:31,524
-Everyone?
-Even if they knew they had lost.

160
00:06:31,591 --> 00:06:35,462
So if you have to fake such actions,
is that also a part

161
00:06:35,528 --> 00:06:36,429
of the training?

162
00:06:37,130 --> 00:06:39,432
-We don't get trained to do that.
-I had been curious.

163
00:06:39,499 --> 00:06:41,968
There must be a better way
of making your case.

164
00:06:42,035 --> 00:06:44,971
I was curious too. I wanted to know.

165
00:06:45,038 --> 00:06:48,141
-There are things you pick up.
-You pick things up?

166
00:06:48,208 --> 00:06:49,409
Like, "That's what I should do"?

167
00:06:49,476 --> 00:06:51,144
-They're better than soccer players.
-How so?

168
00:06:51,211 --> 00:06:52,879
The ball obviously hit my foot last,

169
00:06:52,946 --> 00:06:55,582
but I get furious at the referee.

170
00:06:55,649 --> 00:06:56,483
"Why?"

171
00:06:56,549 --> 00:06:59,619
-"Why?"
-But it was so evident.

172
00:06:59,686 --> 00:07:00,587
"Why?"

173
00:07:01,955 --> 00:07:05,392
Which gold medal do you like better?
The individual event or the team event?

174
00:07:05,458 --> 00:07:08,528
It's different with each match,
but this time, definitely the team event.

175
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:10,163
It was so dramatic.

176
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:12,832
I was the only one
who had played in the Asian Games,

177
00:07:12,899 --> 00:07:14,367
so the team was very nervous.

178
00:07:14,434 --> 00:07:15,735
Although I was also…

179
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:18,338
How can I put this? Intimidated?
I was a bit intimidated,

180
00:07:18,405 --> 00:07:21,174
but I pretended like I wasn't,
that I didn't feel any pressure.

181
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:24,177
I had to be the one
to tell them things like, "It's okay."

182
00:07:24,244 --> 00:07:27,113
"I'll make it happen.
Don't worry about it."

183
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:29,082
I mean, if you were that relaxed…

184
00:07:29,149 --> 00:07:31,217
On the inside, I thought,

185
00:07:31,284 --> 00:07:33,520
-"If we don't win, that's that."
-"That's that"?

186
00:07:34,587 --> 00:07:35,956
-You can't help it.
-Yes, that's true.

187
00:07:36,022 --> 00:07:39,993
-But you didn't say that to your team.
-I didn't share that part.

188
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:40,994
-To yourself.
-All alone.

189
00:07:41,061 --> 00:07:43,296
-Never.
-He's not like me. I said it.

190
00:07:43,363 --> 00:07:44,831
I said, "I'll make it happen."

191
00:07:44,898 --> 00:07:46,366
"But if it doesn't, that's that."

192
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:49,269
-I said that.
-"Don't blame me."

193
00:07:49,336 --> 00:07:52,172
I said, "I'll do my best,
but if you blame me later, you're done."

194
00:07:52,238 --> 00:07:54,040
But Sang-uk's different.

195
00:07:54,107 --> 00:07:57,510
For the individual event,
he was up against a Chinese fencer.

196
00:07:57,577 --> 00:07:59,479
At the start of the bout,

197
00:07:59,546 --> 00:08:01,381
I saw you bumping into the Chinese fencer.

198
00:08:01,448 --> 00:08:03,850
But I didn't want to say it hurt.

199
00:08:03,917 --> 00:08:05,919
I had lost once before because of a pain,

200
00:08:05,986 --> 00:08:09,189
but in the end, I began to think
it was just an excuse.

201
00:08:09,756 --> 00:08:13,460
-So I stomped my foot on the floor…
-To put it back into place?

202
00:08:13,526 --> 00:08:15,996
Yes, to put it back into place.
I kept stomping.

203
00:08:16,062 --> 00:08:19,366
And thankfully,
I felt fine after a night's sleep.

204
00:08:19,933 --> 00:08:21,134
I see.

205
00:08:21,201 --> 00:08:22,936
He's super tough.

206
00:08:23,003 --> 00:08:26,673
Sang-uk started fencing
in his first year of middle school.

207
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:30,477
<i>So he's been fencing for 18 years now.</i>

208
00:08:30,543 --> 00:08:32,212
Is there a habit you have

209
00:08:32,278 --> 00:08:35,215
or something that you do
because you've fenced for so long?

210
00:08:35,281 --> 00:08:37,584
-When I catch the elevator…
-Right.

211
00:08:37,650 --> 00:08:40,220
-Or when I suddenly…
-Step and push.

212
00:08:40,286 --> 00:08:41,888
…have to pick up something.

213
00:08:41,955 --> 00:08:43,823
Yes, I do that.

214
00:08:43,890 --> 00:08:45,658
When the elevator doors
are about to close,

215
00:08:45,725 --> 00:08:49,062
-if I took steps with my right foot,
-"Press the button!"

216
00:08:49,129 --> 00:08:50,463
-…could I get in?
-You missed it.

217
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:51,531
I did? I missed it?

218
00:08:51,865 --> 00:08:54,567
TAKE THE NEXT ONE…

219
00:08:54,634 --> 00:08:56,302
When I have an umbrella in my hand,

220
00:08:56,369 --> 00:08:59,639
-I keep shaking it like this.
-Right. You keep shaking it.

221
00:08:59,706 --> 00:09:01,708
HABITUALLY SHAKING THE SABER

222
00:09:01,775 --> 00:09:03,743
Do you use long umbrellas?

223
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:05,979
Or do you use the foldable ones?

224
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:08,181
Foldable ones don't have a good grip.

225
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:10,250
OBVIOUSLY PREFERS LONG UMBRELLAS

226
00:09:10,316 --> 00:09:11,684
You probably eat differently too.

227
00:09:13,453 --> 00:09:14,554
SEE THAT?

228
00:09:14,621 --> 00:09:15,889
When there's one piece left.

229
00:09:15,955 --> 00:09:17,157
He'll need a fork.

230
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:18,291
SNEAKY

231
00:09:18,691 --> 00:09:20,160
-For someone else's food too.
-Or with chopsticks.

232
00:09:20,226 --> 00:09:22,729
-You're right-handed, right?
-Yes.

233
00:09:22,796 --> 00:09:24,931
Then your body must be unbalanced.

234
00:09:24,998 --> 00:09:26,833
The thicknesses are completely different.

235
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:28,535
-They are?
-Yes.

236
00:09:28,601 --> 00:09:30,603
-You can see that…
-Can we see?

237
00:09:30,670 --> 00:09:31,704
So that happens?

238
00:09:33,073 --> 00:09:35,408
They are. They're quite different.

239
00:09:35,475 --> 00:09:37,944
-Look at the size difference.
-They're different.

240
00:09:38,011 --> 00:09:39,479
He mostly uses the right side.

241
00:09:40,213 --> 00:09:41,815
MINE ARE BOTH THICK

242
00:09:41,881 --> 00:09:44,484
-I guess my right shoulder droops lower.
-Because he uses his hand.

243
00:09:44,551 --> 00:09:47,020
-Yes, it's drooping.
-So when I take ID pictures,

244
00:09:47,087 --> 00:09:48,922
-I get, "Lift your right shoulder."
-"Lift your shoulder."

245
00:09:48,988 --> 00:09:50,824
But it feels like I'm doing this.

246
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:53,126
-But that's the right posture.
-Apparently so.

247
00:09:54,127 --> 00:09:55,128
I guess so.

248
00:09:55,195 --> 00:09:56,963
-Wait, breaking news.
-What is it?

249
00:09:57,030 --> 00:09:58,898
There's a chef here

250
00:09:58,965 --> 00:10:01,901
who has
a very special connection with Sang-uk.

251
00:10:01,968 --> 00:10:03,670
-One of our chefs?
-Yes.

252
00:10:03,736 --> 00:10:05,004
They're family.

253
00:10:05,071 --> 00:10:08,141
-Family?
-Who's Sang-uk's family?

254
00:10:08,208 --> 00:10:11,077
-It's Chef Sung-jun.
-What?

255
00:10:11,144 --> 00:10:12,245
-Chef Sung-jun?
-Family?

256
00:10:12,312 --> 00:10:13,847
-What?
-They're family?

257
00:10:13,913 --> 00:10:15,682
-Really?
-What? They're so different.

258
00:10:15,748 --> 00:10:17,917
-We seem similar.
-I found out a week ago.

259
00:10:17,984 --> 00:10:18,852
You did?

260
00:10:18,918 --> 00:10:23,022
He recently came to do an event
at Jincheon National Training Center.

261
00:10:23,089 --> 00:10:24,824
And he told one of my teammates

262
00:10:24,891 --> 00:10:28,027
that I was his relative.

263
00:10:28,094 --> 00:10:29,095
-Really?
-So…

264
00:10:29,162 --> 00:10:31,898
-I thought, "But he's a Kwon."
-He's a Kwon.

265
00:10:31,965 --> 00:10:34,000
I asked my father and found out

266
00:10:34,067 --> 00:10:36,002
he was my paternal grandfather's…

267
00:10:36,069 --> 00:10:37,804
-Grandfather?
-…younger sibling's…

268
00:10:37,871 --> 00:10:39,205
It's dragging on.

269
00:10:39,272 --> 00:10:41,875
-…son's wife's…
-An uncle?

270
00:10:41,941 --> 00:10:44,777
-…older sister's son.
-It's going far.

271
00:10:44,844 --> 00:10:46,513
You two are strangers.

272
00:10:46,579 --> 00:10:48,248
-No.
-You're pretty much strangers.

273
00:10:48,314 --> 00:10:50,250
-Easily put…
-Yes?

274
00:10:50,316 --> 00:10:55,388
<i>…he is my aunt's husband's cousin's</i>

275
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,357
<i>son.</i>

276
00:10:57,423 --> 00:10:59,959
-Your uncle's…
-We're eighth-degree relatives.

277
00:11:00,026 --> 00:11:03,363
-Like a distant relative.
-We're very distant.

278
00:11:03,429 --> 00:11:05,732
-They're strangers.
-I was surprised just now.

279
00:11:05,798 --> 00:11:08,334
I had known he was my relative
since five years ago.

280
00:11:08,401 --> 00:11:10,170
-Really?
-Yes.

281
00:11:10,236 --> 00:11:13,506
But you said you heard it last week,
so that was kind of disappointing.

282
00:11:13,573 --> 00:11:17,410
If I hadn't visited the show,
I probably would've never known.

283
00:11:17,844 --> 00:11:19,045
DANG IT…

284
00:11:19,112 --> 00:11:22,248
-My father heard I'd be on the show, so…
-That's so disappointing.

285
00:11:22,315 --> 00:11:23,816
-Yes?
-You two have different reaches.

286
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:26,185
He thought I should know

287
00:11:26,252 --> 00:11:29,656
and told me that whole thing
about his father's sibling's son.

288
00:11:29,722 --> 00:11:32,692
I finally realized
why he said we were related back then.

289
00:11:32,759 --> 00:11:34,527
When we heard about this relationship,

290
00:11:34,594 --> 00:11:37,397
it seemed too complicated,
so we asked an AI chatbot.

291
00:11:37,997 --> 00:11:40,366
-Why would you ask that?
-"Chatbot, what do you think

292
00:11:40,433 --> 00:11:42,435
about Sung-jun
and Sang-uk's relationship?"

293
00:11:42,502 --> 00:11:44,971
-And it gave an answer.
-What did it say?

294
00:11:45,038 --> 00:11:46,673
"They're practically strangers."

295
00:11:48,808 --> 00:11:49,943
Machines don't know any better.

296
00:11:51,744 --> 00:11:53,413
What does it know about the family bond?

297
00:11:53,479 --> 00:11:56,115
It said, "They're practically strangers."

298
00:11:56,182 --> 00:11:57,550
-Machines don't know better.
-Right.

299
00:11:57,617 --> 00:11:59,552
What's your age difference?

300
00:11:59,619 --> 00:12:00,887
We're a year apart.

301
00:12:00,954 --> 00:12:03,756
-I was born in 1995.
-And I, 1996.

302
00:12:04,824 --> 00:12:07,160
-I'm a year older.
-Wait, why is that?

303
00:12:07,227 --> 00:12:08,895
The side that's more famous

304
00:12:08,962 --> 00:12:11,130
-don't know the other's age.
-They don't know.

305
00:12:11,598 --> 00:12:13,533
THE LESS FAMOUS SUNG-JUN KNOWS
THE EXACT YEAR

306
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:14,467
-I know exactly.
-Yes.

307
00:12:14,534 --> 00:12:15,902
-The age gap.
-Yes, of course.

308
00:12:15,969 --> 00:12:19,272
Sang-uk, can you
call him <i>hyeong</i> just once?

309
00:12:19,339 --> 00:12:21,441
So… Sung-jun <i>hyeong.</i>

310
00:12:21,507 --> 00:12:22,575
THE HAPPY DISTANT RELATIVE

311
00:12:22,909 --> 00:12:25,645
-It's nice to meet you, Sung-jun.
-"It's nice to meet you."

312
00:12:25,712 --> 00:12:28,514
-Commend him for the Asian Games.
-I enjoyed the game.

313
00:12:29,916 --> 00:12:31,584
-You've done well.
-"You've done well"?

314
00:12:31,784 --> 00:12:33,386
THE OLDER RELATIVE WITH ZERO PRESENCE

315
00:12:33,453 --> 00:12:34,687
He might have the upper hand

316
00:12:34,754 --> 00:12:36,089
-in the family tree.
-Is your aunt

317
00:12:36,656 --> 00:12:38,091
older than your mom?

318
00:12:38,157 --> 00:12:39,125
Yes, he's my first uncle.

319
00:12:39,192 --> 00:12:41,194
-Then he's <i>hyeong.</i>
-Why?

320
00:12:41,261 --> 00:12:45,131
That's what happens in the family tree.
He's your first aunt's relative.

321
00:12:45,198 --> 00:12:47,267
-Call him <i>hyeong</i>.
-He's higher in the hierarchy.

322
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:49,702
-Call him <i>hyeong</i> instead.
-He's higher in the family.

323
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:52,338
I enjoyed the games, Sang-uk <i>hyeong.</i>

324
00:12:52,405 --> 00:12:54,641
-What do I say? Wait.
-Tell him something.

325
00:12:54,707 --> 00:12:57,110
You have to stop doing this.

326
00:12:59,412 --> 00:13:01,180
STABBING HIS HEART WITH A SABER

327
00:13:01,247 --> 00:13:03,016
That had to hurt.

328
00:13:03,082 --> 00:13:04,684
-That must hurt.
-His family had to tell him.

329
00:13:05,118 --> 00:13:06,185
HIS FAMILY POINTS IT OUT

330
00:13:06,252 --> 00:13:07,987
People in that family all do this.

331
00:13:08,054 --> 00:13:10,957
-They both have to shake something.
-They have a grip on something.

332
00:13:11,024 --> 00:13:14,193
Anyway, Chef Sung-jun should be
the best in Korea for finding things

333
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:16,162
to get him involved with the guest.

334
00:13:16,229 --> 00:13:19,966
There's another chef
who's good at getting himself involved.

335
00:13:20,033 --> 00:13:22,702
He claims he has
a lot in common with Sang-uk.

336
00:13:22,769 --> 00:13:23,803
-Who?
-Chef Hyun-suk.

337
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:24,904
-What?
-Why?

338
00:13:24,971 --> 00:13:26,939
How are you and him similar?

339
00:13:27,006 --> 00:13:29,275
This is an extraordinary case,

340
00:13:29,342 --> 00:13:31,310
but there's something called the 190 Club.

341
00:13:31,377 --> 00:13:32,879
-It's hard to get into.
-The 190 Club.

342
00:13:32,945 --> 00:13:33,980
What is that?

343
00:13:34,047 --> 00:13:38,785
It's a place for people
who are over 190 cm tall.

344
00:13:38,851 --> 00:13:42,789
-I think it's just us two on this set.
-You're right.

345
00:13:42,855 --> 00:13:44,290
How tall are you?

346
00:13:44,357 --> 00:13:45,625
I'm 192 cm.

347
00:13:45,692 --> 00:13:46,959
-He's huge.
-And you?

348
00:13:47,026 --> 00:13:48,861
I'm 190.8 cm.

349
00:13:49,395 --> 00:13:51,764
-You've probably shrunk with age.
-You're barely 190 cm.

350
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:54,000
-You've probably shrunk.
-Yes.

351
00:13:54,067 --> 00:13:56,269
Oh, there is a similarity.

352
00:13:56,335 --> 00:13:57,303
What is it?

353
00:13:57,370 --> 00:14:01,340
Just as Chef Hyun-suk
is the wedding singer among chefs,

354
00:14:01,407 --> 00:14:04,510
-Sang-uk is the singer among fencers.
-Really?

355
00:14:04,577 --> 00:14:05,545
-What?
-Really?

356
00:14:05,611 --> 00:14:06,846
HE CAN SING TOO?

357
00:14:06,913 --> 00:14:08,281
-Right?
-He's too perfect.

358
00:14:08,348 --> 00:14:11,551
How did you become
a wedding singer for fencers?

359
00:14:11,617 --> 00:14:13,953
I'm not that good of a singer.

360
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:16,122
I'm not great,

361
00:14:16,189 --> 00:14:18,891
-but the other three were horrible.
-At singing?

362
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:22,128
So when people wanted a singer,
they only had me to ask.

363
00:14:22,195 --> 00:14:23,930
I ended up doing it,

364
00:14:23,996 --> 00:14:25,698
and people who came as a guest

365
00:14:25,765 --> 00:14:28,334
all began to ask me to sing,

366
00:14:28,401 --> 00:14:30,937
so I ended up continuing to do it.

367
00:14:31,003 --> 00:14:33,139
The way he says it is exactly the same.

368
00:14:33,206 --> 00:14:35,708
They're both like,
"I don't really want to do it,

369
00:14:35,775 --> 00:14:38,111
-but people keep asking me."
-Yes.

370
00:14:38,177 --> 00:14:40,613
I have another wedding gig this month.

371
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,315
BUT YOU'RE A CHEF

372
00:14:42,382 --> 00:14:44,183
It was a request.

373
00:14:44,650 --> 00:14:47,320
THE SONG WE'VE HEARD ONE TOO MANY TIMES
SINCE EUN-YEONG'S WEDDING

374
00:14:47,387 --> 00:14:49,589
-He doesn't even pick up new songs.
-No.

375
00:14:49,655 --> 00:14:52,024
Who cares? The guests are different.

376
00:14:52,925 --> 00:14:54,827
He's right. He's right about that.

377
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:57,697
-Yes, it's for different guests.
-Come to think of it,

378
00:14:57,764 --> 00:15:01,067
I always thought
I had to sing a different song.

379
00:15:01,134 --> 00:15:02,201
-No.
-But it's true.

380
00:15:02,268 --> 00:15:03,669
-The guests are different.
-They are.

381
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:05,838
FROM NOW ON, HE'LL SING ONE SONG

382
00:15:05,905 --> 00:15:08,674
A great fencer and a wedding singer,

383
00:15:08,741 --> 00:15:10,877
this talented athlete, Oh Sang-uk,

384
00:15:10,943 --> 00:15:12,879
is here to share his gold medal…

385
00:15:13,846 --> 00:15:17,183
…is here to share his fridge.

386
00:15:17,250 --> 00:15:18,117
He's sharing his medal?

387
00:15:18,184 --> 00:15:19,685
-He can't do that.
-Can we share that?

388
00:15:19,952 --> 00:15:21,621
RIPPING EACH OTHER APART AMICABLY

389
00:15:21,687 --> 00:15:23,623
-Let's just share his fridge.
-Okay.

390
00:15:23,689 --> 00:15:27,693
The fridges we brought
for the Asian Games Special are different.

391
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:31,030
The athletes mostly stay
in the training center,

392
00:15:31,097 --> 00:15:35,468
and they were mostly in Japan
during the entirety of the Asian Games,

393
00:15:35,535 --> 00:15:39,705
so we filled them up with ingredients

394
00:15:39,772 --> 00:15:43,075
that they've mentioned as their favorites.

395
00:15:43,142 --> 00:15:45,611
They must be exhausted physically,

396
00:15:45,678 --> 00:15:47,513
so they need to recharge.

397
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:49,982
We should let them eat what they want.

398
00:15:50,049 --> 00:15:52,485
-We present Sang-uk's fridge!
-Fridge!

399
00:15:54,187 --> 00:15:55,354
FULL OF HIS FAVORITE INGREDIENTS

400
00:15:55,421 --> 00:15:58,825
We bought his favorite ingredients,

401
00:15:58,891 --> 00:16:00,626
so there's nothing in the freezer.

402
00:16:00,693 --> 00:16:02,061
It's whatever he likes.

403
00:16:02,128 --> 00:16:03,729
-He should eat it fresh.
-That's right.

404
00:16:03,796 --> 00:16:05,598
-We didn't need to freeze anything.
-No.

405
00:16:05,665 --> 00:16:07,600
First of all, the top shelf.

406
00:16:07,667 --> 00:16:08,968
We see some <i>jeotgal.</i>

407
00:16:09,035 --> 00:16:12,638
These are made of squid,
cutlassfish, and octopus.

408
00:16:12,705 --> 00:16:13,873
Do you like <i>jeotgal?</i>

409
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:16,776
Yes, so I always pack some
when I compete abroad.

410
00:16:16,843 --> 00:16:18,544
A MUST-PACK FOR HIS TRIPS

411
00:16:18,611 --> 00:16:21,180
My favorite is
cutlassfish innards <i>jeotgal.</i>

412
00:16:22,181 --> 00:16:23,015
It's so good.

413
00:16:23,082 --> 00:16:24,650
I eat it with rice,

414
00:16:24,717 --> 00:16:27,320
-and sometimes, with pork belly.
-Yes, they go well together.

415
00:16:27,386 --> 00:16:29,222
This is either a hit or a miss for people.

416
00:16:29,288 --> 00:16:31,591
It's the same with athletes,

417
00:16:31,657 --> 00:16:35,928
so I also bring <i>jeotgal</i> made with squid
or octopus, which everyone loves.

418
00:16:35,995 --> 00:16:38,431
-So you can share it with them.
-Yes.

419
00:16:38,498 --> 00:16:40,166
Next, kimchi.

420
00:16:40,233 --> 00:16:43,035
White kimchi, cabbage kimchi,
and fresh cabbage kimchi.

421
00:16:43,102 --> 00:16:44,470
I always need kimchi,

422
00:16:44,537 --> 00:16:48,407
and the kind depends
on what kind of food I'm eating.

423
00:16:48,474 --> 00:16:50,676
But my favorite is fresh kimchi.

424
00:16:50,743 --> 00:16:52,178
Fresh kimchi.

425
00:16:52,245 --> 00:16:54,447
We received a tip just now.

426
00:16:54,513 --> 00:16:57,517
The ingredient that helped
Sang-uk grow to his height

427
00:16:57,583 --> 00:16:58,618
was kimchi?

428
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:00,086
-What?
-That's right.

429
00:17:00,152 --> 00:17:02,989
Most people bring up milk or anchovies.

430
00:17:03,055 --> 00:17:06,893
I grew up with my grandmother,
and all I had was kimchi

431
00:17:06,959 --> 00:17:09,228
even when she made lots of food.

432
00:17:09,295 --> 00:17:10,997
ONLY KIMCHI

433
00:17:11,063 --> 00:17:12,398
Once, she gave me a whole head of kimchi,

434
00:17:12,465 --> 00:17:14,934
but I finished everything,
so she had to bring out more.

435
00:17:15,001 --> 00:17:16,402
-That's a lot.
-I loved it that much.

436
00:17:16,469 --> 00:17:19,372
-She must be a good cook.
-Her kimchi must've been great.

437
00:17:19,438 --> 00:17:20,273
It was.

438
00:17:20,339 --> 00:17:21,874
Another thing I remember

439
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:23,943
-is mushrooms.
-Mushrooms?

440
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:26,379
King oyster mushrooms
and oyster mushrooms.

441
00:17:26,445 --> 00:17:28,781
Well, we call it <i>mak-jjigae.</i>

442
00:17:28,848 --> 00:17:29,949
<i>Mak-jjigae</i>?

443
00:17:30,016 --> 00:17:33,553
When we gather at my grandmother's place,
eat barbecue, and have a drink,

444
00:17:33,619 --> 00:17:36,455
we'd all wake up
with a need for a hangover cure.

445
00:17:36,522 --> 00:17:39,191
So she made this <i>jjigae</i>
by throwing everything in,

446
00:17:39,258 --> 00:17:40,660
and she added lots of mushrooms.

447
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:43,863
-Like gochujang <i>jjigae?</i>
-Something like that.

448
00:17:43,930 --> 00:17:46,933
-With everything.
-Yes, it had lots of mushrooms in it.

449
00:17:46,999 --> 00:17:50,403
And that's one of the dishes
I remember the most.

450
00:17:50,469 --> 00:17:53,606
-You must've liked that.
-Grandmother's mushroom <i>jjigae.</i>

451
00:17:53,673 --> 00:17:55,575
That's something only Grandma can make.

452
00:17:55,641 --> 00:17:57,043
You're right.

453
00:17:57,109 --> 00:17:59,946
Do you also take kimchi
when you go abroad?

454
00:18:00,012 --> 00:18:01,881
I need to pack a huge batch.

455
00:18:01,948 --> 00:18:03,816
-He must like it a lot.
-That's all I need

456
00:18:03,883 --> 00:18:06,852
to eat three bowls of rice.

457
00:18:06,919 --> 00:18:08,587
You sound like a big eater.

458
00:18:08,654 --> 00:18:11,791
When I first became a national athlete,
I always calculated

459
00:18:11,857 --> 00:18:15,528
five bowls of instant rice per meal.

460
00:18:15,595 --> 00:18:17,096
SURPRISED

461
00:18:17,163 --> 00:18:19,198
-That's a kilogram.
-Yes, per meal.

462
00:18:19,265 --> 00:18:20,533
When I get really hungry,

463
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,735
-I can eat seven bowls of those.
-Seven?

464
00:18:22,802 --> 00:18:26,372
And when I'd smell someone
making <i>ramyeon</i> next to me,

465
00:18:26,439 --> 00:18:30,576
I'd go and make
two bags for myself after eating rice.

466
00:18:30,643 --> 00:18:34,113
One of the reasons
why he became a big eater

467
00:18:34,180 --> 00:18:36,816
is because he has lots of brothers.

468
00:18:36,882 --> 00:18:38,050
How many brothers do you have?

469
00:18:38,117 --> 00:18:40,553
-There are three of us.
-Three. Which one are you?

470
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:43,189
-I'm the middle child.
-The middle kid.

471
00:18:43,255 --> 00:18:44,724
Was there competition?

472
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:46,459
All three of us eat well.

473
00:18:46,525 --> 00:18:49,629
-All three of them.
-We all like <i>bibim-ramyeon,</i>

474
00:18:49,695 --> 00:18:51,397
and we cook at least 20 bags.

475
00:18:51,464 --> 00:18:52,798
20 BAGS FOR THREE PEOPLE

476
00:18:53,032 --> 00:18:55,001
20 BAGS?

477
00:18:55,067 --> 00:18:57,670
-The three of you?
-Yes, and we compete to eat more…

478
00:18:57,737 --> 00:18:59,005
-Right.
-…until the end.

479
00:18:59,071 --> 00:19:01,607
You had to have
a swift hand since childhood.

480
00:19:01,674 --> 00:19:02,875
-Yes.
-That's true.

481
00:19:02,942 --> 00:19:04,777
-He was built for fencing.
-He had to eat quickly.

482
00:19:04,844 --> 00:19:06,445
-Very quickly.
-Are your brothers tall too?

483
00:19:06,512 --> 00:19:07,480
We're all tall.

484
00:19:07,546 --> 00:19:11,150
-They all ate well.
-My older brother is 186 cm, maybe 187.

485
00:19:11,217 --> 00:19:13,953
-And so is my younger brother.
-They're tall.

486
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:14,987
Their DNA is crazy.

487
00:19:15,054 --> 00:19:16,689
I'm jealous.

488
00:19:16,756 --> 00:19:19,258
When did you grow to be that tall?

489
00:19:19,325 --> 00:19:23,529
I was 160 cm tall
when I got into middle school.

490
00:19:23,596 --> 00:19:25,965
-You were 160 cm then.
-In middle school?

491
00:19:26,032 --> 00:19:27,466
And when I graduated, I was this tall.

492
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:29,268
Thirty centimeters?

493
00:19:29,335 --> 00:19:32,571
-I got up to 191, 192 cm.
-You grew 30 cm?

494
00:19:32,638 --> 00:19:34,507
-I was the same.
-Do we need to eat more kimchi?

495
00:19:34,573 --> 00:19:36,709
-You were like that too?
-I was small…

496
00:19:36,776 --> 00:19:39,245
-…before starting middle school.
-What was that?

497
00:19:39,311 --> 00:19:41,914
-Why did you laugh?
-But I grew ten cm per year.

498
00:19:41,981 --> 00:19:44,116
-Did you eat lots of kimchi too?
-He's just adding.

499
00:19:44,950 --> 00:19:46,619
All we had was kimchi in those days.

500
00:19:46,686 --> 00:19:49,021
So it is kimchi.

501
00:19:49,088 --> 00:19:51,157
He only had kimchi back then.

502
00:19:51,223 --> 00:19:53,459
-Just corn porridge and kimchi.
-Oh, no.

503
00:19:53,526 --> 00:19:55,528
Dear children,

504
00:19:55,594 --> 00:19:58,330
-eat lots of kimchi to grow taller.
-You must eat kimchi.

505
00:19:58,397 --> 00:19:59,899
Next, seafood.

506
00:19:59,965 --> 00:20:02,068
Shrimp, blue crabs,

507
00:20:02,134 --> 00:20:04,703
-cutlassfish, and gizzard shad.
-Gizzard shad.

508
00:20:04,770 --> 00:20:06,872
-You must like seafood.
-First of all,

509
00:20:06,939 --> 00:20:09,975
I prefer seafood over meat.

510
00:20:10,042 --> 00:20:11,410
-Over meat.
-Yes.

511
00:20:11,477 --> 00:20:13,646
Real foodies seem to love seafood more.

512
00:20:13,712 --> 00:20:16,048
My favorite is in-season seafood.

513
00:20:16,115 --> 00:20:17,817
Whatever is in season.

514
00:20:17,883 --> 00:20:19,118
-It's fall now.
-Shrimp and gizzard shad.

515
00:20:19,185 --> 00:20:21,487
It's fall now, so there are fall seafoods.

516
00:20:21,554 --> 00:20:24,890
And in winter,
I have to go and get oysters.

517
00:20:24,957 --> 00:20:27,159
In April, I go to Dangjin

518
00:20:27,226 --> 00:20:30,196
to eat some icefish.

519
00:20:30,262 --> 00:20:32,431
-He sounds like a gourmet.
-You love in-season seafood.

520
00:20:32,498 --> 00:20:35,267
So what's good right now?

521
00:20:35,334 --> 00:20:37,770
-Right now? Gizzard shad.
-Gizzard shad.

522
00:20:37,837 --> 00:20:39,338
-Gizzard shad?
-Gizzard shad.

523
00:20:39,405 --> 00:20:41,140
-Do you eat the bones too?
-Yes.

524
00:20:41,207 --> 00:20:43,175
You do eat really well.

525
00:20:43,242 --> 00:20:44,610
-Yes.
-Do you like mussels?

526
00:20:44,677 --> 00:20:47,847
I like all shellfish,

527
00:20:47,913 --> 00:20:49,582
but I don't prefer those

528
00:20:49,648 --> 00:20:52,251
that only give a tiny amount of meat
like Manila clams.

529
00:20:52,318 --> 00:20:54,920
-Not the ones with tiny meat.
-Like blood clams.

530
00:20:54,987 --> 00:20:56,655
I don't get enough food

531
00:20:56,722 --> 00:20:59,225
compared to the amount of work I put in.

532
00:20:59,492 --> 00:21:01,293
THE EFFICIENT BIG EATER

533
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:02,595
And there's no bite to it.

534
00:21:02,661 --> 00:21:04,330
It needs to be as big as a mussel.

535
00:21:04,396 --> 00:21:06,332
-That's right.
-I see.

536
00:21:06,398 --> 00:21:08,167
-A review by a big eater.
-Big eater indeed.

537
00:21:08,234 --> 00:21:10,202
-He has some meat.
-He does have some meat.

538
00:21:10,269 --> 00:21:13,005
Beef tenderloin and pork belly.

539
00:21:13,072 --> 00:21:16,509
Do you like your pork belly
with a bigger fat ratio?

540
00:21:16,575 --> 00:21:18,344
-Yes, I like pork belly…
-It's a lot.

541
00:21:18,410 --> 00:21:22,681
-with about a 4.5 to 5.5 ratio of fat.
-About 4.5 of fat.

542
00:21:22,748 --> 00:21:24,550
There still has to be a bit more meat.

543
00:21:24,617 --> 00:21:28,087
-So 5.5 of red meat.
-Yes, I like it like that.

544
00:21:28,154 --> 00:21:29,555
-He needs some fat.
-That's a good cut.

545
00:21:29,622 --> 00:21:32,158
Why don't you go for pork neck instead?

546
00:21:32,224 --> 00:21:34,260
Pork neck pieces are tiny,

547
00:21:34,326 --> 00:21:36,796
-and they get even smaller when grilled.
-Yes.

548
00:21:36,862 --> 00:21:39,698
So compared to the work I put in,

549
00:21:39,765 --> 00:21:41,534
-Not much?
-…there's not much to eat.

550
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:45,171
-How about pork belly slices?
-Pork belly slices?

551
00:21:45,237 --> 00:21:48,207
-I pour everything and grill it like this.
-And then you eat it.

552
00:21:48,274 --> 00:21:51,243
-I eat it like this. Yes.
-Four to five pieces at once.

553
00:21:51,310 --> 00:21:53,212
Next, the crisper drawer.

554
00:21:53,279 --> 00:21:55,381
Fruit. Do you enjoy fruit?

555
00:21:55,447 --> 00:21:59,218
I'm not particular
about fruit or vegetables.

556
00:21:59,285 --> 00:22:01,387
I see a lot in here. Chives, eggplants,

557
00:22:01,453 --> 00:22:03,189
perilla leaves, crown daisy,

558
00:22:03,255 --> 00:22:04,590
-young radish.
-He likes everything.

559
00:22:04,657 --> 00:22:05,724
What's your favorite?

560
00:22:05,791 --> 00:22:08,160
Do you see asparagus there?

561
00:22:08,227 --> 00:22:10,729
-Yes, asparagus. It's right here.
-That one.

562
00:22:10,796 --> 00:22:13,599
-I like the texture.
-The crunchy texture.

563
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:15,034
-I like to grill it.
-Lightly, right?

564
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:18,037
-These look like swords too.
-Right.

565
00:22:18,103 --> 00:22:20,773
-That's why he likes it.
-Everything leads to fencing.

566
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:23,375
EVERYTHING HE TOUCHES TURNS INTO FENCING

567
00:22:23,442 --> 00:22:25,211
-There's a grip.
-You can grab it.

568
00:22:25,277 --> 00:22:27,413
HAS A NICE GRIP

569
00:22:27,479 --> 00:22:28,514
Stab me.

570
00:22:28,581 --> 00:22:30,316
-Stabbed!
-Owie!

571
00:22:30,549 --> 00:22:31,917
PERFECT TEAMWORK

572
00:22:31,984 --> 00:22:33,352
He likes things similar to swords.

573
00:22:33,419 --> 00:22:35,120
If you want your child to be a fencer,

574
00:22:35,187 --> 00:22:36,755
-eat asparagus.
-Eat asparagus.

575
00:22:38,490 --> 00:22:39,692
Look at these.

576
00:22:39,758 --> 00:22:41,961
-Do you like beer?
-Yes.

577
00:22:42,027 --> 00:22:43,896
-I like to drink beer alone.
-Alone.

578
00:22:43,963 --> 00:22:46,165
-After a meet,
-So he likes beer?

579
00:22:46,232 --> 00:22:47,967
…I come home and have some beer

580
00:22:48,033 --> 00:22:50,002
with skewers.

581
00:22:50,069 --> 00:22:52,238
Of course, he's into skewers too.

582
00:22:52,304 --> 00:22:53,939
Yes, skewers.

583
00:22:54,006 --> 00:22:55,875
-Skewers.
-He likes things that are like swords.

584
00:22:55,941 --> 00:22:58,110
I don't really like deep-fried food.

585
00:22:58,177 --> 00:22:59,778
-…deep-fried food.
-Really?

586
00:22:59,845 --> 00:23:01,413
-No Chinese food.
-So skewers go well…

587
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:02,314
-…with beer, so…
-You're out.

588
00:23:02,381 --> 00:23:03,916
Do you enjoy Chinese food?

589
00:23:03,983 --> 00:23:05,784
I love Chinese food.

590
00:23:05,851 --> 00:23:07,419
But there are lots of deep-fried dishes.

591
00:23:07,486 --> 00:23:10,022
I rarely order sweet and sour pork.

592
00:23:11,056 --> 00:23:12,725
I usually eat <i>jjamppong,</i>

593
00:23:12,791 --> 00:23:14,860
and if I want more,
I just order <i>jjajangmyeon.</i>

594
00:23:14,927 --> 00:23:17,129
-I see.
-He'd rather eat more noodles.

595
00:23:17,196 --> 00:23:19,365
I love noodles. I really do.

596
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:21,166
-And look.
-He has <i>ramyeon.</i>

597
00:23:21,233 --> 00:23:22,268
<i>Ramyeon.</i>

598
00:23:22,334 --> 00:23:24,770
-I heard you eat it often.
-Yes.

599
00:23:24,837 --> 00:23:26,238
I can eat up to five bags at once.

600
00:23:27,373 --> 00:23:29,241
I love noodles so much.

601
00:23:29,308 --> 00:23:30,442
I love them.

602
00:23:30,509 --> 00:23:32,778
Do you have a requirement for noodles?

603
00:23:32,845 --> 00:23:35,748
-I think the thickness is important.
-The thickness.

604
00:23:35,814 --> 00:23:37,216
The noodle's thickness.

605
00:23:37,283 --> 00:23:40,319
I need the soup to seep into the noodles,
but be as thick as possible.

606
00:23:41,553 --> 00:23:42,588
That's hard.

607
00:23:42,655 --> 00:23:44,456
If anyone wants to make noodles,

608
00:23:44,523 --> 00:23:48,027
-make it thick.
-And make sure it soaks up enough sauce.

609
00:23:48,093 --> 00:23:51,063
That's all for Sang-uk's fridge.

610
00:23:54,266 --> 00:23:56,735
Before we share Sang-uk's orders,

611
00:23:56,802 --> 00:23:58,671
I have an announcement to make.

612
00:23:58,737 --> 00:24:02,508
Because we've specially invited
these national athletes,

613
00:24:02,574 --> 00:24:06,578
we decided to hold a special item round.

614
00:24:06,645 --> 00:24:08,948
<i>We let the chefs know</i>

615
00:24:09,014 --> 00:24:10,649
<i>that they could bring an item</i>…

616
00:24:10,716 --> 00:24:15,454
<i>they could use as a weapon.</i>

617
00:24:15,521 --> 00:24:16,889
STAY TUNED TO SEE THE ITEMS

618
00:24:16,956 --> 00:24:19,959
And you must use the item

619
00:24:20,025 --> 00:24:22,094
you have brought.

620
00:24:23,529 --> 00:24:25,864
-If you don't, there's a penalty.
-What if it isn't right for the order?

621
00:24:25,931 --> 00:24:27,066
Include it somehow.

622
00:24:27,132 --> 00:24:29,168
It already sounds fun.

623
00:24:29,234 --> 00:24:32,071
Will your items help you or mess you up?

624
00:24:32,137 --> 00:24:35,741
Let's find out Sang-uk's orders.

625
00:24:35,808 --> 00:24:36,675
Show us.

626
00:24:38,744 --> 00:24:41,313
Delicious Beer SaberNacks.

627
00:24:41,380 --> 00:24:43,148
SaberNacks.

628
00:24:43,215 --> 00:24:45,250
Are you talking about bar snacks?

629
00:24:45,317 --> 00:24:48,287
When people think
of something to eat with beer,

630
00:24:48,354 --> 00:24:50,689
they mostly land on something deep-fried.

631
00:24:50,756 --> 00:24:53,992
-But because I don't like it too much,
-You don't like it.

632
00:24:54,059 --> 00:24:57,396
…I wanted to know
if there's anything more delicious.

633
00:24:58,330 --> 00:24:59,431
All right.

634
00:24:59,498 --> 00:25:01,633
What's his second order?

635
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:03,869
-Seasonal SeaVengers.
-SeaVengers.

636
00:25:03,936 --> 00:25:07,439
-SeaVengers.
-SeaVengers, with sea for seafood.

637
00:25:07,506 --> 00:25:10,376
I love seasonal seafood so much

638
00:25:10,442 --> 00:25:13,112
that I travel to the local area
just to eat them.

639
00:25:13,178 --> 00:25:15,681
I'd be grateful if you made me

640
00:25:15,748 --> 00:25:19,418
a seasonal seafood dish.

641
00:25:19,485 --> 00:25:20,986
SeaVengers.

642
00:25:21,053 --> 00:25:24,857
We'll accept four contenders.

643
00:25:24,923 --> 00:25:26,692
Yes? The 190 Club?

644
00:25:26,759 --> 00:25:28,127
Yes, Club 190.

645
00:25:28,193 --> 00:25:29,261
-Club 190.
-Yes.

646
00:25:29,328 --> 00:25:31,397
As a fellow member,

647
00:25:31,463 --> 00:25:34,400
I'd like to make you a wonderful dish.

648
00:25:34,466 --> 00:25:35,300
Which one?

649
00:25:35,367 --> 00:25:36,435
SeaVengers.

650
00:25:36,502 --> 00:25:39,071
-The seafood dish.
-The seasonal seafood dish.

651
00:25:39,138 --> 00:25:41,974
We're in sync
as if we were made for each other.

652
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:43,809
Do you have a wedding song gig this month?

653
00:25:43,876 --> 00:25:44,910
This month?

654
00:25:44,977 --> 00:25:46,045
I do.

655
00:25:46,111 --> 00:25:47,546
-See?
-They both do.

656
00:25:47,613 --> 00:25:49,515
-They both do.
-They take reservations.

657
00:25:49,581 --> 00:25:51,417
-I have two weddings.
-I think…

658
00:25:51,483 --> 00:25:52,518
You have two?

659
00:25:52,584 --> 00:25:53,752
I sing two songs.

660
00:25:55,287 --> 00:25:57,723
You two might end up
doing the gig as a duo.

661
00:25:58,857 --> 00:26:01,727
-Form a duo.
-You two can go around together.

662
00:26:01,794 --> 00:26:04,363
-Sing together.
-The 190 Brothers. Not bad.

663
00:26:04,430 --> 00:26:05,597
Become a duo.

664
00:26:05,664 --> 00:26:07,966
-Who wants to join?
-Who's in?

665
00:26:08,033 --> 00:26:10,269
Jong-won raised his hand.

666
00:26:10,335 --> 00:26:12,004
I'll join SeaVengers too.

667
00:26:12,071 --> 00:26:14,773
-That'll be fun.
-Are you joining the 190 Club?

668
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:16,175
I'm not quite there,

669
00:26:16,241 --> 00:26:18,677
-but I am over 180 cm tall.
-He's over 180 cm.

670
00:26:18,944 --> 00:26:20,245
THE 100 (KG) CLUB

671
00:26:20,312 --> 00:26:22,548
-Are you in the 140 Club?
-"The 140 Club"?

672
00:26:22,614 --> 00:26:24,650
-It's the 150 Club.
-It's 150?

673
00:26:24,716 --> 00:26:25,951
Choi Hyun-suk versus Son Jong-won.

674
00:26:26,018 --> 00:26:28,187
-It's been a while.
-That's going to be fun.

675
00:26:28,253 --> 00:26:31,757
-It's the biggest match the show can…
-The biggest we can offer, right?

676
00:26:31,824 --> 00:26:33,158
-Yes.
-Excellent.

677
00:26:33,225 --> 00:26:36,228
Who wants to make a beer snack?
A great dish with beer.

678
00:26:36,295 --> 00:26:38,197
He just stabbed something!

679
00:26:38,263 --> 00:26:40,132
His relative is in.

680
00:26:40,199 --> 00:26:41,934
-And you should.
-That was fast.

681
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:42,835
It was fast.

682
00:26:42,901 --> 00:26:45,170
-He's my blood. I should cook.
-Your blood?

683
00:26:45,237 --> 00:26:47,372
Your blood?

684
00:26:47,439 --> 00:26:49,441
I heard the calling of my family.

685
00:26:49,508 --> 00:26:52,010
-Sang-uk's very distant relative.
-Yes.

686
00:26:52,077 --> 00:26:53,045
Do this again.

687
00:26:53,112 --> 00:26:55,214
-Why is your arm so short?
-It's long.

688
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:56,748
Good thing you didn't fence.

689
00:26:56,815 --> 00:26:58,317
Cut it out.

690
00:26:58,383 --> 00:27:00,853
You won't give him extra points
because he's a relative, right?

691
00:27:00,919 --> 00:27:02,020
-Not at all.
-No.

692
00:27:02,087 --> 00:27:04,456
-So please feel free to jump in.
-Of course not.

693
00:27:04,523 --> 00:27:06,925
I'll take on Sung-jun.

694
00:27:06,992 --> 00:27:08,494
-Hey.
-Beer snack.

695
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:10,996
They're even more distant than strangers.

696
00:27:11,063 --> 00:27:12,831
DISTANT

697
00:27:12,898 --> 00:27:15,767
I want to beat him today and win a star,

698
00:27:15,834 --> 00:27:17,202
so I'll go with that round.

699
00:27:17,269 --> 00:27:18,770
All right.

700
00:27:18,837 --> 00:27:22,908
Then let us begin the first round
between Chef Sung-jun and Eun-yeong!

701
00:27:23,509 --> 00:27:24,443
Saber.

702
00:27:25,777 --> 00:27:30,182
<i>The first round of cook-off</i>
<i>with Sang-uk's ingredients.</i>

703
00:27:30,249 --> 00:27:32,918
The national athlete of lunacy,
Chef Park Eun-yeong,

704
00:27:32,985 --> 00:27:37,189
versus the national athlete of bravado,
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

705
00:27:37,256 --> 00:27:38,724
LUNACY VS BRAVADO

706
00:27:38,790 --> 00:27:40,125
Korea operates on

707
00:27:40,192 --> 00:27:42,594
school ties, regional ties,
and familial ties.

708
00:27:44,429 --> 00:27:47,199
You can't say something like that
in this day and age.

709
00:27:47,266 --> 00:27:48,166
It's inevitable.

710
00:27:48,233 --> 00:27:49,835
He learned the wrong things in life.

711
00:27:49,902 --> 00:27:50,736
It's a bit…

712
00:27:50,802 --> 00:27:52,804
His bravado is a bit extreme today.

713
00:27:52,871 --> 00:27:54,673
Our relation may seem distant,

714
00:27:54,740 --> 00:27:56,141
but back in the Joseon era,

715
00:27:56,208 --> 00:27:59,077
we would've been close enough
to meet on every holiday.

716
00:27:59,144 --> 00:28:00,445
-We're family.
-I think

717
00:28:00,512 --> 00:28:02,748
they would've been strangers
even back then.

718
00:28:02,814 --> 00:28:04,349
-Totally.
-Even back then?

719
00:28:04,416 --> 00:28:06,618
If their relationship is this distant,

720
00:28:06,685 --> 00:28:09,121
I don't think Sang-uk is the type
who would care about that.

721
00:28:09,188 --> 00:28:11,056
-Of course.
-He wouldn't be swayed.

722
00:28:11,123 --> 00:28:13,158
I looked in the mirror
and asked a question.

723
00:28:13,225 --> 00:28:14,760
-The mirror?
-Mirror, mirror on the wall.

724
00:28:14,826 --> 00:28:16,295
Who do you think will win today?

725
00:28:17,596 --> 00:28:19,264
Of course, it's Chef Park Eun-yeong.

726
00:28:19,331 --> 00:28:20,799
Of course, it's Chef Park Eun-yeong.

727
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:23,602
-What was that?
-Are you serious?

728
00:28:23,669 --> 00:28:24,870
Do you prepare these things?

729
00:28:24,937 --> 00:28:26,405
Is this a musical or what?

730
00:28:26,471 --> 00:28:28,540
-Did something happen last week?
-I've never seen that.

731
00:28:30,375 --> 00:28:32,010
-Come on.
-She talks to mirrors.

732
00:28:32,344 --> 00:28:33,378
SIMPLY AMAZED…

733
00:28:33,445 --> 00:28:34,646
The national lunacy athlete.

734
00:28:34,713 --> 00:28:37,516
If lunacy was a competition,

735
00:28:37,583 --> 00:28:40,385
she'd win the grand slam.
Every single one.

736
00:28:40,452 --> 00:28:43,021
Both the individual event
and the team event.

737
00:28:43,088 --> 00:28:47,492
You're about to begin the item round.

738
00:28:47,559 --> 00:28:51,630
We believe both of you
have brought your secret weapon.

739
00:28:51,697 --> 00:28:54,733
Chef Eun-yeong, what's your item?

740
00:28:54,800 --> 00:28:56,535
I'll bring it.

741
00:28:56,602 --> 00:28:58,003
-Where is she going?
-Is it big?

742
00:28:58,070 --> 00:28:59,738
-What's that?
-What is it?

743
00:28:59,805 --> 00:29:00,772
Did you order that?

744
00:29:00,839 --> 00:29:01,873
Is that <i>jjajangmyeon</i>?

745
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:03,041
<i>-Jjajangmyeon</i>?
<i>-Jjajangmyeon</i>? Stop.

746
00:29:03,108 --> 00:29:04,810
-"Delivery, please."
-Did you get it delivered?

747
00:29:04,876 --> 00:29:06,178
What is it? Show us.

748
00:29:06,245 --> 00:29:08,380
My secret weapon is…

749
00:29:08,447 --> 00:29:11,116
My secret weapon is

750
00:29:11,183 --> 00:29:12,217
-dried sea cucumber.
-Sea cucumber.

751
00:29:12,284 --> 00:29:14,753
-She brought expensive stuff.
-Sea cucumbers?

752
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:16,421
-She brought expensive stuff.
-Sea cucumbers?

753
00:29:16,488 --> 00:29:18,423
-Sea cucumbers?
-Sea cucumbers?

754
00:29:18,490 --> 00:29:19,825
-Dried sea cucumbers.
-They're pricey.

755
00:29:19,891 --> 00:29:21,727
-They're so expensive.
-It's an ingredient.

756
00:29:21,793 --> 00:29:25,330
In Korea, we often eat it raw,

757
00:29:25,397 --> 00:29:29,101
but in Chinese cuisine,
we soak dried sea cucumbers.

758
00:29:29,167 --> 00:29:32,137
Once it's dried and soaked,
it becomes 30 times more expensive.

759
00:29:32,204 --> 00:29:35,274
So it's considered a luxury ingredient.

760
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:38,744
Even now, it's about
1.5 million won per kilogram.

761
00:29:38,810 --> 00:29:40,345
You're paying your way to victory.

762
00:29:40,412 --> 00:29:42,147
And you can't soak it overnight.

763
00:29:42,214 --> 00:29:44,516
<i>You have to spend days</i>

764
00:29:44,583 --> 00:29:46,952
<i>boiling it and then cooling it down.</i>

765
00:29:47,619 --> 00:29:51,189
Also, only the senior staff
can handle this ingredient.

766
00:29:51,256 --> 00:29:54,626
-Because of the price.
-And it melts easily in oil too.

767
00:29:54,693 --> 00:29:56,228
-I see.
-That's a good ingredient.

768
00:29:56,295 --> 00:29:59,064
Chef Eun-yeong brought
a precious ingredient

769
00:29:59,131 --> 00:30:01,366
-as her weapon of choice.
-That's really special.

770
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:04,403
CHEF EUN-YEONG'S INGREDIENTS:
BLUE CRAB, SHRIMP

771
00:30:04,469 --> 00:30:06,938
PORK BELLY, OYSTER SAUCE,
HOISIN SAUCE, DRIED SEA CUCUMBER

772
00:30:07,005 --> 00:30:08,940
Tell us about your dish, Chef Eun-yeong.

773
00:30:09,007 --> 00:30:10,475
Right now? Okay.

774
00:30:10,542 --> 00:30:11,643
I guess she's going to dance.

775
00:30:11,710 --> 00:30:13,912
-No, I'm not. I need this.
-What's she doing?

776
00:30:13,979 --> 00:30:15,180
She's onto something.

777
00:30:15,247 --> 00:30:16,615
-My dish is called…
-Yes?

778
00:30:16,682 --> 00:30:18,850
DeSabertroy Kwon Sung-jun.

779
00:30:19,284 --> 00:30:20,719
BUMP BUMP

780
00:30:22,187 --> 00:30:23,288
DeSabertroy?

781
00:30:23,355 --> 00:30:24,589
HER CUTE SABER STEPS

782
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:26,425
-DeSabertroy Kwon Sung-jun.
-That was funny.

783
00:30:26,491 --> 00:30:28,226
-DeSabertroy.
-DeSabertroy.

784
00:30:28,293 --> 00:30:29,428
You won.

785
00:30:29,494 --> 00:30:32,531
Sang-uk has given his all at the games,

786
00:30:32,597 --> 00:30:35,267
so I wanted to make something
to give him more energy.

787
00:30:35,334 --> 00:30:37,135
Sea cucumbers are the ginseng of the sea.

788
00:30:37,202 --> 00:30:38,470
So I will

789
00:30:38,537 --> 00:30:42,274
destroy seafood and meat that you love,
fill the sea cucumbers with it,

790
00:30:42,341 --> 00:30:45,210
then steam it to make steak,
a whole cucumber steak.

791
00:30:45,277 --> 00:30:47,012
-So with the sea's ginseng,
-Sounds so good.

792
00:30:47,079 --> 00:30:49,915
…I'll make you
a dish to help you recharge.

793
00:30:49,981 --> 00:30:53,318
Also, you said you liked <i>bibim-myeon,</i>

794
00:30:53,385 --> 00:30:55,687
so I'll make noodles from scratch

795
00:30:55,754 --> 00:30:57,422
and prepare seasoned noodles too.

796
00:30:57,989 --> 00:31:00,759
-Will she have enough time?
-It won't be easy.

797
00:31:00,826 --> 00:31:03,095
She's making <i>bibim-myeon</i>
that the brothers loved so much.

798
00:31:03,161 --> 00:31:05,163
Your mouth is already watering.

799
00:31:06,498 --> 00:31:09,067
Sung-jun, you're up against that.
What's your secret weapon?

800
00:31:09,134 --> 00:31:10,802
-Actually…
-Did you invite a relative?

801
00:31:10,869 --> 00:31:12,871
I considered bringing my uncle.

802
00:31:12,938 --> 00:31:15,140
-He thought of bringing his uncle.
-Then you'd win.

803
00:31:15,207 --> 00:31:17,242
Yes, if their uncle stood there…

804
00:31:17,309 --> 00:31:19,711
But I couldn't rely
too much on our relations,

805
00:31:19,778 --> 00:31:20,979
so I brought my friend.

806
00:31:21,046 --> 00:31:23,849
-Is your friend there?
-He's bringing it too.

807
00:31:23,915 --> 00:31:25,283
My secret weapon is this.

808
00:31:26,318 --> 00:31:27,886
-Huh? What's that?
-Levain.

809
00:31:27,953 --> 00:31:30,389
-It's dough that I made…
-Levain?

810
00:31:30,455 --> 00:31:31,490
…with my pet levain.

811
00:31:31,556 --> 00:31:32,758
-Dough?
-Dough?

812
00:31:32,824 --> 00:31:33,992
-Dough?
-Dough.

813
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:36,795
I brought it when I was a guest,
and I hoped one of you would use it,

814
00:31:36,862 --> 00:31:39,397
-but no one even looked at it.
-Right.

815
00:31:39,464 --> 00:31:41,566
So I brought it here to use it myself.

816
00:31:41,633 --> 00:31:42,968
Did you say three days?

817
00:31:43,034 --> 00:31:46,772
All you have to do
is throw them in water, right?

818
00:31:46,838 --> 00:31:48,774
But I made this dough myself

819
00:31:48,840 --> 00:31:50,509
with the levain I've kept for two years.

820
00:31:50,575 --> 00:31:52,911
-Doesn't it grow on its own?
-No, I feed it every day.

821
00:31:52,978 --> 00:31:56,181
<i>And then, I fermented it twice</i>
<i>over six and a half hours,</i>

822
00:31:56,248 --> 00:31:57,816
<i>then again in the fridge for 24 hours.</i>

823
00:31:57,883 --> 00:32:00,285
So I brought it in prime condition.

824
00:32:00,352 --> 00:32:02,154
-That's too precious.
-So is levain dough

825
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:04,256
on another level,
compared to regular dough?

826
00:32:04,322 --> 00:32:05,357
Completely.

827
00:32:06,291 --> 00:32:08,660
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS: YOUNG RADISH,
BACON, ALMOND, KING OYSTER MUSHROOM

828
00:32:08,727 --> 00:32:10,362
OYSTER MUSHROOM,
CUTLASSFISH INNARDS JEOTGAL, AND MORE

829
00:32:10,429 --> 00:32:12,531
What are you making, Sung-jun?

830
00:32:12,597 --> 00:32:14,232
The name of my dish is

831
00:32:15,066 --> 00:32:16,802
Pizza Is Thicker Than Water.

832
00:32:18,003 --> 00:32:18,870
TRYING NOT TO LAUGH

833
00:32:18,937 --> 00:32:21,039
That's so shameless. You're going there.

834
00:32:21,106 --> 00:32:24,142
Just say, "Blood is thicker than water."

835
00:32:24,209 --> 00:32:25,710
He had the conscience to leave it out.

836
00:32:25,777 --> 00:32:27,546
After all, I'm nicknamed Matpizza.

837
00:32:27,612 --> 00:32:30,348
I believe beer goes best with pizza.

838
00:32:30,415 --> 00:32:32,150
-Wait, pizza?
-But again,

839
00:32:32,217 --> 00:32:34,619
it'd be too boring to make regular pizza.

840
00:32:34,686 --> 00:32:38,657
So I'm going to make
traditional Neapolitan deep-fried pizza.

841
00:32:38,723 --> 00:32:39,958
-Deep-fried?
-He doesn't like that.

842
00:32:40,025 --> 00:32:41,726
He said he didn't like deep-fried dishes,

843
00:32:41,793 --> 00:32:43,361
but I have a feeling

844
00:32:43,428 --> 00:32:46,364
that he doesn't like that crispy texture.

845
00:32:46,431 --> 00:32:47,933
Mine will be different.

846
00:32:48,266 --> 00:32:49,668
AH…

847
00:32:49,734 --> 00:32:52,737
-Napoli Matpizza's deep-fried pizza.
-He's done.

848
00:32:52,804 --> 00:32:54,506
It'll be different because…

849
00:32:54,573 --> 00:32:57,042
What he prepared
doesn't go with his order.

850
00:32:57,108 --> 00:32:59,144
He brought levain,

851
00:32:59,211 --> 00:33:01,646
and he had to
use it for the order somehow.

852
00:33:01,713 --> 00:33:03,715
You brought levain,

853
00:33:03,782 --> 00:33:07,686
so you have to deep-fry something,
but Sang-uk said he didn't like that.

854
00:33:07,752 --> 00:33:11,256
So in the end, he claims
his deep-fried dish will be different.

855
00:33:11,323 --> 00:33:13,692
Deep-fried pizza does have
a different texture.

856
00:33:13,758 --> 00:33:15,493
-Really?
-It's different from normal fritters.

857
00:33:15,560 --> 00:33:17,696
-If it turns out right?
-Yes, it's different.

858
00:33:17,762 --> 00:33:20,332
-It's bread, so it's different.
-Kind of like donuts.

859
00:33:20,398 --> 00:33:22,634
Sam is helping him out.

860
00:33:22,701 --> 00:33:25,337
-Because it's true.
-Is it really?

861
00:33:25,403 --> 00:33:27,305
-He's not making it up?
-You two

862
00:33:27,372 --> 00:33:28,507
have been quite close recently.

863
00:33:29,074 --> 00:33:30,642
We've become closer.

864
00:33:30,709 --> 00:33:33,612
Thanks for pointing out
what levain can be like.

865
00:33:33,678 --> 00:33:36,348
We'll now begin the cook-off.

866
00:33:36,414 --> 00:33:40,418
Can you share
a little fencing jargon for our viewers?

867
00:33:40,485 --> 00:33:42,954
First, "ready, go."
To say "get ready," it's "<i>Prêts.</i>"

868
00:33:43,021 --> 00:33:44,890
<i>-Prêts.</i>
<i>-Prêts.</i>

869
00:33:44,956 --> 00:33:46,825
-<i>"Allez</i>" is when we start.
<i>-Allez.</i>

870
00:33:47,692 --> 00:33:48,927
<i>Prêts, allez.</i>

871
00:33:48,994 --> 00:33:50,962
<i>Prêts </i>for the cook-off.

872
00:33:51,029 --> 00:33:54,599
Let us begin the round,
"Delicious Beer SaberNacks."

873
00:33:54,666 --> 00:33:56,101
Give us the timer.

874
00:33:56,167 --> 00:33:57,869
-Wow.
-Sung-jun may…

875
00:33:57,936 --> 00:34:00,272
Gosh. I'm hungry.

876
00:34:00,338 --> 00:34:02,774
Sung-jun may claim to be Sang-uk's family,

877
00:34:02,841 --> 00:34:04,175
but she claims

878
00:34:04,242 --> 00:34:07,245
she's changed her last name to Oh
and has become Oh Eun-yeong.

879
00:34:07,312 --> 00:34:09,014
Oh Eun-yeong?

880
00:34:09,080 --> 00:34:10,749
He needs to learn a lesson.

881
00:34:10,815 --> 00:34:14,753
He claims his mom's sister's husband's
first cousin once removed is family.

882
00:34:14,819 --> 00:34:19,457
The troublemaker of his family,
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

883
00:34:19,524 --> 00:34:20,358
CHUCKLE

884
00:34:21,359 --> 00:34:23,762
Even I find it ridiculous.

885
00:34:23,828 --> 00:34:26,865
Which chef will seize
the honor of the family?

886
00:34:26,932 --> 00:34:29,668
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

887
00:34:29,734 --> 00:34:32,203
<i>-Allez!</i>
<i>-Allez!</i>

888
00:34:33,104 --> 00:34:35,173
THE BEER SNACK ROUND

889
00:34:36,007 --> 00:34:36,975
First, blue crabs.

890
00:34:37,042 --> 00:34:39,244
Chef Eun-yeong preps a blue crab.

891
00:34:39,711 --> 00:34:41,379
SANG-UK LOVES SEAFOOD

892
00:34:41,446 --> 00:34:42,714
Gosh.

893
00:34:42,781 --> 00:34:45,150
-That must be the filling.
-No.

894
00:34:45,216 --> 00:34:47,786
It's the topping for the noodles.

895
00:34:47,852 --> 00:34:49,955
On top of the seasoned noodles?

896
00:34:50,021 --> 00:34:51,790
He just clapped!

897
00:34:51,856 --> 00:34:55,226
-That sounds delicious.
-Crab noodles sound so good.

898
00:34:55,293 --> 00:34:57,629
We should call her
Chef Oh Eun-yeong today.

899
00:34:57,696 --> 00:35:00,465
-She's an Oh today.
-Chef Oh.

900
00:35:00,532 --> 00:35:02,901
She's here to discipline Chef Sung-jun.

901
00:35:02,968 --> 00:35:04,569
He's a troublemaker.

902
00:35:04,636 --> 00:35:06,972
Which chef will make
a more delicious bar snack?

903
00:35:07,038 --> 00:35:08,506
What are you cutting? Ham?

904
00:35:08,573 --> 00:35:10,141
-Bacon.
-Bacon.

905
00:35:10,208 --> 00:35:12,077
-I see.
-How's he going to fry pizza?

906
00:35:12,143 --> 00:35:16,681
1. COOK BACON, ONION,
AND MINCED GARLIC IN OLIVE OIL

907
00:35:16,948 --> 00:35:19,117
KING OYSTER MUSHROOM

908
00:35:19,184 --> 00:35:21,052
-What's he doing?
-He's not chopping right.

909
00:35:21,119 --> 00:35:23,922
Wait! Did I chop those mushrooms?

910
00:35:23,989 --> 00:35:25,991
-They didn't get separated.
-They're not cut.

911
00:35:26,157 --> 00:35:26,992
GLANCING

912
00:35:27,058 --> 00:35:28,560
Sung-jun, you can't do that.

913
00:35:28,927 --> 00:35:30,228
DOCTOR OH IS HERE

914
00:35:30,295 --> 00:35:31,663
Don't chop like that.

915
00:35:32,564 --> 00:35:34,866
Sung-jun looks flustered.

916
00:35:34,933 --> 00:35:37,235
It was an unexpected attack.

917
00:35:37,302 --> 00:35:38,870
I was wondering what's up,

918
00:35:38,937 --> 00:35:41,006
-and he didn't bring his knife.
-Really?

919
00:35:41,072 --> 00:35:43,575
-You forgot your knife?
-He was only thinking of his levain.

920
00:35:43,641 --> 00:35:46,044
I brought my secret weapon,
but forgot my knife.

921
00:35:46,111 --> 00:35:48,013
You only brought that and not the knife?

922
00:35:48,079 --> 00:35:49,848
But I believe Sang-uk has won before

923
00:35:49,914 --> 00:35:51,916
even with someone else's foil.

924
00:35:51,983 --> 00:35:54,452
OH SANG-UK WINS GOLD WITH A BORROWED FOIL
IN ASIAN CHAMPIONSHIPS

925
00:35:54,519 --> 00:35:56,688
I guess your family
shares that information.

926
00:35:56,755 --> 00:35:57,922
It's a family secret.

927
00:35:57,989 --> 00:35:59,958
EXPOSING THE FAMILY SECRET(?)

928
00:36:00,525 --> 00:36:02,227
He's using lots of mushrooms.

929
00:36:02,293 --> 00:36:03,862
I think he's using everything.

930
00:36:03,928 --> 00:36:05,063
Isn't that too much?

931
00:36:05,130 --> 00:36:07,365
-It's going inside anyway.
-I'll grind them.

932
00:36:07,432 --> 00:36:09,067
-You'll grind them?
-Yes.

933
00:36:09,134 --> 00:36:10,969
Grind them?

934
00:36:11,036 --> 00:36:13,138
He's grinding the toppings?

935
00:36:13,204 --> 00:36:15,540
-Is he using that as the topping?
-He'll probably stuff it…

936
00:36:15,607 --> 00:36:18,710
-…like a dumpling.
-That's correct.

937
00:36:18,777 --> 00:36:20,211
Eun-yeong, are you making noodles?

938
00:36:20,278 --> 00:36:22,681
Yes, I'm kneading dough to make noodles.

939
00:36:22,747 --> 00:36:24,716
Chef Oh Eun-yeong kneads the dough.

940
00:36:24,783 --> 00:36:26,885
You'll have to flatten it
to stop it from tearing.

941
00:36:26,951 --> 00:36:28,019
Is that used for Chinese food too?

942
00:36:28,086 --> 00:36:30,188
-It's my first time using it.
-The first time?

943
00:36:30,488 --> 00:36:32,390
A PASTA MACHINE

944
00:36:32,457 --> 00:36:34,793
-She's putting the dough through that.
-She made the dough.

945
00:36:34,859 --> 00:36:36,795
It'll break if you make it too thick.

946
00:36:36,861 --> 00:36:39,297
So you have to flatten the dough a bit.

947
00:36:39,364 --> 00:36:41,366
I once used that and ruined my dish.

948
00:36:42,367 --> 00:36:43,668
SPINNING AGAIN

949
00:36:43,735 --> 00:36:46,705
-Sung-jun, it's weird. It's not working!
-What?

950
00:36:46,771 --> 00:36:47,706
Is it the dough?

951
00:36:47,772 --> 00:36:49,607
-Sung-jun, help me out.
-I'll help.

952
00:36:49,674 --> 00:36:51,976
-You have to help.
-This isn't going to work.

953
00:36:52,043 --> 00:36:54,746
-Is it too runny?
-You need more flour.

954
00:36:54,813 --> 00:36:58,316
The troublemaker ran over and helped
when Dr. Oh needed him.

955
00:36:58,383 --> 00:36:59,751
THAT'S WHY THEY'RE FAMILY(?)

956
00:36:59,818 --> 00:37:03,088
-You don't even know how to use it.
-Sung-jun, you have to help me.

957
00:37:03,154 --> 00:37:05,223
-It looks a bit dry.
-Watch what I'm doing.

958
00:37:05,290 --> 00:37:07,292
Is it turning into noodles?

959
00:37:08,326 --> 00:37:09,627
MANY HOLES

960
00:37:09,694 --> 00:37:10,862
That's not how you do it!

961
00:37:10,929 --> 00:37:11,996
Is that how you do it?

962
00:37:12,063 --> 00:37:13,531
-Just fold it.
-There are holes.

963
00:37:13,598 --> 00:37:15,066
-Is that okay?
-Keep folding.

964
00:37:15,133 --> 00:37:16,534
-Really?
-Fold it a few more times.

965
00:37:16,601 --> 00:37:18,770
If you keep folding it,
it creates more gluten

966
00:37:18,837 --> 00:37:21,005
and makes the noodles chewier.

967
00:37:21,072 --> 00:37:22,307
And more springy.

968
00:37:22,373 --> 00:37:24,008
If you can't do it, tell me.

969
00:37:24,075 --> 00:37:26,144
-Just fold it a few more times.
-Thanks, Sung-jun.

970
00:37:26,211 --> 00:37:27,045
A few more times?

971
00:37:27,445 --> 00:37:29,748
DOING HER BEST TO REPEAT THE PROCESS

972
00:37:29,814 --> 00:37:30,715
Is it not working?

973
00:37:30,782 --> 00:37:32,984
-It is.
-It's working.

974
00:37:34,085 --> 00:37:36,521
One touch from Jong-won
will make the noodles.

975
00:37:37,956 --> 00:37:39,457
EPISODE 84, WHEN THE MACHINE DIDN'T WORK

976
00:37:39,757 --> 00:37:41,893
A STRATEGIC BLOCK OF THE CAMERA

977
00:37:42,627 --> 00:37:44,529
FINISHED WITHOUT A SINGLE WITNESS

978
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:46,131
Should I block the camera?

979
00:37:46,197 --> 00:37:47,665
Yes, block it.

980
00:37:48,533 --> 00:37:49,934
-I should go.
-Jong-won.

981
00:37:50,001 --> 00:37:51,469
-Jong-won runs over.
-Block it.

982
00:37:51,836 --> 00:37:53,538
LURKING AROUND

983
00:37:53,605 --> 00:37:54,572
Block the camera.

984
00:37:54,639 --> 00:37:56,207
BLOCKS

985
00:37:56,641 --> 00:37:57,809
MAXIMUM SECURITY

986
00:37:57,876 --> 00:37:59,244
Hey, wait!

987
00:37:59,978 --> 00:38:02,113
WHILE THE VIEW IS OBSCURED…

988
00:38:02,180 --> 00:38:04,549
-It looks great.
-It looks good.

989
00:38:04,616 --> 00:38:06,551
-See? It happened.
-It did.

990
00:38:06,618 --> 00:38:07,919
It looks great.

991
00:38:07,986 --> 00:38:10,355
It looks great!

992
00:38:10,421 --> 00:38:12,657
-It's like magic.
-It suddenly looks great.

993
00:38:12,724 --> 00:38:15,226
-It's such a wonder.
-Totally.

994
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:17,762
Chef Sung-jun isn't a troublemaker today,
but is a troubleshooter.

995
00:38:17,829 --> 00:38:19,797
He's hard at work.

996
00:38:19,864 --> 00:38:20,999
Is he blending tomatoes?

997
00:38:21,065 --> 00:38:22,600
-Are you blending tomatoes?
-Yes.

998
00:38:22,667 --> 00:38:25,937
Garlic, cutlassfish innards <i>jeotgal,</i>
and blended tomatoes.

999
00:38:26,004 --> 00:38:27,372
-With tomatoes?
-He's mixing them.

1000
00:38:27,438 --> 00:38:30,375
Sang-uk's favorite,
cutlassfish innards <i>jeotgal.</i>

1001
00:38:30,441 --> 00:38:32,110
You have ten minutes left.

1002
00:38:32,177 --> 00:38:34,746
Whoa. Time really flies.

1003
00:38:34,813 --> 00:38:36,981
-She's using the other side now.
-Hurry up and do it.

1004
00:38:37,048 --> 00:38:37,916
Five minutes have passed.

1005
00:38:37,982 --> 00:38:40,652
It won't be easy to make noodles.

1006
00:38:40,718 --> 00:38:42,854
She hasn't made
fresh noodles often on the show.

1007
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,123
Sung-jun was really quick.

1008
00:38:45,190 --> 00:38:46,691
I only need two minutes. What's she doing?

1009
00:38:47,292 --> 00:38:48,927
That'll turn into noodles, right?

1010
00:38:48,993 --> 00:38:50,528
Yes, there are the noodles.

1011
00:38:50,595 --> 00:38:52,797
-The noodles…
-They look good.

1012
00:38:52,864 --> 00:38:54,833
There it is. It looks great.

1013
00:38:54,899 --> 00:38:56,601
It's her first time,
but she's good with it.

1014
00:38:56,668 --> 00:38:59,637
ROLLING WITHOUT STOPPING

1015
00:38:59,704 --> 00:39:01,739
-Does it look good?
-It looks good!

1016
00:39:01,806 --> 00:39:03,608
-Thank you, Sung-jun.
-How did that happen?

1017
00:39:03,675 --> 00:39:04,842
"Thank you"?

1018
00:39:04,909 --> 00:39:06,311
That looks great.

1019
00:39:06,377 --> 00:39:08,413
-Nice job, Eun-yeong.
-I can't let that happen.

1020
00:39:08,479 --> 00:39:09,981
It looks like straightened hair.

1021
00:39:10,048 --> 00:39:12,417
-Yes.
-So soft and flowy.

1022
00:39:12,483 --> 00:39:14,586
-She must've used conditioner on it.
-Conditioner?

1023
00:39:14,886 --> 00:39:17,021
SILKY AND FLOWY

1024
00:39:17,088 --> 00:39:19,157
How does the thickness look?

1025
00:39:19,224 --> 00:39:20,859
They look thin.

1026
00:39:20,925 --> 00:39:23,228
-It looks excellent.
-Does it?

1027
00:39:23,294 --> 00:39:25,129
Eun-yeong cooks the noodles.

1028
00:39:25,196 --> 00:39:28,366
He blanched young radish
to turn it into pesto.

1029
00:39:28,433 --> 00:39:30,935
4. BLEND YOUNG RADISH, OLIVE OIL,
ALMONDS, AND PARMESAN

1030
00:39:31,002 --> 00:39:32,303
He's using lots of cheese.

1031
00:39:32,871 --> 00:39:34,806
-Lots of cheese.
-Why is he using that one

1032
00:39:34,873 --> 00:39:36,107
instead of the countertop one?

1033
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:39,444
It becomes too runny if I use that.

1034
00:39:39,510 --> 00:39:41,446
-I need some texture.
-For texture.

1035
00:39:42,013 --> 00:39:43,448
When's the pizza coming out?

1036
00:39:43,514 --> 00:39:45,183
When will you use your levain?

1037
00:39:45,250 --> 00:39:47,118
-Eight minutes left.
-Eight minutes.

1038
00:39:47,185 --> 00:39:48,887
Doesn't time fly by?

1039
00:39:48,953 --> 00:39:51,289
Yes, it's fascinating. They're so cool.

1040
00:39:51,356 --> 00:39:54,025
She's peeling shrimp.

1041
00:39:54,092 --> 00:39:55,627
Shrimp take lots of work.

1042
00:39:55,693 --> 00:39:59,097
-He won't appreciate anything too small.
-That's right.

1043
00:39:59,163 --> 00:40:01,866
The texture and bite
is important to him too.

1044
00:40:01,933 --> 00:40:04,269
-He's a big eater.
-Mussels or larger, no Manila clams.

1045
00:40:04,335 --> 00:40:05,870
-He's a big eater.
-He is.

1046
00:40:05,937 --> 00:40:08,139
He also said he liked seafood,

1047
00:40:08,206 --> 00:40:09,974
-so what she's using…
-Yes.

1048
00:40:10,041 --> 00:40:11,809
-…seems closer to his taste.
-True.

1049
00:40:11,876 --> 00:40:14,245
You're using pork belly now?

1050
00:40:14,312 --> 00:40:18,483
-Pork fat will make the filling flavorful.
-Yes, right.

1051
00:40:18,549 --> 00:40:20,818
I see. She's grinding everything.

1052
00:40:21,886 --> 00:40:23,454
SEA CUCUMBER STEAK FILLING, DONE

1053
00:40:24,289 --> 00:40:25,857
FINALLY, HER SECRET WEAPON

1054
00:40:25,924 --> 00:40:27,058
Sea cucumber.

1055
00:40:27,592 --> 00:40:28,726
SECRET WEAPON: DRIED SEA CUCUMBER

1056
00:40:28,793 --> 00:40:29,794
There's the sea cucumber.

1057
00:40:29,861 --> 00:40:32,830
-She grabs one.
-She's filling it.

1058
00:40:32,897 --> 00:40:35,066
-She said she'd make steak.
-She'll steam it.

1059
00:40:35,233 --> 00:40:36,200
INTO THE STEAMER

1060
00:40:36,267 --> 00:40:38,236
The blue crabs should be ready.

1061
00:40:38,303 --> 00:40:40,204
Which side will make a better bar snack?

1062
00:40:40,271 --> 00:40:41,506
Into the steamer.

1063
00:40:42,440 --> 00:40:44,342
-Levain.
-I see.

1064
00:40:44,409 --> 00:40:46,811
-He finally grabs his levain!
-There it is.

1065
00:40:47,278 --> 00:40:48,980
LOL

1066
00:40:49,047 --> 00:40:50,782
Who's laughing at my levain?

1067
00:40:50,848 --> 00:40:53,985
-There's the dough.
-It's been proofed nicely.

1068
00:40:54,052 --> 00:40:55,787
-It looks plump.
-It does.

1069
00:40:55,853 --> 00:40:58,423
-It looks like Sung-jun.
-You're right.

1070
00:40:58,489 --> 00:41:01,793
-He's working on his face.
-He's massaging himself.

1071
00:41:02,160 --> 00:41:04,195
POWERFUL MASSAGE

1072
00:41:04,262 --> 00:41:06,397
-They have the same tone.
-Is that the pizza dough?

1073
00:41:06,464 --> 00:41:08,166
-You're right.
-Right?

1074
00:41:08,633 --> 00:41:09,767
THE WEAPON HE GREW FOR 2 YEARS

1075
00:41:09,834 --> 00:41:12,203
-It already looks good.
-Six minutes.

1076
00:41:12,270 --> 00:41:15,406
Sung-jun uses his secret weapon.

1077
00:41:15,473 --> 00:41:17,842
Wait, don't you make pizza like this?

1078
00:41:17,909 --> 00:41:19,644
You spin the dough.

1079
00:41:19,711 --> 00:41:20,778
Like this.

1080
00:41:20,845 --> 00:41:22,480
-He should spin it.
-Why is he doing that?

1081
00:41:22,547 --> 00:41:23,614
He must have a plan.

1082
00:41:23,681 --> 00:41:25,883
I guess he'll grind everything.

1083
00:41:25,950 --> 00:41:28,353
GRINDING THE TOPPINGS

1084
00:41:29,187 --> 00:41:31,089
-I'm running out of time!
-The time!

1085
00:41:31,155 --> 00:41:32,190
I have no time.

1086
00:41:32,256 --> 00:41:33,358
-Five minutes.
-Yes.

1087
00:41:33,424 --> 00:41:35,493
Sung-ju, please report from over there.

1088
00:41:35,560 --> 00:41:37,929
Let me go and check.

1089
00:41:37,996 --> 00:41:39,831
Eun-yeong and Sung-jun.

1090
00:41:39,897 --> 00:41:41,632
-Five minutes.
-Lots of cheese?

1091
00:41:41,699 --> 00:41:43,534
You're laying out cheese first?

1092
00:41:43,601 --> 00:41:45,269
Isn't it sauce first?

1093
00:41:45,336 --> 00:41:47,171
You're doing that before the sauce?

1094
00:41:47,238 --> 00:41:49,474
What's he doing? Reversed pizza?

1095
00:41:49,540 --> 00:41:52,677
-He's going to fold it.
-It'll be like a dumpling.

1096
00:41:52,744 --> 00:41:54,946
-The levain looks nicely proofed.
-Sung-ju.

1097
00:41:55,013 --> 00:41:57,382
Try a bite of the levain.

1098
00:41:57,448 --> 00:41:58,850
Like this? Raw?

1099
00:41:58,916 --> 00:42:00,518
-Can I eat this raw?
-No.

1100
00:42:00,585 --> 00:42:02,653
-No, right? No!
-You're folding it like that?

1101
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:04,922
-He says no.
-It's fine, you can digest it.

1102
00:42:04,989 --> 00:42:07,025
-It looks nice and plump.
-Isn't that too big?

1103
00:42:07,091 --> 00:42:08,826
How's he going to deep-fry
that huge piece?

1104
00:42:08,893 --> 00:42:11,662
It looks plump and delicious.
I can already tell it'll be chewy.

1105
00:42:11,729 --> 00:42:13,931
-Does it look plump?
-Yes, it looks great.

1106
00:42:13,998 --> 00:42:15,400
The levain.

1107
00:42:15,767 --> 00:42:17,969
EUN-YEONG PUSHES CRAB MEAT OUT

1108
00:42:18,036 --> 00:42:21,038
-Blue crabs are in season right now.
-They're delicious.

1109
00:42:21,105 --> 00:42:22,173
So sweet.

1110
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:25,143
Noodles with crab meat? It's game over.

1111
00:42:25,410 --> 00:42:26,711
MAKING THE NOODLE SAUCE

1112
00:42:26,878 --> 00:42:28,513
SOY SAUCE, SUGAR

1113
00:42:28,579 --> 00:42:31,249
I'm using this for the noodles.

1114
00:42:31,315 --> 00:42:32,250
It looks delicious.

1115
00:42:32,817 --> 00:42:35,686
It must be soy sauce-based,
not something spicy.

1116
00:42:36,254 --> 00:42:37,088
Four minutes.

1117
00:42:37,155 --> 00:42:38,990
-No!
-Four minutes.

1118
00:42:39,056 --> 00:42:41,826
He needs to fry that
for at least eight minutes.

1119
00:42:41,893 --> 00:42:43,161
It has to be cooked.

1120
00:42:43,227 --> 00:42:44,328
Wait, what?

1121
00:42:45,263 --> 00:42:46,597
DUNKING THE LEVAIN

1122
00:42:46,664 --> 00:42:47,932
It might pop. Careful.

1123
00:42:47,999 --> 00:42:49,200
-It's in.
-Be careful.

1124
00:42:49,267 --> 00:42:51,202
-"My pizza's different."
-It won't pop.

1125
00:42:51,269 --> 00:42:54,005
Sang-uk said
he didn't like deep-fried food.

1126
00:42:54,072 --> 00:42:56,441
-He's deep-frying.
-So he's actually deep-frying that.

1127
00:42:56,507 --> 00:42:57,875
He's deep-frying the levain.

1128
00:42:57,942 --> 00:43:00,545
-He's frying it like that?
-This tomato sauce.

1129
00:43:00,611 --> 00:43:02,914
-It rose to the surface.
-This one…

1130
00:43:02,980 --> 00:43:04,615
It's like a submarine.

1131
00:43:04,682 --> 00:43:06,417
-It rose.
-It suddenly rose.

1132
00:43:06,484 --> 00:43:08,052
Let me try this one.

1133
00:43:08,119 --> 00:43:09,353
The sauce.

1134
00:43:09,420 --> 00:43:11,956
-You added cutlassfish <i>jeotgal</i> too.
-Cutlassfish innards <i>jeotgal.</i>

1135
00:43:12,023 --> 00:43:14,559
<i>-Jeotgal</i> in tomato sauce?
-Tomato sauce with <i>jeotgal.</i>

1136
00:43:14,625 --> 00:43:16,627
The tomato sauce.

1137
00:43:16,694 --> 00:43:18,563
Is pizza thicker than water?

1138
00:43:18,629 --> 00:43:20,765
Is pizza thicker than water?

1139
00:43:20,832 --> 00:43:22,166
It is!

1140
00:43:22,733 --> 00:43:24,368
Great job with this.

1141
00:43:24,435 --> 00:43:25,703
I thought it'd be bland, but no.

1142
00:43:25,770 --> 00:43:28,473
-It's thanks to the <i>jeotgal.</i>
-It must have lots of umami.

1143
00:43:28,539 --> 00:43:29,807
Three minutes and 14 seconds.

1144
00:43:30,074 --> 00:43:31,142
MAKING THE SAUCE FOR THE STEAK

1145
00:43:31,375 --> 00:43:33,244
3. FRY GREEN ONION, GARLIC,
HOISIN SAUCE, WATER

1146
00:43:33,311 --> 00:43:34,846
Three minutes left.

1147
00:43:34,912 --> 00:43:38,382
Eun-yeong is making noodles,
so she should finish first.

1148
00:43:39,951 --> 00:43:40,852
That's hot.

1149
00:43:41,519 --> 00:43:42,987
-Whoa.
-Careful.

1150
00:43:43,054 --> 00:43:44,722
That's the steak sauce.

1151
00:43:46,123 --> 00:43:48,493
-It's boiling over!
-It's boiling over!

1152
00:43:48,559 --> 00:43:51,195
-It's boiling over!
-It's hot!

1153
00:43:51,262 --> 00:43:53,564
-The noodles.
-It looks finished.

1154
00:43:53,631 --> 00:43:54,665
The noodles!

1155
00:43:54,732 --> 00:43:56,868
Is it good enough to destroy Sung-jun?

1156
00:43:56,934 --> 00:43:59,237
Is it good enough to destroy Sung-jun?

1157
00:43:59,303 --> 00:44:01,139
Yes, it's cooked. It's fine.

1158
00:44:01,205 --> 00:44:02,640
-Scoop them out.
-It's done.

1159
00:44:02,707 --> 00:44:04,876
Get them out. They're ready.

1160
00:44:04,942 --> 00:44:06,444
You need time to mix it with sauce.

1161
00:44:07,278 --> 00:44:08,479
You need time.

1162
00:44:08,546 --> 00:44:10,615
This pizza doesn't look conventional.

1163
00:44:10,681 --> 00:44:12,150
He's laying down the toppings first.

1164
00:44:12,216 --> 00:44:13,217
I'm almost done.

1165
00:44:13,284 --> 00:44:15,152
He laid out the toppings first,

1166
00:44:15,219 --> 00:44:17,622
and he'll probably put that on top.

1167
00:44:17,688 --> 00:44:20,625
Sang-uk, you've never had
anything that big deep-fried, right?

1168
00:44:20,691 --> 00:44:23,494
It's a first,
and I wonder what the texture is like.

1169
00:44:23,561 --> 00:44:26,097
-That makes the dough chewy.
-It does.

1170
00:44:26,163 --> 00:44:28,499
A minute and a half.

1171
00:44:28,566 --> 00:44:31,102
-Eun-yeong, you should hurry.
-Okay.

1172
00:44:31,435 --> 00:44:32,737
GREEN ONION & GARLIC, OUT

1173
00:44:33,704 --> 00:44:36,274
That sauce looks authentic.

1174
00:44:36,340 --> 00:44:37,508
Top it with the sea cucumber steak.

1175
00:44:37,575 --> 00:44:38,676
OPENS THE LID

1176
00:44:38,743 --> 00:44:40,111
-It's one bite.
-One bite.

1177
00:44:40,178 --> 00:44:41,445
That looks classy.

1178
00:44:42,146 --> 00:44:43,614
You should flip that dough.

1179
00:44:43,681 --> 00:44:46,050
-Will he flip the levain?
-You'd better flip it smoothly.

1180
00:44:46,117 --> 00:44:48,286
There it is. It looks all right.

1181
00:44:48,352 --> 00:44:50,454
-It looks good.
-It looks delicious.

1182
00:44:50,521 --> 00:44:52,890
He checks the time.

1183
00:44:52,957 --> 00:44:54,992
-He pours oil.
-This is new.

1184
00:44:55,059 --> 00:44:58,062
If you pour oil on top, the dough rises.

1185
00:44:58,129 --> 00:44:59,697
So you keep doing that?

1186
00:44:59,764 --> 00:45:01,766
-It needs to rise.
-It gets showered with oil.

1187
00:45:01,832 --> 00:45:04,702
-It needs the oil shower.
-Sang-uk may hate deep-fried dishes,

1188
00:45:04,769 --> 00:45:06,304
-but not this?
-It'll be different.

1189
00:45:06,370 --> 00:45:07,605
One minute left.

1190
00:45:07,672 --> 00:45:10,208
Please hurry up. It's time to wrap up.

1191
00:45:10,274 --> 00:45:13,177
-Eun-yeong washes the noodles.
-Fifty seconds, Eun-yeong.

1192
00:45:13,244 --> 00:45:14,812
Chef Oh… No, Park Eun-yeong.

1193
00:45:14,879 --> 00:45:16,847
They definitely need more time.

1194
00:45:17,081 --> 00:45:18,482
WORRIED

1195
00:45:18,549 --> 00:45:20,818
-She has to mix it. It's <i>bibim-myeon.</i>
-Forty seconds.

1196
00:45:20,885 --> 00:45:23,554
It's not the spicy sauce,
but soy sauce-based.

1197
00:45:23,621 --> 00:45:24,722
She's using crab meat too.

1198
00:45:24,789 --> 00:45:26,958
-Thirty seconds.
-Thirty seconds left.

1199
00:45:27,024 --> 00:45:29,160
-What? 30 seconds?
-Eun-yeong looks busy.

1200
00:45:29,727 --> 00:45:31,529
Eun-yeong looks busy.

1201
00:45:32,463 --> 00:45:34,198
-Won't that be too salty?
-It looks salty.

1202
00:45:34,265 --> 00:45:35,566
-It's fine.
-It is?

1203
00:45:35,633 --> 00:45:37,268
-You haven't tried it.
-It's okay.

1204
00:45:37,335 --> 00:45:38,936
BUT IT REALLY LOOKS SALTY…

1205
00:45:39,937 --> 00:45:41,005
-Wrap it up.
-No, it does look salty.

1206
00:45:41,305 --> 00:45:44,475
ADD MORE NOODLES

1207
00:45:44,542 --> 00:45:47,245
-Mix it.
-She's squeezing the sauce out.

1208
00:45:47,311 --> 00:45:49,714
Please hurry and wrap it up.

1209
00:45:50,781 --> 00:45:52,116
Shouldn't you cut it?

1210
00:45:53,884 --> 00:45:55,786
You're serving it like that?

1211
00:45:55,853 --> 00:45:57,822
You don't have much time.

1212
00:45:57,888 --> 00:46:00,191
Please wrap it up. He's finishing up.

1213
00:46:00,257 --> 00:46:01,525
Sung-jun is almost done.

1214
00:46:01,592 --> 00:46:02,793
Ten seconds left.

1215
00:46:02,860 --> 00:46:04,128
Ten. Nine.

1216
00:46:04,362 --> 00:46:05,196
GROUND PEPPER

1217
00:46:06,264 --> 00:46:08,466
KWON SUNG-JUN FINISHES FIRST

1218
00:46:08,833 --> 00:46:10,901
A NEVER-BEFORE-SEEN DEEP-FRIED PIZZA

1219
00:46:10,968 --> 00:46:14,138
-Ten.
-Ten. Sung-jun finishes.

1220
00:46:14,205 --> 00:46:15,206
-Sung-jun's done.
-Eight.

1221
00:46:15,273 --> 00:46:16,974
ARE THE NOODLES SEASONED WELL?

1222
00:46:17,608 --> 00:46:18,776
It's not too salty!

1223
00:46:18,843 --> 00:46:20,811
-It's not too salty!
-It's not?

1224
00:46:20,878 --> 00:46:22,546
CRAB MEAT

1225
00:46:22,613 --> 00:46:24,382
-Are you done?
-Need help?

1226
00:46:24,682 --> 00:46:25,716
OH EUN-YEONG ADDS SAUCE
AND FINISHES TOO

1227
00:46:25,783 --> 00:46:28,386
She's done. Park Eun-yeong finishes too.

1228
00:46:28,552 --> 00:46:29,553
CLAPPING

1229
00:46:29,620 --> 00:46:31,355
That was like a war.

1230
00:46:31,889 --> 00:46:34,492
WHICH DISH WILL GO BETTER WITH BEER?

1231
00:46:35,126 --> 00:46:37,194
What is this?

1232
00:46:37,261 --> 00:46:39,096
That's pizza?

1233
00:46:39,163 --> 00:46:40,398
Deep-fried pizza.

1234
00:46:40,464 --> 00:46:41,532
AMAZED

1235
00:46:41,599 --> 00:46:45,503
Everything has been finished for Sang-uk.

1236
00:46:45,569 --> 00:46:47,538
What did you think of the cook-off?

1237
00:46:47,605 --> 00:46:49,073
It was so cool.

1238
00:46:49,140 --> 00:46:50,708
<i>-It was?</i>
<i>-They moved swiftly,</i>

1239
00:46:50,775 --> 00:46:53,177
<i>and the dishes quickly came together.</i>

1240
00:46:53,244 --> 00:46:54,945
-It was so cool.
-Wasn't it?

1241
00:46:55,012 --> 00:46:56,313
Especially the final spurt.

1242
00:46:56,380 --> 00:46:59,717
I overheard Mi-mi say this

1243
00:47:00,284 --> 00:47:01,252
to Niel,

1244
00:47:01,319 --> 00:47:03,020
"These chefs are so cool."

1245
00:47:03,087 --> 00:47:04,455
She said, "Even more than athletes."

1246
00:47:04,522 --> 00:47:06,557
-Even more than athletes?
-Yes.

1247
00:47:07,658 --> 00:47:09,860
Aren't they so cool?

1248
00:47:09,927 --> 00:47:11,829
They seem cooler than athletes.

1249
00:47:11,896 --> 00:47:13,431
-Good cooks look really cool.
-Right?

1250
00:47:13,497 --> 00:47:16,534
-Did you think so?
-Yes, they seem cooler than athletes.

1251
00:47:16,600 --> 00:47:19,904
Let's see if the dishes turned out
as cool as the chefs?

1252
00:47:20,771 --> 00:47:24,342
DEEP-FRIED PIZZA

1253
00:47:24,408 --> 00:47:28,212
MADE WITH HIS SECRET WEAPON, LEVAIN

1254
00:47:28,979 --> 00:47:32,483
CHEF KWON SUNG-JUN'S
PIZZA IS THICKER THAN WATER

1255
00:47:32,550 --> 00:47:34,952
So this is apparently pizza.

1256
00:47:35,019 --> 00:47:38,756
The order was,
"Delicious Beer SaberNacks,"

1257
00:47:38,823 --> 00:47:41,792
-so we'll bring you some beer.
-Okay.

1258
00:47:42,159 --> 00:47:44,195
THE MAN WITH BEER STEPS UP

1259
00:47:44,261 --> 00:47:45,996
Gosh. You've brought glory to Korea.

1260
00:47:46,063 --> 00:47:47,131
He did.

1261
00:47:47,198 --> 00:47:49,633
Server Jung-hwan pours the drink himself.

1262
00:47:50,134 --> 00:47:51,969
-Tilt it a little.
-Yes.

1263
00:47:52,036 --> 00:47:54,538
The foam… That's it.

1264
00:47:54,605 --> 00:47:55,439
All right.

1265
00:47:55,806 --> 00:47:57,608
WOW♥

1266
00:47:57,675 --> 00:48:00,711
Is there anything else you need
when you drink beer?

1267
00:48:00,778 --> 00:48:01,846
CHUG

1268
00:48:01,912 --> 00:48:03,848
He's drinking the rest.

1269
00:48:03,914 --> 00:48:06,250
Because I'm an athlete,

1270
00:48:06,317 --> 00:48:09,520
I pay more attention to calories.

1271
00:48:09,587 --> 00:48:13,157
So I usually go for a lighter beer,
if possible.

1272
00:48:13,224 --> 00:48:14,058
As you probably do.

1273
00:48:14,125 --> 00:48:18,095
Now, please try the food
with that light beer.

1274
00:48:18,162 --> 00:48:19,630
-Thank you.
-Pizza Is Thicker Than Water.

1275
00:48:20,564 --> 00:48:23,134
-It'd go great with beer.
-It looks soft.

1276
00:48:23,200 --> 00:48:26,137
-Yes, that levain.
-It's not crunchy.

1277
00:48:26,203 --> 00:48:29,106
It looked amazingly plump and chewy.

1278
00:48:29,173 --> 00:48:31,709
-My levain.
-He kept pouring oil on top

1279
00:48:31,776 --> 00:48:33,310
and let it rise.

1280
00:48:33,377 --> 00:48:34,912
REVEALING LEVAIN'S INSIDE

1281
00:48:34,979 --> 00:48:36,313
Look at the stuffing.

1282
00:48:36,981 --> 00:48:38,015
Whoa. That's amazing.

1283
00:48:38,082 --> 00:48:39,550
AMAZED

1284
00:48:39,617 --> 00:48:41,218
There's something inside.

1285
00:48:41,285 --> 00:48:44,355
<i>I filled it with mozzarella</i>

1286
00:48:44,422 --> 00:48:46,290
<i>and the mushroom bacon pesto.</i>

1287
00:48:46,357 --> 00:48:48,592
You kept pouring oil over it,

1288
00:48:48,659 --> 00:48:50,194
yet it doesn't seem too greasy.

1289
00:48:50,261 --> 00:48:51,629
It's all about heat management.

1290
00:48:51,695 --> 00:48:55,199
<i>It doesn't absorb much oil if you start</i>
<i>on low heat and finish on high heat.</i>

1291
00:48:55,266 --> 00:48:58,302
-He didn't like fried food, so what now?
-That's right.

1292
00:48:58,369 --> 00:49:01,872
Will that troublemaker
successfully make him happy?

1293
00:49:02,940 --> 00:49:03,941
He takes a bite.

1294
00:49:05,342 --> 00:49:06,477
It'll go well with beer.

1295
00:49:06,544 --> 00:49:08,078
-Yes.
-Mmm.

1296
00:49:08,145 --> 00:49:10,681
-A beer snack.
-It reminds me of funnel cake.

1297
00:49:10,748 --> 00:49:12,449
It has to be good.

1298
00:49:12,516 --> 00:49:14,418
-But he doesn't like deep-fried food.
-Right.

1299
00:49:14,485 --> 00:49:16,420
He looks cautious.

1300
00:49:16,487 --> 00:49:18,355
Is it different from ordinary fritters?

1301
00:49:18,422 --> 00:49:19,723
-Completely.
-Completely?

1302
00:49:19,790 --> 00:49:21,592
I was imagining

1303
00:49:21,659 --> 00:49:26,130
something so crispy
that it'd be hard to the touch.

1304
00:49:26,197 --> 00:49:28,732
-But it's soft.
-It's soft?

1305
00:49:28,799 --> 00:49:31,335
Then you can use it as a neck pillow.

1306
00:49:32,570 --> 00:49:33,804
And that…

1307
00:49:34,672 --> 00:49:35,906
What was the dough again?

1308
00:49:35,973 --> 00:49:37,675
-Levain.
-Levain, yes.

1309
00:49:37,741 --> 00:49:39,510
-It has a hint of sourness.
-Levain?

1310
00:49:39,577 --> 00:49:41,111
<i>He says it tastes sour.</i>

1311
00:49:41,178 --> 00:49:42,847
<i>-It's been fermented.</i>
<i>-That's right.</i>

1312
00:49:42,913 --> 00:49:44,281
Levain comes with acidity.

1313
00:49:44,348 --> 00:49:46,450
-I see.
-That's levain.

1314
00:49:46,517 --> 00:49:49,019
What about the filling?
He added lots of mushrooms too.

1315
00:49:49,086 --> 00:49:50,221
Here's the thing.

1316
00:49:50,955 --> 00:49:52,756
I rarely ever eat cheese.

1317
00:49:53,057 --> 00:49:55,726
OUT OF THE PAN AND INTO THE FIRE

1318
00:49:56,060 --> 00:49:57,394
OH, DANG…

1319
00:49:57,461 --> 00:49:58,929
-He loves Korean food!
-Where's the light?

1320
00:49:58,996 --> 00:50:00,264
The emergency light.

1321
00:50:00,331 --> 00:50:01,532
USING THE HANDS INSTEAD

1322
00:50:01,599 --> 00:50:03,100
He takes it during his turn.

1323
00:50:03,167 --> 00:50:04,902
But it's not that bad.

1324
00:50:04,969 --> 00:50:06,904
I'm fine with this
as long as it's not crunchy.

1325
00:50:06,971 --> 00:50:10,307
-And the inside is so soft.
-And moist.

1326
00:50:10,374 --> 00:50:12,710
The texture is excellent.

1327
00:50:13,444 --> 00:50:16,747
Does it go well
with the tomato sauce and all?

1328
00:50:16,814 --> 00:50:18,282
The more I dip it, the better.

1329
00:50:18,349 --> 00:50:20,718
-The more, the better?
-Yes, I tried dipping it lightly.

1330
00:50:20,784 --> 00:50:22,386
So you need more sauce.

1331
00:50:22,453 --> 00:50:25,322
-Compared to the size of that pizza.
-Yes.

1332
00:50:25,389 --> 00:50:27,958
<i>He made the sauce</i>
<i>with cutlassfish innards </i>jeotgal.

1333
00:50:28,025 --> 00:50:29,793
-Right. The <i>jeotgal.</i>
-Yes.

1334
00:50:29,860 --> 00:50:31,795
-Cutlassfish sauce.
-That must be it.

1335
00:50:31,862 --> 00:50:33,797
He made it
with cutlassfish innards <i>jeotgal.</i>

1336
00:50:33,864 --> 00:50:35,132
YUM

1337
00:50:36,901 --> 00:50:38,502
THAT LOOKS SO GOOD…

1338
00:50:38,569 --> 00:50:40,938
-May I drink this?
-Of course.

1339
00:50:41,005 --> 00:50:43,707
You have to, because that was your order.

1340
00:50:43,774 --> 00:50:46,644
-See if it goes well.
-He loves to drink alone.

1341
00:50:46,710 --> 00:50:48,545
-All right.
-It's honestly so good.

1342
00:50:48,612 --> 00:50:49,847
Beer and pizza.

1343
00:50:49,914 --> 00:50:53,317
Fighting against that
is Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

1344
00:50:54,485 --> 00:50:58,756
DRIED SEA CUCUMBER STEAK
FILLED WITH SHRIMP MEAT

1345
00:51:00,357 --> 00:51:03,294
BIBIM-MYEON MADE FROM SCRATCH
WITH CRAB MEAT

1346
00:51:03,894 --> 00:51:07,131
CHEF PARK EUN-YEONG'S
DESABERTROY KWON SUNG-JUN

1347
00:51:07,731 --> 00:51:08,832
<i>Bibim-myeon</i> first.

1348
00:51:08,899 --> 00:51:10,100
Gosh.

1349
00:51:11,268 --> 00:51:13,304
-I love noodles.
-Right.

1350
00:51:13,370 --> 00:51:16,340
Sang-uk the noodle lover.

1351
00:51:16,840 --> 00:51:19,610
She made it from scratch,
and the thickness is important.

1352
00:51:19,677 --> 00:51:21,145
A BITE OF BIBIM-MYEON FIRST

1353
00:51:21,211 --> 00:51:22,046
SLURP

1354
00:51:22,813 --> 00:51:24,114
SLURP

1355
00:51:24,682 --> 00:51:26,583
-I hope it's seasoned well.
-Yes.

1356
00:51:26,650 --> 00:51:28,485
MOUTH FULL

1357
00:51:28,552 --> 00:51:29,853
Have it with crab meat.

1358
00:51:29,920 --> 00:51:30,854
With crab meat.

1359
00:51:31,655 --> 00:51:34,058
It's not every day
that you get to eat noodles with crab.

1360
00:51:35,259 --> 00:51:36,226
GULP

1361
00:51:37,428 --> 00:51:38,996
SMILING

1362
00:51:39,363 --> 00:51:40,197
It's good.

1363
00:51:40,264 --> 00:51:43,200
-He smiled automatically.
-It's an emergency.

1364
00:51:43,267 --> 00:51:44,468
Emergency.

1365
00:51:44,535 --> 00:51:46,270
EMERGENCY, KWON SUNG-JUN

1366
00:51:46,337 --> 00:51:48,572
He gladly moves on to the sea cucumber.

1367
00:51:48,639 --> 00:51:51,909
He's moving on immediately
without leaving a comment.

1368
00:51:51,976 --> 00:51:54,345
Try the sea cucumber
with a good amount of sauce.

1369
00:51:54,411 --> 00:51:56,480
-Lots of sauce?
-If that's good too, then…

1370
00:51:56,547 --> 00:51:57,915
It's a precious ingredient.

1371
00:51:57,982 --> 00:51:59,249
-It's expensive.
-Yes.

1372
00:51:59,316 --> 00:52:01,585
I couldn't have it often
when I was in China.

1373
00:52:01,652 --> 00:52:02,486
Exactly.

1374
00:52:02,553 --> 00:52:04,822
Isn't that a snack for kaoliang liquor?

1375
00:52:04,888 --> 00:52:06,357
It has umami, so it goes well with beer.

1376
00:52:06,423 --> 00:52:08,959
The appetizer was the noodles,

1377
00:52:09,026 --> 00:52:10,394
and this is the main dish.

1378
00:52:10,461 --> 00:52:12,229
Tell us about it. What's it like?

1379
00:52:12,296 --> 00:52:15,032
I get why she wanted me
to eat it with the crab meat.

1380
00:52:15,099 --> 00:52:16,634
It's so good together.

1381
00:52:17,468 --> 00:52:19,903
I also thought it'd be too salty

1382
00:52:19,970 --> 00:52:22,039
because of the soy sauce.

1383
00:52:22,106 --> 00:52:25,042
But it's seasoned perfectly.

1384
00:52:25,109 --> 00:52:26,276
What about the thickness?

1385
00:52:26,343 --> 00:52:28,078
It's great.

1386
00:52:28,145 --> 00:52:30,781
-Is it seasoned well?
-It is.

1387
00:52:30,848 --> 00:52:34,585
Would it have been better
if she used spicy sauce?

1388
00:52:34,652 --> 00:52:37,588
I would prefer that, actually.

1389
00:52:37,654 --> 00:52:39,023
You should've added chili powder.

1390
00:52:39,089 --> 00:52:41,225
EMERGENCY, PARK EUN-YEONG

1391
00:52:41,291 --> 00:52:42,793
-I still have a chance.
-That's the <i>bibim-myeon.</i>

1392
00:52:42,860 --> 00:52:43,994
What about the sea cucumber?

1393
00:52:44,061 --> 00:52:46,730
It tasted like a very classy dish

1394
00:52:46,797 --> 00:52:49,066
I'd order at a Chinese restaurant.

1395
00:52:49,133 --> 00:52:51,468
And I really like the sauce.

1396
00:52:51,535 --> 00:52:53,804
-He loves the sauce.
-Also,

1397
00:52:53,871 --> 00:52:55,973
the texture of this sea cucumber

1398
00:52:56,040 --> 00:52:57,608
was my absolute favorite.

1399
00:52:57,908 --> 00:52:58,976
AGAIN, EMERGENCY, KWON SUNG-JUN

1400
00:52:59,043 --> 00:53:00,978
Texture's important to me.

1401
00:53:01,045 --> 00:53:04,448
It would've been great
if she made it with spicy sauce.

1402
00:53:04,515 --> 00:53:07,151
-Yes.
-But even the soy sauce one…

1403
00:53:07,217 --> 00:53:08,585
HE LOVES ALL KINDS OF NOODLES

1404
00:53:10,354 --> 00:53:11,822
He must really love noodles.

1405
00:53:11,889 --> 00:53:14,525
But they need to go well with beer.

1406
00:53:14,591 --> 00:53:16,226
Delicious Beer SaberNacks.

1407
00:53:16,293 --> 00:53:19,163
He drinks so well. Look at him.

1408
00:53:19,697 --> 00:53:20,998
All right.

1409
00:53:21,065 --> 00:53:22,933
GOSH…

1410
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:27,538
Please try Chef Sung-jun's dish.

1411
00:53:27,604 --> 00:53:29,073
Mi-mi, try some.

1412
00:53:29,139 --> 00:53:30,974
Daniel has a nut allergy,

1413
00:53:31,041 --> 00:53:32,810
and that was made with nuts.

1414
00:53:32,876 --> 00:53:35,512
This bread.
It turned out like actual bread.

1415
00:53:35,779 --> 00:53:37,581
THE LEVAIN EXPERT TAKES A BITE TOO

1416
00:53:38,315 --> 00:53:39,616
It's tender.

1417
00:53:39,683 --> 00:53:43,220
It doesn't feel like it's deep-fried.

1418
00:53:43,287 --> 00:53:44,521
Whoa. This is good.

1419
00:53:44,588 --> 00:53:45,522
It's so good.

1420
00:53:45,589 --> 00:53:47,591
-Is it? Whoa.
-It's good.

1421
00:53:47,658 --> 00:53:50,227
I see why he fermented this for days.

1422
00:53:53,997 --> 00:53:56,400
EATS WELL

1423
00:53:56,467 --> 00:53:58,302
-So this is levain.
-That's right.

1424
00:53:58,368 --> 00:54:00,070
Isn't it good?

1425
00:54:00,137 --> 00:54:02,706
Mi-mi eats so well.

1426
00:54:02,773 --> 00:54:04,741
So? What's it like?

1427
00:54:04,808 --> 00:54:07,010
I wouldn't call it pizza,

1428
00:54:07,077 --> 00:54:09,146
but it didn't seem like bread either.

1429
00:54:09,213 --> 00:54:10,514
Do you get what I mean?

1430
00:54:10,581 --> 00:54:12,382
-Somewhere in the middle?
-Yes.

1431
00:54:12,449 --> 00:54:15,753
-Pizza bread?
-It's similar to pizza bread.

1432
00:54:15,819 --> 00:54:18,522
But it tastes much classier than that.

1433
00:54:18,589 --> 00:54:21,492
It's like that sticky rice donut.

1434
00:54:21,558 --> 00:54:24,328
On the outside, it has that donut texture,

1435
00:54:24,394 --> 00:54:28,232
<i>but inside, it's pillowy like focaccia,</i>

1436
00:54:28,298 --> 00:54:29,766
<i>-and it tastes like pizza.</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

1437
00:54:29,833 --> 00:54:32,436
Sam, do you think
the levain turned out nicely?

1438
00:54:32,503 --> 00:54:34,037
Yes, because levain

1439
00:54:34,705 --> 00:54:37,441
is supposed to be sour on the inside.

1440
00:54:37,508 --> 00:54:38,675
And I could taste that too.

1441
00:54:38,742 --> 00:54:41,845
I love this fermented smell.

1442
00:54:41,912 --> 00:54:44,681
Next, Chef Eun-yeong's dish.

1443
00:54:45,182 --> 00:54:47,151
NIEL'S FIRST BITE

1444
00:54:47,317 --> 00:54:48,152
SLURP

1445
00:54:49,286 --> 00:54:52,055
He's finishing the noodles until the end.

1446
00:54:52,122 --> 00:54:53,624
-He eats well.
-Yes.

1447
00:54:53,690 --> 00:54:55,993
Sang-uk eats so well too.

1448
00:54:56,860 --> 00:54:58,328
It tastes so refined.

1449
00:54:58,395 --> 00:55:01,965
DEAD SERIOUS

1450
00:55:02,032 --> 00:55:03,734
He's like a cooking contest judge,

1451
00:55:03,801 --> 00:55:06,003
focusing on the dish.

1452
00:55:06,069 --> 00:55:07,771
-Yes.
-We have to wait.

1453
00:55:07,838 --> 00:55:10,340
Daniel even licks the sauce off the knife.

1454
00:55:10,407 --> 00:55:11,642
What do you think?

1455
00:55:11,708 --> 00:55:13,610
I've never had
this kind of texture in my life.

1456
00:55:13,677 --> 00:55:15,612
You've never had anything like it?

1457
00:55:15,679 --> 00:55:18,015
I personally don't prefer seafood,

1458
00:55:18,081 --> 00:55:20,384
but if all seafood dishes were like this,

1459
00:55:20,450 --> 00:55:21,718
I'd eat it every day.

1460
00:55:21,785 --> 00:55:23,120
HE WANTS IT EVERY DAY

1461
00:55:23,187 --> 00:55:25,455
-And the sauce was delicious.
-Was the sauce good?

1462
00:55:25,522 --> 00:55:28,258
It was so soft in my mouth,

1463
00:55:28,325 --> 00:55:30,794
and it was something
I'd probably never have again in my life.

1464
00:55:31,161 --> 00:55:32,429
A ONE-TIME EXPERIENCE

1465
00:55:32,496 --> 00:55:34,464
She feels like she won't
have anything like this again.

1466
00:55:34,531 --> 00:55:36,366
Did it taste that sophisticated?

1467
00:55:36,433 --> 00:55:40,037
-It would definitely give me more energy.
-It'd give her energy.

1468
00:55:40,103 --> 00:55:42,272
The sauce was similar
to that of Dongpo pork.

1469
00:55:42,339 --> 00:55:44,041
-I see.
-Dongpo pork.

1470
00:55:44,107 --> 00:55:46,777
-We couldn't give you much.
-It's so refined.

1471
00:55:46,844 --> 00:55:49,580
It's the kind of sauce
you'd eat at a classy Chinese restaurant.

1472
00:55:49,646 --> 00:55:50,647
They both brought

1473
00:55:50,714 --> 00:55:55,519
the absolute best ingredients
they could bring today.

1474
00:55:55,586 --> 00:55:58,255
All right, then. It's time to choose.

1475
00:55:58,322 --> 00:56:00,457
<i>Press one for Eun-yeong</i>

1476
00:56:00,524 --> 00:56:02,726
<i>and two for Sung-jun.</i>

1477
00:56:02,793 --> 00:56:06,163
Sang-uk, the two-time gold medalist
of the Aichi-Nagoya Asian Games.

1478
00:56:06,230 --> 00:56:07,564
-Did you choose?
-I did.

1479
00:56:07,631 --> 00:56:11,034
This was their
first individual cook-off this year.

1480
00:56:11,101 --> 00:56:13,403
Let's find out the result. Show us!

1481
00:56:14,738 --> 00:56:17,074
Delicious Beer SaberNacks.

1482
00:56:17,140 --> 00:56:20,744
The distant relative
who claims to be Sang-uk's family,

1483
00:56:20,811 --> 00:56:22,212
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

1484
00:56:22,279 --> 00:56:25,082
"If you're his family, I'm Oh Eun-yeong,"

1485
00:56:25,148 --> 00:56:26,483
says Chef Park Eun-yeong.

1486
00:56:26,550 --> 00:56:29,419
Their first individual cook-off this year.

1487
00:56:29,486 --> 00:56:32,389
Who did Sang-uk choose?

1488
00:56:32,456 --> 00:56:34,324
Show us the result!

1489
00:56:34,992 --> 00:56:36,193
What's better with beer?

1490
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:37,961
-Whoa!
-Kwon Sung-jun!

1491
00:56:38,295 --> 00:56:40,197
CHEF KWON SUNG-JUN'S
PIZZA IS THICKER THAN WATER WINS

1492
00:56:40,264 --> 00:56:42,499
-Sung-jun wins.
-Great job.

1493
00:56:42,566 --> 00:56:45,235
The distant relative wins.

1494
00:56:45,302 --> 00:56:46,136
I knew it.

1495
00:56:46,203 --> 00:56:47,771
-My gosh.
-I can't believe it.

1496
00:56:47,838 --> 00:56:49,940
This could cause a misunderstanding.

1497
00:56:50,007 --> 00:56:51,842
-It's not about that.
-No?

1498
00:56:51,909 --> 00:56:53,310
What was the reason?

1499
00:56:53,377 --> 00:56:55,979
-What he made for me…
-Pizza Is Thicker Than Water.

1500
00:56:56,046 --> 00:57:00,083
<i>…immediately made me crave beer.</i>

1501
00:57:00,150 --> 00:57:03,587
I felt like I had to drink something.

1502
00:57:03,654 --> 00:57:08,558
But this was closer to an actual meal.

1503
00:57:08,625 --> 00:57:10,961
-Pizza and beer are great.
-Yes.

1504
00:57:11,028 --> 00:57:12,796
Are you doing this

1505
00:57:12,863 --> 00:57:14,731
because of your family?

1506
00:57:14,798 --> 00:57:15,899
No, right?

1507
00:57:15,966 --> 00:57:18,168
-Not at all.
-That is not the case.

1508
00:57:18,235 --> 00:57:22,706
Please put a star
on Chef Sung-jun's chest.

1509
00:57:22,773 --> 00:57:24,741
Right above his name tag.

1510
00:57:24,808 --> 00:57:27,577
-Congratulations, Chef Sung-jun!
-Thank you.

1511
00:57:27,644 --> 00:57:28,478
NOW TYING WITH HO-YOUNG!

1512
00:57:29,179 --> 00:57:33,116
<i>The second round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with Sang-uk's ingredients.</i>

1513
00:57:33,183 --> 00:57:36,053
Chef Choi Hyun-suk of the 190 Club,

1514
00:57:36,119 --> 00:57:39,122
versus Chef Son Jong-won of the 180 Club.

1515
00:57:39,189 --> 00:57:41,191
The two tall chefs.

1516
00:57:41,258 --> 00:57:43,827
They're national chefs
who always cook for important guests,

1517
00:57:43,894 --> 00:57:45,896
those who brought honor to our country.

1518
00:57:45,962 --> 00:57:49,399
<i>Chef & My Fridge</i>
is a classy cooking and talk show,

1519
00:57:49,466 --> 00:57:52,769
but it's also a place for
professional chefs to show their caliber.

1520
00:57:52,836 --> 00:57:54,204
Yes, of course.

1521
00:57:54,271 --> 00:57:56,173
You're about to witness our show's best.

1522
00:57:56,540 --> 00:57:57,741
FULL OF EXCITEMENT

1523
00:57:57,808 --> 00:58:01,311
He's saying, "Practically, the two of us
are the only professionals here."

1524
00:58:01,378 --> 00:58:03,046
-Is that it?
-No, everyone is.

1525
00:58:03,113 --> 00:58:04,982
Not Sam, not Ho-young.

1526
00:58:05,048 --> 00:58:06,249
We're the comic relief.

1527
00:58:06,316 --> 00:58:07,951
-The comic relief?
-We're nothing?

1528
00:58:08,018 --> 00:58:09,252
We're nothing?

1529
00:58:09,319 --> 00:58:11,555
-We're just extras?
-They're all great chefs.

1530
00:58:11,621 --> 00:58:13,824
But this will be an unpredictable
neck-and-neck match.

1531
00:58:13,890 --> 00:58:17,194
And right now,
I'd like to congratulate Sang-uk.

1532
00:58:17,260 --> 00:58:19,296
You're about to taste
the best dish of your life.

1533
00:58:19,363 --> 00:58:21,031
-He's saying that now.
-Right now.

1534
00:58:21,098 --> 00:58:23,166
It's because he'll get to eat
Jong-won's dish.

1535
00:58:23,233 --> 00:58:25,936
-You're about to eat that.
-I'm jealous.

1536
00:58:26,003 --> 00:58:27,804
-Thank you.
-I'm jealous too.

1537
00:58:27,871 --> 00:58:29,773
We don't know how it'll go.

1538
00:58:29,840 --> 00:58:31,408
Chef Jong-won makes amazing food.

1539
00:58:31,475 --> 00:58:34,344
I came with a lighter heart today.

1540
00:58:34,411 --> 00:58:37,581
I get to be here with great chefs,
awesome hosts, and wonderful guests.

1541
00:58:37,647 --> 00:58:38,982
TOUCHED

1542
00:58:39,049 --> 00:58:40,717
-So I'll cook with a good heart.
-He has class.

1543
00:58:40,784 --> 00:58:42,686
-Give him a hand!
-He's different.

1544
00:58:42,753 --> 00:58:44,654
-This is sportsmanship.
-Sportsmanship.

1545
00:58:44,921 --> 00:58:46,556
"SON JONG-WON"

1546
00:58:46,623 --> 00:58:48,492
He's already won.

1547
00:58:48,558 --> 00:58:49,893
Hyun-suk was harsh,

1548
00:58:49,960 --> 00:58:52,896
-but he showed sportsmanship.
-Sportsmanship.

1549
00:58:53,663 --> 00:58:55,232
Fine. I'm the jerk.

1550
00:58:55,465 --> 00:58:56,933
HAVING FUN

1551
00:58:57,000 --> 00:58:59,936
Anyway, in the round before this one,

1552
00:59:00,003 --> 00:59:02,406
the secret weapon played a decisive role.

1553
00:59:02,472 --> 00:59:05,776
I'm sure you two brought
your secret weapons too.

1554
00:59:05,842 --> 00:59:08,545
Chef Hyun-suk, tell us about yours.

1555
00:59:08,612 --> 00:59:10,280
CURIOUS

1556
00:59:10,347 --> 00:59:12,682
-It's covered.
-Did he bring doves?

1557
00:59:12,749 --> 00:59:14,785
-Are those doves?
-Is he a magician today?

1558
00:59:14,851 --> 00:59:16,820
Is he about to release doves?

1559
00:59:17,120 --> 00:59:18,688
FLUTTER

1560
00:59:18,755 --> 00:59:21,058
-Here's my secret weapon.
-Did he join a circus?

1561
00:59:21,124 --> 00:59:22,926
This is his secret weapon.

1562
00:59:22,993 --> 00:59:24,027
-Whoa.
-Whoa.

1563
00:59:24,094 --> 00:59:24,928
-His signature item.
-What is it?

1564
00:59:25,328 --> 00:59:27,164
WHAT IS THAT?

1565
00:59:27,230 --> 00:59:28,632
-An oxygen tank?
-Wait, no.

1566
00:59:28,698 --> 00:59:31,334
My secret weapon

1567
00:59:31,401 --> 00:59:33,336
-is nitrogen.
-Nitrogen?

1568
00:59:33,403 --> 00:59:35,939
-Liquid nitrogen.
-Liquid nitrogen?

1569
00:59:36,006 --> 00:59:38,275
For molecular gastronomy, right?

1570
00:59:38,341 --> 00:59:39,543
Yes, that's right.

1571
00:59:39,609 --> 00:59:41,244
That fad is long gone.

1572
00:59:43,580 --> 00:59:45,348
No, don't say that.

1573
00:59:45,415 --> 00:59:47,350
That's his signature, so stop it.

1574
00:59:47,417 --> 00:59:49,419
It went out of trend ages ago.

1575
00:59:49,486 --> 00:59:51,455
It was once popular.

1576
00:59:51,521 --> 00:59:52,989
He was the pioneer.

1577
00:59:53,056 --> 00:59:54,758
That's his signature.

1578
00:59:54,825 --> 00:59:57,060
Nothing goes out of fad in food.

1579
00:59:57,127 --> 00:59:58,862
He's triggered.

1580
00:59:58,929 --> 01:00:01,998
I'll use liquid nitrogen
to create a new texture

1581
01:00:02,065 --> 01:00:03,934
and present the best dish for you.

1582
01:00:04,000 --> 01:00:05,802
-So I've prepared…
-We get to see it live?

1583
01:00:05,869 --> 01:00:08,238
…liquid nitrogen and a Dewar flask.

1584
01:00:08,972 --> 01:00:13,877
Also, the nitrogen gas tank,
and siphon guns to put the gas in.

1585
01:00:13,944 --> 01:00:15,145
How many items did he bring?

1586
01:00:15,212 --> 01:00:17,414
-You brought four items.
-That's a lot.

1587
01:00:17,481 --> 01:00:19,516
-You've brought a lot.
-Wait.

1588
01:00:19,583 --> 01:00:21,351
My secret weapon is nitrogen,

1589
01:00:21,418 --> 01:00:23,753
but I can't carry liquid nitrogen
and nitrogen gas

1590
01:00:23,820 --> 01:00:25,255
in my hands or in a plastic bag.

1591
01:00:25,822 --> 01:00:27,924
There's nitrogen gas in potato chips.

1592
01:00:27,991 --> 01:00:29,793
-Yes, they're full.
-They are.

1593
01:00:29,860 --> 01:00:32,095
The pressure is too low,
so I can't add it anywhere.

1594
01:00:32,162 --> 01:00:33,063
Really?

1595
01:00:33,130 --> 01:00:36,099
If you have no professional knowledge,
don't jump in when chefs talk.

1596
01:00:36,166 --> 01:00:37,200
Uh-huh.

1597
01:00:37,267 --> 01:00:38,668
Okay.

1598
01:00:38,935 --> 01:00:40,370
UNFAZED

1599
01:00:40,437 --> 01:00:41,471
So…

1600
01:00:41,538 --> 01:00:43,807
I can use nitrogen gas
to freeze his tongue.

1601
01:00:43,874 --> 01:00:45,542
-You want to freeze this?
-Quick-freeze.

1602
01:00:45,609 --> 01:00:47,177
Was that why you brought that?

1603
01:00:47,244 --> 01:00:48,111
THE POWER OF -196°C

1604
01:00:48,178 --> 01:00:52,482
Chef Hyun-suk brought a strong weapon.
We'll see molecular gastronomy today.

1605
01:00:53,316 --> 01:00:55,385
CHEF HYUN-SUK'S INGREDIENTS:
CUTLASSFISH, SCALLOP, EGGPLANT

1606
01:00:55,452 --> 01:00:57,487
ASPARAGUS, PINE NUT, WHITE KIMCHI,
STRAWBERRY JAM, NITROGEN, AND MORE

1607
01:00:57,554 --> 01:00:59,422
The name of my dish is

1608
01:01:00,357 --> 01:01:02,392
Molecular Gastronomy Is Easy, Isn't It?

1609
01:01:02,893 --> 01:01:04,528
-That again?
-Bob Ross?

1610
01:01:04,594 --> 01:01:05,929
THE TALKY GRANDPA BOB

1611
01:01:05,996 --> 01:01:07,364
-Bob Ross.
-We've seen it.

1612
01:01:07,430 --> 01:01:08,999
"Easy, isn't it?"

1613
01:01:09,065 --> 01:01:11,935
I'll use scallops
and make a cold appetizer.

1614
01:01:12,002 --> 01:01:13,837
Scallops as an appetizer.

1615
01:01:13,904 --> 01:01:16,473
And I'll use cutlassfish and steam it.

1616
01:01:16,540 --> 01:01:18,542
-Cutlassfish <i>jjim.</i>
-Those two dishes.

1617
01:01:18,608 --> 01:01:20,377
They both sound ordinary.

1618
01:01:20,443 --> 01:01:24,414
-How's he going to use it with nitrogen?
-I wonder.

1619
01:01:24,481 --> 01:01:27,851
What kind of secret weapon
did Chef Jong-won bring?

1620
01:01:27,918 --> 01:01:30,120
He always brings huge bags like this.

1621
01:01:30,187 --> 01:01:31,988
Chef Jong-won's secret weapon.

1622
01:01:32,055 --> 01:01:35,559
Wait, I didn't bring my knife.

1623
01:01:35,625 --> 01:01:37,594
-You can't forget your knife.
-Should we ask?

1624
01:01:37,661 --> 01:01:39,496
I think I forgot to bring them.

1625
01:01:39,563 --> 01:01:42,165
-But then, I think I have one there.
-Really?

1626
01:01:42,232 --> 01:01:43,767
-Is it a special knife?
-May I bring it?

1627
01:01:43,833 --> 01:01:45,168
-A special knife.
-Is it your weapon?

1628
01:01:45,235 --> 01:01:47,037
-May I have my knife?
-The knife, please.

1629
01:01:47,103 --> 01:01:48,705
Please bring the knife.

1630
01:01:48,772 --> 01:01:50,173
Is there a bag… What?

1631
01:01:50,540 --> 01:01:51,708
WAIT

1632
01:01:51,775 --> 01:01:52,642
THAT GUY

1633
01:01:52,709 --> 01:01:54,177
Please bring the knife.

1634
01:01:54,244 --> 01:01:56,713
WALKS IN WITH THE KNIVES

1635
01:01:57,514 --> 01:01:58,782
-His right-hand man?
-A person?

1636
01:01:59,115 --> 01:02:00,050
BUZZING

1637
01:02:00,116 --> 01:02:01,518
-The human knife?
-It's a person.

1638
01:02:01,585 --> 01:02:04,554
-That's Chef Woo Do-hee.
-Chef Woo Do-hee.

1639
01:02:04,921 --> 01:02:07,123
CAME AS JONG-WON'S RIGHT-HAND MAN
ON EPISODE 78

1640
01:02:07,190 --> 01:02:08,458
-Grind the pepper.
-Yes, Chef.

1641
01:02:08,625 --> 01:02:10,026
THE MAN WHO SHOWED
PERFECT TEAMWORK WITH HIM

1642
01:02:10,360 --> 01:02:11,962
CHEF WOO DO-HEE

1643
01:02:12,028 --> 01:02:13,830
Woo Do-hee. The word <i>do</i> also means knife,

1644
01:02:13,897 --> 01:02:15,432
so I brought my best knife.

1645
01:02:15,498 --> 01:02:16,900
SECRET WEAPON: WOO KNIFE-HEE

1646
01:02:16,967 --> 01:02:18,969
The kitchen toolman.

1647
01:02:19,035 --> 01:02:22,405
-He brought his kitchen toolman?
-Oh, come on.

1648
01:02:22,472 --> 01:02:25,609
I knew it was odd when he made
an effort to carry a huge bag.

1649
01:02:25,675 --> 01:02:27,143
-Like he's moving.
-He didn't tell us.

1650
01:02:27,444 --> 01:02:29,779
THE SECRET WEAPON FROM HIS BAG(?)

1651
01:02:29,846 --> 01:02:31,348
Was he hiding in the bag?

1652
01:02:31,414 --> 01:02:33,216
I checked the rules first.

1653
01:02:33,283 --> 01:02:35,385
I asked if it had to be an object,
and the staff said no.

1654
01:02:35,452 --> 01:02:37,921
I see. That's true. We didn't set a limit.

1655
01:02:37,988 --> 01:02:40,123
Dang it. I should've brought my mom.

1656
01:02:40,190 --> 01:02:41,891
My mom is a great cook.

1657
01:02:41,958 --> 01:02:43,693
-I'm bringing my mom next week.
-Unbelievable.

1658
01:02:43,760 --> 01:02:46,062
-We didn't expect you to bring a person.
-A person?

1659
01:02:46,129 --> 01:02:48,965
So you're using him as your knife?

1660
01:02:49,032 --> 01:02:52,369
-He's here only to use his knife.
-Only the knife?

1661
01:02:52,435 --> 01:02:55,071
-But I won't touch one.
-I see.

1662
01:02:55,138 --> 01:02:57,907
Chef Do-hee, does <i>do</i> in your name
really mean knife?

1663
01:02:58,475 --> 01:02:59,743
As of today, yes.

1664
01:02:59,809 --> 01:03:01,077
CHANGING NAMES AS OF TODAY

1665
01:03:01,144 --> 01:03:03,113
DO-HEE CAME AS SHARP AS A KNIFE

1666
01:03:03,179 --> 01:03:05,448
These two are hilarious.

1667
01:03:05,515 --> 01:03:07,550
Jong-won isn't looking to lose today.

1668
01:03:07,617 --> 01:03:10,253
<i>Do</i> in his name stands for a knife,
a very sharp knife.

1669
01:03:10,320 --> 01:03:11,821
Chef Hyun-suk looks a bit tense.

1670
01:03:11,888 --> 01:03:15,258
Actually, I don't really know
how the gas is supposed to mix,

1671
01:03:15,325 --> 01:03:17,560
but Chef Seon-yeop is good at that.

1672
01:03:18,194 --> 01:03:19,929
Is he here? Tell him to come in.

1673
01:03:19,996 --> 01:03:22,332
-Should I call him?
-Call him now.

1674
01:03:22,399 --> 01:03:24,634
Tell him to come if he can make it.

1675
01:03:24,701 --> 01:03:26,569
I'll let him chop for Hyun-suk too.

1676
01:03:26,636 --> 01:03:28,538
Chef Hyun-suk, do you want him?

1677
01:03:28,605 --> 01:03:29,873
In that case,

1678
01:03:29,939 --> 01:03:32,776
I'll make sure Chef Jong-won also uses
liquid nitrogen and nitrogen gas.

1679
01:03:33,109 --> 01:03:34,611
TRUE FAIR PLAY

1680
01:03:34,678 --> 01:03:36,913
-Why would he use that?
-"Jong-won, use nitrogen too."

1681
01:03:36,980 --> 01:03:39,082
Freeze someone's mouth or whatever else.

1682
01:03:39,816 --> 01:03:41,251
LIKE THIS?

1683
01:03:42,185 --> 01:03:44,688
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
BLUE CRAB, GIZZARD SHAD, RADISH

1684
01:03:44,754 --> 01:03:47,257
NAPA CABBAGE, ENOKI, SHIITAKE,
BEAN SPROUT, CHIVE, PERILLA LEAF, AND MORE

1685
01:03:47,324 --> 01:03:50,193
And Chef Jong-won,
what will you be making?

1686
01:03:50,260 --> 01:03:51,428
It's titled,

1687
01:03:51,494 --> 01:03:53,596
Make Gizzard Shad for His Majesty!

1688
01:03:53,663 --> 01:03:57,467
-Make Gizzard Shad for His Majesty!
-Make Gizzard Shad for His Majesty!

1689
01:03:57,534 --> 01:04:00,070
Sang-uk just cracked up big time.

1690
01:04:00,136 --> 01:04:03,907
Sang-uk is the emperor of fencing,

1691
01:04:03,973 --> 01:04:05,875
so I want to make royal cuisine

1692
01:04:05,942 --> 01:04:10,046
using seasonal ingredients for fall,
blue crabs and gizzard shads.

1693
01:04:10,113 --> 01:04:11,781
I'll prepare gizzard shads in two ways.

1694
01:04:11,848 --> 01:04:15,285
First, as expected, I'll grill them.

1695
01:04:15,351 --> 01:04:16,953
Second, I'll season them raw.

1696
01:04:17,020 --> 01:04:18,421
That sounds so good.

1697
01:04:18,488 --> 01:04:19,589
I'm also making <i>gegamjeong,</i>

1698
01:04:19,656 --> 01:04:21,691
a dish found in the records
of the Royal Procession to Hwaseong

1699
01:04:21,758 --> 01:04:25,995
in the year 1795,
left behind in the reign of King Jeongjo,

1700
01:04:26,062 --> 01:04:28,364
a historical dish
enjoyed by our ancestors.

1701
01:04:28,431 --> 01:04:31,801
<i>Gamjeong</i> is a word used
for <i>jjigae</i> made with gochujang.

1702
01:04:31,868 --> 01:04:34,237
-So I'll make crab <i>gamjeong, gegamjeong.</i>
<i>-Gegamjeong.</i>

1703
01:04:34,304 --> 01:04:36,373
-A royal court cuisine.
-Like gochujang <i>jjigae.</i>

1704
01:04:36,439 --> 01:04:38,675
It was a dish for King Jeongjo's meals.

1705
01:04:38,742 --> 01:04:40,543
Make Gizzard Shad for His Majesty!

1706
01:04:41,111 --> 01:04:42,679
-All right.
-That sounds delicious.

1707
01:04:42,746 --> 01:04:44,948
You didn't say that
after Chef Hyun-suk's presentation.

1708
01:04:45,014 --> 01:04:46,783
He only talked about nitrogen.

1709
01:04:46,850 --> 01:04:49,619
All I remember
is nitrogen and molecular something.

1710
01:04:49,686 --> 01:04:51,388
-Isn't it difficult?
-It's hard to understand.

1711
01:04:51,454 --> 01:04:53,390
But he'll explain it while cooking.

1712
01:04:53,456 --> 01:04:55,125
-I see.
-Yes.

1713
01:04:55,191 --> 01:04:57,861
HOW WILL THEIR DISHES TURN OUT
WITH THEIR SECRET WEAPONS?

1714
01:04:57,927 --> 01:05:01,064
Let us begin their cook-off.

1715
01:05:02,065 --> 01:05:04,801
Wait, we're shedding light
on nitrogen too?

1716
01:05:05,735 --> 01:05:08,304
-It's like a person.
-It's so big.

1717
01:05:08,371 --> 01:05:11,174
-It looks dramatic.
-The light is on both Do-hee and nitrogen.

1718
01:05:11,408 --> 01:05:12,976
NO PREJUDICE IN THIS STUDIO

1719
01:05:13,877 --> 01:05:15,578
It looks like a tin can robot.

1720
01:05:16,212 --> 01:05:17,981
We have to show his secret weapon.

1721
01:05:18,047 --> 01:05:19,983
I cannitrogen lose today.

1722
01:05:20,049 --> 01:05:21,918
-Cannitrogen lose.
-Cannitrogen lose.

1723
01:05:22,952 --> 01:05:24,821
"I got Woo Do-hee!"

1724
01:05:24,888 --> 01:05:27,924
Here with his super teammate,
Chef Son Jong-won.

1725
01:05:27,991 --> 01:05:30,360
"I cannitrogen lose if I have nitrogen!"

1726
01:05:30,427 --> 01:05:33,429
The best of molecular gastronomy,
Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

1727
01:05:33,496 --> 01:05:36,733
Which one of them will
use their secret weapon and win?

1728
01:05:36,800 --> 01:05:40,103
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1729
01:05:41,638 --> 01:05:42,839
-Please cut that first.
-Yes, Chef.

1730
01:05:42,906 --> 01:05:44,440
I'm looking forward to this.

1731
01:05:44,507 --> 01:05:47,076
Now, Chef Do-hee
is only allowed to use his knife.

1732
01:05:47,310 --> 01:05:49,078
SAME SIZE

1733
01:05:49,946 --> 01:05:50,880
CHIC

1734
01:05:51,147 --> 01:05:53,783
1. CHOP RADISH AND CABBAGE
WITH DO-HEE THE KNIFE

1735
01:05:53,850 --> 01:05:55,518
He's not touching the nitrogen yet.

1736
01:05:55,585 --> 01:05:57,954
Why are you peeling the eggplant halfway?

1737
01:05:58,021 --> 01:06:00,623
It's to make the dish pretty.

1738
01:06:00,690 --> 01:06:02,158
For the presentation.

1739
01:06:02,225 --> 01:06:03,426
It needs color to look prettier.

1740
01:06:04,327 --> 01:06:07,130
I can't give up on the shape.

1741
01:06:07,197 --> 01:06:08,465
He leaves some purple on it.

1742
01:06:10,066 --> 01:06:11,100
JONG-WON BEGINS MAKING GEGAMJEONG

1743
01:06:11,167 --> 01:06:12,535
DOENJANG, GOCHUJANG

1744
01:06:13,269 --> 01:06:15,271
Jong-won, are you making the soup first?

1745
01:06:15,338 --> 01:06:17,440
Yes, I'm making <i>gamjeong,</i>
the gochujang <i>jjigae.</i>

1746
01:06:17,507 --> 01:06:18,675
<i>Gegamjeong.</i>

1747
01:06:18,741 --> 01:06:20,743
<i>-Gegamjeong.</i>
-He'll probably add crab to that.

1748
01:06:20,810 --> 01:06:22,579
I've had spicy crab soup,

1749
01:06:22,645 --> 01:06:24,547
but never crab gochujang<i> jjigae.</i>

1750
01:06:24,614 --> 01:06:25,882
-Right.
-What's that like?

1751
01:06:25,949 --> 01:06:28,284
-We've all had crab soup.
-Yes, crab soup.

1752
01:06:28,351 --> 01:06:30,587
-He's making stone pot rice too.
-And rice?

1753
01:06:30,653 --> 01:06:33,990
I'm making stone pot rice
with seasonal mushrooms.

1754
01:06:34,057 --> 01:06:36,226
I'm using scallops, which are in season.

1755
01:06:36,292 --> 01:06:39,162
Only the soft portion.

1756
01:06:39,229 --> 01:06:40,630
It's much softer.

1757
01:06:40,697 --> 01:06:42,398
Isn't it easy to add flavors?

1758
01:06:44,334 --> 01:06:45,535
Easy, isn't it?

1759
01:06:45,602 --> 01:06:46,703
DO-HEE THE KNIFE PREPS MUSHROOMS

1760
01:06:46,769 --> 01:06:48,671
Chef Do-hee only chops things.

1761
01:06:48,738 --> 01:06:51,007
If we catch him doing anything else,
it's cheating.

1762
01:06:52,408 --> 01:06:55,011
They have a similar image
when their heads are bowed.

1763
01:06:55,078 --> 01:06:57,247
-Yes, you're right.
-You're right.

1764
01:06:57,313 --> 01:06:58,648
It's like he has cloned himself.

1765
01:07:00,483 --> 01:07:02,051
-Okay.
-Whoa.

1766
01:07:02,118 --> 01:07:03,820
A PROFESSIONAL MUST CATCH ATTENTION!

1767
01:07:03,887 --> 01:07:05,622
-What was that?
-Okay.

1768
01:07:05,688 --> 01:07:09,993
-He's throwing salt differently.
-Was that fencing?

1769
01:07:10,059 --> 01:07:11,494
That was a crab.

1770
01:07:11,561 --> 01:07:12,629
-"That was crap"?
-Like fencing.

1771
01:07:12,695 --> 01:07:15,231
No, like he's fencing!

1772
01:07:15,298 --> 01:07:17,867
It was a fencing move. He did a stab.

1773
01:07:17,934 --> 01:07:19,402
STAB! AND MISS

1774
01:07:19,469 --> 01:07:20,770
"That was crap"?

1775
01:07:20,837 --> 01:07:23,840
-No, a crab.
-A crab?

1776
01:07:23,907 --> 01:07:26,342
I thought it was a crab,
but he was fencing.

1777
01:07:26,709 --> 01:07:28,511
HERE'S THE REAL CRAB

1778
01:07:29,345 --> 01:07:30,914
He's prepping the crab.

1779
01:07:30,980 --> 01:07:33,816
It's not easy to get the meat out.

1780
01:07:33,883 --> 01:07:36,986
-Right.
-That crab looks too fresh.

1781
01:07:37,053 --> 01:07:40,089
-It's hard to get out when it's raw.
-I've tried, and I couldn't do it.

1782
01:07:40,156 --> 01:07:40,990
FOUND IT

1783
01:07:41,057 --> 01:07:43,593
But Do-hee isn't allowed to help.

1784
01:07:43,660 --> 01:07:45,128
<i>Gegamjeong.</i>

1785
01:07:45,194 --> 01:07:46,563
<i>Gegamjeong.</i>

1786
01:07:46,629 --> 01:07:48,164
That <i>jjigae</i> looks delicious.

1787
01:07:48,231 --> 01:07:51,434
The vegetables Chef Do-hee prepped
all went in.

1788
01:07:52,135 --> 01:07:54,837
BLANCH BEAN SPROUTS IN BOILING WATER

1789
01:07:56,572 --> 01:07:57,707
HYUN-SUK USES CUTLASSFISH

1790
01:07:57,774 --> 01:07:58,608
Cutlassfish.

1791
01:07:58,675 --> 01:08:01,344
-That looks so meaty.
-It's as wide as five fingers.

1792
01:08:01,411 --> 01:08:02,879
-Five?
-Five fingers.

1793
01:08:03,546 --> 01:08:04,847
So it's this big?

1794
01:08:04,914 --> 01:08:06,015
It's this big.

1795
01:08:06,082 --> 01:08:09,185
-"Five fingers" mean it's this big.
-Yes.

1796
01:08:09,252 --> 01:08:12,722
Sang-uk deserves cutlassfish
as big as that one.

1797
01:08:12,789 --> 01:08:14,424
That's so neat.

1798
01:08:14,490 --> 01:08:15,325
He shows it to us.

1799
01:08:15,692 --> 01:08:16,526
EXPECTATIONS GOING UP

1800
01:08:16,592 --> 01:08:18,161
Sang-uk's a big eater.

1801
01:08:19,595 --> 01:08:21,397
I think he's using both sides.

1802
01:08:21,464 --> 01:08:23,466
Why didn't you borrow Chef Do-hee?

1803
01:08:23,533 --> 01:08:24,834
He'd do that well.

1804
01:08:24,901 --> 01:08:27,971
-Gizzard shad.
-He's prepping gizzard shads.

1805
01:08:28,037 --> 01:08:29,238
It's not easy to prep.

1806
01:08:29,305 --> 01:08:31,074
Jong-won's knife, Woo Do-hee.

1807
01:08:31,140 --> 01:08:33,609
He's nipping off the stomach.

1808
01:08:33,676 --> 01:08:35,111
"Nipping off"?

1809
01:08:35,178 --> 01:08:36,746
Say that he's removing the innards.

1810
01:08:36,813 --> 01:08:37,747
-Is that so?
-Yes.

1811
01:08:37,814 --> 01:08:39,315
That's a crab.

1812
01:08:39,382 --> 01:08:41,851
"Nipping off" sounds too violent.

1813
01:08:41,918 --> 01:08:44,554
Okay. He's nipping off the innards.

1814
01:08:44,621 --> 01:08:46,589
Stop it. Nipping off what?

1815
01:08:46,656 --> 01:08:48,625
The only thing he nipped
was the gold medal.

1816
01:08:48,958 --> 01:08:50,560
NICE LINE

1817
01:08:50,626 --> 01:08:53,463
Many sushi places
are reluctant to work with gizzard shad.

1818
01:08:53,529 --> 01:08:56,165
-It has lots of bones.
-That too, and it's hard to prep.

1819
01:08:56,232 --> 01:08:57,133
BOOM!

1820
01:08:57,734 --> 01:09:01,404
HE WALKS HIS OWN PROFESSIONAL PATH
EVEN WHEN NO ONE'S WATCHING

1821
01:09:01,471 --> 01:09:05,308
We were all focused on Do-hee.

1822
01:09:05,375 --> 01:09:06,643
Try to get the salt in.

1823
01:09:06,709 --> 01:09:09,278
The management told us
that the building has been eroding.

1824
01:09:09,345 --> 01:09:12,315
-The management office?
-Hyun-suk tries hard too,

1825
01:09:12,382 --> 01:09:14,517
showing his salt sprinkling move.

1826
01:09:14,584 --> 01:09:15,718
Scallops.

1827
01:09:15,785 --> 01:09:18,087
-Scallops, right?
-Scallops.

1828
01:09:18,721 --> 01:09:20,023
I WON'T LET YOU WIN!

1829
01:09:20,857 --> 01:09:21,991
-Can you chop this?
-Yes, Chef.

1830
01:09:22,058 --> 01:09:23,459
REQUESTING CHOPPING

1831
01:09:24,894 --> 01:09:28,297
Jong-won keeps giving him
just enough work to do.

1832
01:09:28,364 --> 01:09:30,099
He never stops moving.

1833
01:09:30,166 --> 01:09:32,835
Yes, he has a lot to chop.

1834
01:09:32,902 --> 01:09:35,071
PERILLA LEAVES

1835
01:09:36,339 --> 01:09:38,775
JONG-WON IS FREED FROM KNIFE DUTY

1836
01:09:39,675 --> 01:09:40,610
TOFU

1837
01:09:41,277 --> 01:09:42,812
CRAB INNARDS, MINCED BEEF

1838
01:09:43,312 --> 01:09:44,180
BLANCHED BEAN SPROUTS

1839
01:09:44,847 --> 01:09:45,782
MINCED GARLIC

1840
01:09:45,848 --> 01:09:46,683
SALT

1841
01:09:46,749 --> 01:09:47,884
5. MIX EVERYTHING

1842
01:09:47,950 --> 01:09:50,353
-He's filling the crab shell.
-Inside the shell?

1843
01:09:50,420 --> 01:09:54,924
It's crab meat, tofu,
beef, and bean sprouts.

1844
01:09:54,991 --> 01:09:56,492
CRAB SHELL JEON

1845
01:09:56,559 --> 01:09:58,161
He's using the crab shell.

1846
01:09:58,227 --> 01:10:00,563
This is molecular gastronomy
from the Joseon era.

1847
01:10:00,630 --> 01:10:02,131
3. STEAM CUTLASSFISH

1848
01:10:02,198 --> 01:10:07,003
Hyun-suk ruins Jong-won's lines.

1849
01:10:07,070 --> 01:10:09,372
Chef Do-hee
is washing something with water.

1850
01:10:09,439 --> 01:10:10,373
SPOTTED CHEATING

1851
01:10:11,441 --> 01:10:12,575
He can't do that.

1852
01:10:12,642 --> 01:10:14,444
You're only there as a knife!

1853
01:10:14,510 --> 01:10:16,012
-He can't wash those!
-Wait!

1854
01:10:16,212 --> 01:10:17,213
LAUGHING

1855
01:10:17,280 --> 01:10:18,681
That's not using a knife!

1856
01:10:18,748 --> 01:10:19,882
-That's not chopping!
-It's washing.

1857
01:10:19,949 --> 01:10:21,584
-He's washing!
-He's washing instead!

1858
01:10:21,851 --> 01:10:23,219
WASHING?

1859
01:10:23,553 --> 01:10:24,554
SMILING

1860
01:10:24,821 --> 01:10:26,389
SPOTTED WASHING

1861
01:10:26,456 --> 01:10:28,091
-He's washing.
-He's washing.

1862
01:10:28,157 --> 01:10:29,592
-He's washing.
-That's washing.

1863
01:10:30,760 --> 01:10:31,694
Do-hee's washing!

1864
01:10:31,761 --> 01:10:35,865
-Do-hee the Water.
-Do-hee the Water.

1865
01:10:35,932 --> 01:10:39,035
That's a warning, Do-hee the Water.

1866
01:10:39,102 --> 01:10:40,636
You're here as a knife.

1867
01:10:40,703 --> 01:10:43,272
-And next. Right.
-Egg wash.

1868
01:10:43,339 --> 01:10:45,074
Gosh, that looks so good.

1869
01:10:45,141 --> 01:10:47,143
What will you do with that crab? Grill it?

1870
01:10:47,210 --> 01:10:50,480
I'll make <i>jeon,</i>
and then throw it into the soup.

1871
01:10:50,546 --> 01:10:51,881
7. GRIDDLE COATED CRAB SHELL JEON

1872
01:10:52,482 --> 01:10:53,950
HYUN-SUK GETS THE BLENDER READY

1873
01:10:54,350 --> 01:10:55,852
PINE NUTS

1874
01:10:56,119 --> 01:10:58,020
WATER, FRESH CREAM

1875
01:10:58,454 --> 01:11:00,590
-What did you blend?
-Pine nuts.

1876
01:11:00,656 --> 01:11:02,458
-Pine nuts?
-There were pine nuts.

1877
01:11:02,525 --> 01:11:03,793
AND AGAIN, IT BEGINS…

1878
01:11:04,327 --> 01:11:06,662
THE LONELY PATH OF THE PROFESSIONAL

1879
01:11:07,029 --> 01:11:08,931
THE LONELY EXPERT CHOI

1880
01:11:08,998 --> 01:11:10,733
No one pays him attention.

1881
01:11:11,167 --> 01:11:14,036
5. STRAIN BOILED PINE NUT CREAM

1882
01:11:14,103 --> 01:11:16,706
Usually, everyone watches Hyun-suk
when he does something,

1883
01:11:16,773 --> 01:11:19,375
but Jong-won is doing a great job
at stealing the attention today.

1884
01:11:19,442 --> 01:11:21,244
Stone pot rice with mushrooms.

1885
01:11:21,310 --> 01:11:22,912
That looks so good.

1886
01:11:22,979 --> 01:11:24,413
I added bracken too.

1887
01:11:24,480 --> 01:11:25,782
-Even bracken?
-That'd be delicious.

1888
01:11:25,848 --> 01:11:27,683
He's making gizzard shad fillets.

1889
01:11:27,750 --> 01:11:30,553
-It's so hard to turn them into fillets.
-Is it?

1890
01:11:30,620 --> 01:11:32,822
Do-hee makes thin gizzard shad pieces.

1891
01:11:32,889 --> 01:11:34,557
-That looks so good.
-Is there enough time?

1892
01:11:35,091 --> 01:11:38,394
He added cooking wine, soy sauce, sugar,

1893
01:11:38,461 --> 01:11:41,831
kelp, and squeezed ginger juice into it.

1894
01:11:41,898 --> 01:11:42,865
That's<i> tsuyu.</i>

1895
01:11:42,932 --> 01:11:44,500
Japanese seasoned soy sauce?

1896
01:11:44,567 --> 01:11:46,435
It's been a bit past half.

1897
01:11:46,502 --> 01:11:48,337
I have two here, Chef.

1898
01:11:48,404 --> 01:11:49,906
He's cooking them whole?

1899
01:11:49,972 --> 01:11:51,107
Will those get cooked?

1900
01:11:51,174 --> 01:11:52,408
ONE WITH NO CUTS, OUT

1901
01:11:52,475 --> 01:11:53,609
I don't think it'll cook thoroughly.

1902
01:11:54,076 --> 01:11:55,978
BURNING!

1903
01:11:56,279 --> 01:11:57,547
He's dropping it in there.

1904
01:11:57,613 --> 01:11:59,081
He added the shell.

1905
01:11:59,148 --> 01:12:00,383
He grilled it first, and then added it.

1906
01:12:00,983 --> 01:12:01,951
That's awesome.

1907
01:12:02,852 --> 01:12:03,820
FAVORITE VEGGIE: ASPARAGUS

1908
01:12:03,886 --> 01:12:05,188
Asparagus looks great.

1909
01:12:06,155 --> 01:12:07,223
ASPARAGUS, TSUYU

1910
01:12:07,290 --> 01:12:08,558
PINE NUT CREAM

1911
01:12:08,858 --> 01:12:11,627
7. BOIL ASPARAGUS BRIEFLY

1912
01:12:11,694 --> 01:12:12,528
COOLING

1913
01:12:13,062 --> 01:12:15,765
GRILLED SCALLOPS AND EGGPLANTS ARE DONE

1914
01:12:16,566 --> 01:12:18,334
AND FINALLY…

1915
01:12:19,001 --> 01:12:20,303
SECRET WEAPON: NITROGEN SET

1916
01:12:20,369 --> 01:12:21,437
There it is.

1917
01:12:21,504 --> 01:12:24,640
-There are two different espumas.
-He grabs the nitrogen!

1918
01:12:24,707 --> 01:12:29,045
This one is a flask
that can contain hot liquid.

1919
01:12:29,111 --> 01:12:31,047
Espuma.

1920
01:12:31,113 --> 01:12:33,082
-Is it pine nut espuma?
-This red one

1921
01:12:33,149 --> 01:12:35,284
can stand up to 60°C.

1922
01:12:36,452 --> 01:12:38,187
Molecular gastronomy is easy, isn't it?

1923
01:12:39,355 --> 01:12:40,523
It's so easy.

1924
01:12:41,357 --> 01:12:43,526
I guess he's ready to use nitrogen soon.

1925
01:12:43,593 --> 01:12:46,696
There are about six minutes left.

1926
01:12:46,896 --> 01:12:48,764
PEAR, WHITE KIMCHI

1927
01:12:49,432 --> 01:12:51,667
-Do we have a pear?
-I'm on it, Chef.

1928
01:12:51,734 --> 01:12:55,471
-He's grilling gizzard shads.
-It takes a while to soften the bones.

1929
01:12:55,538 --> 01:12:57,840
They look golden brown.

1930
01:12:57,907 --> 01:13:01,344
Most people cook that over a gridiron.

1931
01:13:01,410 --> 01:13:02,812
Yes, over charcoal.

1932
01:13:02,879 --> 01:13:04,113
Is it okay to cook it in a pan?

1933
01:13:04,180 --> 01:13:08,351
If you cook it without oil,
the fish will give out its fat,

1934
01:13:08,417 --> 01:13:10,753
and absorb it back,
making it even more delicious.

1935
01:13:10,820 --> 01:13:12,288
So you're using its fat.

1936
01:13:12,355 --> 01:13:13,356
Huh?

1937
01:13:13,923 --> 01:13:16,692
-Chef Do-hee!
-Yes?

1938
01:13:16,759 --> 01:13:18,694
He's stuffing something with kimchi!

1939
01:13:18,761 --> 01:13:20,529
-That's not cutting!
-It's not cutting!

1940
01:13:20,596 --> 01:13:22,064
-It's not cutting!
-It's stuffing.

1941
01:13:22,131 --> 01:13:24,433
-Stuffing.
-He just picked up the knife.

1942
01:13:24,500 --> 01:13:28,004
He keeps dropping the knife
whenever we look away for a second.

1943
01:13:28,070 --> 01:13:30,973
He can't do anything other than cutting.

1944
01:13:31,040 --> 01:13:33,743
Knife techniques are endless,
so if you think it's cutting, it is.

1945
01:13:34,744 --> 01:13:35,578
What now?

1946
01:13:35,645 --> 01:13:37,079
Sang-uk probably agrees.

1947
01:13:37,380 --> 01:13:38,614
HEART OF SWORDSMANSHIP

1948
01:13:38,681 --> 01:13:42,318
-What are you saying?
-Jong-won has become unreasonable.

1949
01:13:42,385 --> 01:13:44,921
If you think something is cutting, it is.

1950
01:13:45,955 --> 01:13:47,623
Strawberry jam, lemon juice.

1951
01:13:48,858 --> 01:13:50,526
-Strawberry jam?
-Strawberry jam?

1952
01:13:50,593 --> 01:13:51,494
What?

1953
01:13:52,161 --> 01:13:54,664
-What's he going to do?
-Strawberry jam and lemon juice?

1954
01:13:54,730 --> 01:13:55,631
Huh?

1955
01:13:55,698 --> 01:13:56,766
Strawberry jam and lemon juice.

1956
01:13:56,832 --> 01:13:59,969
He's straining the strawberry jam
and lemon juice mix.

1957
01:14:00,036 --> 01:14:01,304
What's that white thing?

1958
01:14:01,370 --> 01:14:03,506
-Fresh cream.
-Cream?

1959
01:14:03,572 --> 01:14:06,042
This time, I'll make cold espuma

1960
01:14:06,108 --> 01:14:07,977
with this cold siphon gun.

1961
01:14:08,044 --> 01:14:11,013
-I'll pour this inside.
-So that'll turn into foam.

1962
01:14:11,080 --> 01:14:12,348
Strawberry jam and lemon juice.

1963
01:14:12,415 --> 01:14:13,416
Two types.

1964
01:14:13,849 --> 01:14:15,551
WOO DO-HEE IS BACK ON THE KNIFE

1965
01:14:15,785 --> 01:14:18,521
SLICING FISH FOR THE COLD SALAD

1966
01:14:18,854 --> 01:14:21,791
11. SLICE GIZZARD SHAD FILLET

1967
01:14:21,857 --> 01:14:23,092
I'll step out, Chef.

1968
01:14:23,159 --> 01:14:24,493
-Are you done?
-Yes, Chef.

1969
01:14:24,560 --> 01:14:25,761
Do you need to chop anything?

1970
01:14:25,828 --> 01:14:28,331
-I don't.
-Jung-hwan, five minutes.

1971
01:14:28,397 --> 01:14:30,566
Please get over there and check.

1972
01:14:30,633 --> 01:14:31,701
CLEANING UP HIS KNIFE

1973
01:14:32,435 --> 01:14:33,903
I think Do-hee's done.

1974
01:14:33,970 --> 01:14:36,038
-He is.
-He's sitting down.

1975
01:14:36,105 --> 01:14:37,873
-Have a seat.
-Do-hee's gone,

1976
01:14:37,940 --> 01:14:40,009
so Jong-won can't use a knife.

1977
01:14:40,176 --> 01:14:41,077
REMINISCING

1978
01:14:41,143 --> 01:14:43,212
-He's using gizzard shad.
-I want to use him too.

1979
01:14:43,279 --> 01:14:44,480
I WANT WOO DO-HEE THE KNIFE TOO

1980
01:14:44,547 --> 01:14:46,482
Are you free for the next episode?

1981
01:14:46,816 --> 01:14:48,851
POONG FLIRTS

1982
01:14:48,918 --> 01:14:50,820
If Poong gets his help…

1983
01:14:50,886 --> 01:14:53,956
I can probably do anything.

1984
01:14:54,023 --> 01:14:56,525
PERILLA POWDER

1985
01:14:56,792 --> 01:15:00,629
12. MIX PERILLA POWDER AND OIL, SOY SAUCE,
CHILI POWDER, FISH, PERILLA LEAVES, CHIVES

1986
01:15:00,930 --> 01:15:02,131
CHIVES, PERILLA LEAVES

1987
01:15:02,398 --> 01:15:05,301
FALL SEASONED GIZZARD SHAD IS DONE

1988
01:15:07,103 --> 01:15:09,138
THE FIVE-FINGER CUTLASSFISH GOT STEAMED

1989
01:15:10,206 --> 01:15:11,474
PLUMP

1990
01:15:11,540 --> 01:15:14,210
-He's using nitrogen!
-He's grabbing it.

1991
01:15:14,276 --> 01:15:15,478
He's adding nitrogen.

1992
01:15:15,544 --> 01:15:17,146
We get to see this?

1993
01:15:17,213 --> 01:15:18,314
Wow. Nitrogen.

1994
01:15:18,381 --> 01:15:20,282
It's the first time on the show.

1995
01:15:20,349 --> 01:15:22,918
He's filling it up with nitrogen.

1996
01:15:22,985 --> 01:15:24,453
Add nitrogen.

1997
01:15:24,520 --> 01:15:26,022
What happens?

1998
01:15:26,088 --> 01:15:28,391
The cream turns into foam.

1999
01:15:28,457 --> 01:15:31,060
-That flask is often used at coffee shops.
-Coffee shops.

2000
01:15:31,127 --> 01:15:32,862
Now, shake it.

2001
01:15:32,928 --> 01:15:34,163
Let's see it.

2002
01:15:34,630 --> 01:15:36,365
SO?

2003
01:15:38,334 --> 01:15:39,568
What's with the gas?

2004
01:15:39,635 --> 01:15:42,338
-It's not working!
-No!

2005
01:15:42,405 --> 01:15:45,107
-There's no gas!
-No!

2006
01:15:45,174 --> 01:15:47,576
What are you doing? You're a professional!

2007
01:15:47,643 --> 01:15:49,612
-He's adding more now.
-Don't be an amateur.

2008
01:15:49,678 --> 01:15:51,480
-Why isn't it working?
-What's going on?

2009
01:15:51,547 --> 01:15:54,116
-It may not have enough cream inside.
-Really?

2010
01:15:54,183 --> 01:15:56,218
-It doesn't work in that case?
-It doesn't.

2011
01:15:56,285 --> 01:15:58,554
-Will the nitrogen shoot out?
-Come on out!

2012
01:15:58,621 --> 01:15:59,889
-Nitrogen.
-Will it come out?

2013
01:15:59,955 --> 01:16:02,658
-What's with this?
-It's not! What's going on?

2014
01:16:03,459 --> 01:16:04,927
There's no gas in there.

2015
01:16:04,994 --> 01:16:07,063
-There's no gas?
-That might be the case.

2016
01:16:07,129 --> 01:16:08,431
Can Do-hee help?

2017
01:16:08,497 --> 01:16:10,433
-Go help him.
-Do-hee, help him.

2018
01:16:10,499 --> 01:16:12,802
-Can you do this, Do-hee?
-Wait.

2019
01:16:12,868 --> 01:16:14,770
-He's good at it.
-Do-hee's back in.

2020
01:16:14,837 --> 01:16:17,106
Jung-hwan,
what's Jong-won's <i>gegamjeong </i>like?

2021
01:16:17,173 --> 01:16:19,241
<i>-Gegamjeong.</i>
-The soup.

2022
01:16:19,308 --> 01:16:21,777
<i>Gegamjeong.</i> I'll try this.

2023
01:16:21,844 --> 01:16:24,680
-King Jeongjo's soup.
-King Jeongjo.

2024
01:16:24,747 --> 01:16:29,118
Is it good enough for His Majesty to come?

2025
01:16:29,518 --> 01:16:30,920
THE FIRST SIP OF ROYAL COURT CUISINE!

2026
01:16:30,986 --> 01:16:32,621
…for His Majesty to come?

2027
01:16:32,688 --> 01:16:34,623
His Majesty has come!

2028
01:16:36,492 --> 01:16:37,426
AH-HA

2029
01:16:37,493 --> 01:16:40,162
-It sounds good.
-It's seasoned perfectly.

2030
01:16:40,229 --> 01:16:41,263
Is it?

2031
01:16:41,330 --> 01:16:42,565
ROYAL COURT CUISINE, GEGAMJEONG

2032
01:16:42,765 --> 01:16:44,133
AND ON THE SIDE,

2033
01:16:44,500 --> 01:16:46,402
SWEET AND SOUR PEAR AND WHITE KIMCHI

2034
01:16:47,636 --> 01:16:50,272
13. TOP IT WITH FROZEN WHITE KIMCHI JUICE

2035
01:16:50,339 --> 01:16:51,273
There's no gas?

2036
01:16:51,340 --> 01:16:52,641
-This looks bad!
-No.

2037
01:16:52,708 --> 01:16:54,210
-There's no gas.
-Has it been too long

2038
01:16:54,276 --> 01:16:55,744
since he has used those items?

2039
01:16:55,811 --> 01:16:57,413
He should've tested it first.

2040
01:16:57,480 --> 01:16:59,949
-This is bad.
-His juniors mostly use those now.

2041
01:17:00,015 --> 01:17:02,351
Chef Do-hee continues to check.

2042
01:17:03,385 --> 01:17:04,753
TORCH OF ANGER

2043
01:17:04,820 --> 01:17:07,756
Chef Hyun-suk torches the fish.

2044
01:17:09,592 --> 01:17:11,460
Will it work this time?

2045
01:17:11,527 --> 01:17:14,263
-No!
-What is that?

2046
01:17:14,330 --> 01:17:15,998
What now?

2047
01:17:16,065 --> 01:17:17,566
-Hyun-suk.
-It won't work.

2048
01:17:17,633 --> 01:17:20,436
-How can he make molecular gastronomy?
-Oh, no.

2049
01:17:20,503 --> 01:17:22,905
Will he serve just the scallops?

2050
01:17:22,972 --> 01:17:24,573
I can't believe this.

2051
01:17:24,640 --> 01:17:26,442
Do-hee shakes his head too.

2052
01:17:26,509 --> 01:17:28,477
-Is it not working?
-There's no gas.

2053
01:17:28,544 --> 01:17:31,280
-There's no gas!
-Oh, no!

2054
01:17:31,347 --> 01:17:33,082
-Hyun-suk!
-He leaves.

2055
01:17:33,149 --> 01:17:35,684
-It's such a shame.
-Just whip it by hand.

2056
01:17:35,751 --> 01:17:37,052
12. PLATE SCALLOPS, EGGPLANT, WHITE KIMCHI

2057
01:17:37,119 --> 01:17:38,954
This means someone let the gas out.

2058
01:17:39,021 --> 01:17:39,989
-What?
-What?

2059
01:17:40,055 --> 01:17:41,457
-We'll investigate later.
-Wait.

2060
01:17:41,524 --> 01:17:42,625
Was it Sam Kim?

2061
01:17:42,691 --> 01:17:44,426
-Wait, it's not me.
-It might be Sam!

2062
01:17:44,493 --> 01:17:46,562
-Did he leave it open?
-Hyun-suk.

2063
01:17:46,629 --> 01:17:47,830
Excuse me.

2064
01:17:48,397 --> 01:17:50,366
If all needles point

2065
01:17:50,432 --> 01:17:52,535
to something red on the gauge,

2066
01:17:52,601 --> 01:17:54,637
-that means it's empty.
-It's empty.

2067
01:17:54,870 --> 01:17:56,305
INTERNATIONAL STANDARD

2068
01:17:56,372 --> 01:17:58,774
But he must've known that.
He seems quite flustered.

2069
01:17:58,841 --> 01:18:00,843
-Even now, he…
-The last attempt!

2070
01:18:00,910 --> 01:18:03,946
-It's his last attempt! It has to work!
-Last time!

2071
01:18:04,013 --> 01:18:06,282
-No!
-No! It's still liquid!

2072
01:18:06,615 --> 01:18:09,852
WE HAVE NO CAPTION
FOR THIS KIND OF MISFORTUNE

2073
01:18:09,919 --> 01:18:11,854
Hyun-suk!

2074
01:18:11,921 --> 01:18:12,755
Are you okay?

2075
01:18:12,821 --> 01:18:15,057
I'll make do with what I have.

2076
01:18:15,124 --> 01:18:16,625
He looks devastated.

2077
01:18:16,692 --> 01:18:18,694
Jong-won has a slight smile on his face.

2078
01:18:18,761 --> 01:18:20,563
-Jong-won smiles.
-"Yes!"

2079
01:18:20,629 --> 01:18:23,232
-I saw it too.
-"Yes!"

2080
01:18:23,299 --> 01:18:24,500
Hyun-suk is over.

2081
01:18:24,567 --> 01:18:25,834
WILL HE GO DOWN LIKE THIS?

2082
01:18:25,901 --> 01:18:27,503
He's grilling them again.

2083
01:18:27,570 --> 01:18:29,338
He's fanning the flames.

2084
01:18:29,905 --> 01:18:32,508
THE SMELL OF GRILLED FALL GIZZARD SHAD
FILLS THE STUDIO

2085
01:18:32,575 --> 01:18:35,344
It's so good that
it brings back runaway daughters-in-law.

2086
01:18:35,411 --> 01:18:36,979
-One minute left.
-One minute!

2087
01:18:37,046 --> 01:18:38,647
Hurry up.

2088
01:18:38,714 --> 01:18:40,549
I think he's given up
on molecular gastronomy.

2089
01:18:41,150 --> 01:18:45,521
Chef & My Fridge<i> is a place for</i>
<i>professional chefs to show their caliber.</i>

2090
01:18:46,388 --> 01:18:47,790
Liquid nitrogen.

2091
01:18:47,957 --> 01:18:49,692
I WON'T BE A PRO IF I BACK DOWN LIKE THIS!

2092
01:18:50,159 --> 01:18:51,660
HE FAILED WITH NITROGEN GAS, BUT…

2093
01:18:51,727 --> 01:18:53,495
HE STILL HAS LIQUID NITROGEN!

2094
01:18:53,562 --> 01:18:54,897
What's that?

2095
01:18:54,964 --> 01:18:57,967
-You can save it!
-It makes the temperature very low.

2096
01:18:58,033 --> 01:18:59,068
Down to minus 196°C.

2097
01:18:59,134 --> 01:19:01,403
-Minus 196°C?
-You'll freeze your hands.

2098
01:19:01,470 --> 01:19:04,440
-It makes everything freeze, right?
-Yes,

2099
01:19:04,506 --> 01:19:06,775
-He pours it in right there.
-In there?

2100
01:19:06,842 --> 01:19:09,445
ADDING STRAWBERRY CREAM TO LIQUID NITROGEN

2101
01:19:09,511 --> 01:19:11,814
He wanted to squeeze it out,
but it's just going in there.

2102
01:19:11,880 --> 01:19:14,350
-It freezes when it touches it, right?
-Right away.

2103
01:19:14,416 --> 01:19:17,152
-It's minus 190°C.
-No, minus 196°C.

2104
01:19:17,219 --> 01:19:19,021
That liquid must start freezing.

2105
01:19:19,088 --> 01:19:21,690
INSTANTLY BECAME STRAWBERRY ICE CREAM

2106
01:19:21,757 --> 01:19:23,959
-Is it safe to eat that?
-Of course.

2107
01:19:24,526 --> 01:19:25,594
He just asked…

2108
01:19:25,928 --> 01:19:27,196
WHAT DID YOU MEAN BY THAT?

2109
01:19:27,263 --> 01:19:31,734
Sang-uk is in fear for his safety.

2110
01:19:31,800 --> 01:19:34,036
-"Is it safe to eat that?"
-It is.

2111
01:19:35,537 --> 01:19:39,508
13. FINELY GRIND STRAWBERRY ICE CREAM
FROZEN WITH LIQUID NITROGEN

2112
01:19:40,476 --> 01:19:42,478
Look at Hyun-suk's long face.

2113
01:19:43,379 --> 01:19:44,947
They must've been heavy too.

2114
01:19:45,781 --> 01:19:48,984
-And it was his best move.
-Seriously.

2115
01:19:49,051 --> 01:19:50,052
It was his best weapon.

2116
01:19:50,119 --> 01:19:52,721
He wanted to use nitrogen,
but it wasn't here.

2117
01:19:53,756 --> 01:19:56,291
-Thirty seconds.
-Thirty seconds left.

2118
01:19:56,358 --> 01:19:58,627
Hyun-suk faces something unexpected.

2119
01:19:59,962 --> 01:20:02,097
Is there no nitrogen?

2120
01:20:02,164 --> 01:20:03,799
Or has too much time passed?

2121
01:20:03,866 --> 01:20:06,502
BITTERLY WHIPS IT BY HAND…

2122
01:20:07,236 --> 01:20:08,370
Fifteen seconds.

2123
01:20:08,437 --> 01:20:10,472
Unfortunately, time keeps ticking.

2124
01:20:10,539 --> 01:20:11,807
Choi Hyun-suk.

2125
01:20:11,874 --> 01:20:14,543
He was once the leading chef
in molecular gastronomy.

2126
01:20:14,610 --> 01:20:16,812
And now, five.

2127
01:20:17,546 --> 01:20:20,082
A MEAL MADE WITH SEASONAL INGREDIENTS,
BLUE CRABS AND GIZZARD SHADS

2128
01:20:20,149 --> 01:20:23,285
-Chef Jong-won finishes.
-Chef Jong-won finishes.

2129
01:20:23,352 --> 01:20:24,687
-Hyun-suk.
-Ten seconds.

2130
01:20:24,753 --> 01:20:27,790
Hyun-suk, the man who once led
the world of molecular gastronomy.

2131
01:20:28,257 --> 01:20:31,994
THE CHEF WILL SERVE THE MOLECULES

2132
01:20:32,061 --> 01:20:34,196
-Two. One.
-It's like ice cream.

2133
01:20:34,263 --> 01:20:35,731
Time's up.

2134
01:20:36,999 --> 01:20:38,233
-He's done.
-Hyun-suk's done.

2135
01:20:38,300 --> 01:20:40,235
Yes, Chef Hyun-suk's done.

2136
01:20:40,302 --> 01:20:42,104
Just serve it like that.
You had no choice.

2137
01:20:42,171 --> 01:20:43,972
-It's looking dangerous here.
-Look at that.

2138
01:20:44,039 --> 01:20:47,242
Jong-won must've felt bad.
He came over to wipe the dish.

2139
01:20:47,309 --> 01:20:48,877
STAY STRONG, HYUN-SUK!

2140
01:20:48,944 --> 01:20:50,212
That looks good.

2141
01:20:50,779 --> 01:20:52,348
It's such a shame.

2142
01:20:52,414 --> 01:20:54,416
How sad. I wanted to see him do that.

2143
01:20:54,483 --> 01:20:57,653
Sang-uk wanted to see
molecular gastronomy in the making.

2144
01:20:57,720 --> 01:21:01,023
But for now,
they all finished making their dishes.

2145
01:21:01,090 --> 01:21:02,891
It smells so good.

2146
01:21:04,827 --> 01:21:05,928
Huh?

2147
01:21:05,994 --> 01:21:07,529
-Wait a second. I…
-What is it?

2148
01:21:08,063 --> 01:21:10,799
I was out of it,
but I can serve what I've made, right?

2149
01:21:11,367 --> 01:21:12,267
Man, today is…

2150
01:21:12,334 --> 01:21:14,169
-Hyun-suk.
-What is it?

2151
01:21:14,236 --> 01:21:17,206
-Go ahead and serve it.
-Nitrogen got the best of him.

2152
01:21:17,272 --> 01:21:19,675
It's okay. I didn't have it,

2153
01:21:19,742 --> 01:21:23,078
but I still did my best
to make something similar.

2154
01:21:23,145 --> 01:21:24,847
Great job.

2155
01:21:24,913 --> 01:21:27,316
He almost left out the asparagus.

2156
01:21:27,383 --> 01:21:30,719
And now, they finished making
these dishes for Sang-uk.

2157
01:21:30,786 --> 01:21:32,888
First of all,

2158
01:21:32,955 --> 01:21:35,424
I think some explanation is due.

2159
01:21:35,491 --> 01:21:37,426
There was a bit of an accident.

2160
01:21:37,493 --> 01:21:40,829
Let me make this really easy.

2161
01:21:40,896 --> 01:21:42,030
So…

2162
01:21:42,097 --> 01:21:43,966
It's like diving underwater
with an oxygen tank,

2163
01:21:44,032 --> 01:21:45,467
but you couldn't breathe.

2164
01:21:46,034 --> 01:21:48,270
A NITROGEN TANK WITHOUT NITROGEN

2165
01:21:48,470 --> 01:21:49,872
LOL

2166
01:21:49,938 --> 01:21:53,342
-Although I didn't have oxygen,
-It's so funny that I'm crying.

2167
01:21:53,409 --> 01:21:55,978
…I managed to hold my breath

2168
01:21:56,044 --> 01:21:58,580
and picked up at least
a couple of abalones.

2169
01:21:58,647 --> 01:22:00,215
-Gosh.
-That's him.

2170
01:22:00,282 --> 01:22:02,751
I did my best to make that dish look
as similar as I could.

2171
01:22:02,818 --> 01:22:04,553
-He can free dive.
-But first,

2172
01:22:04,620 --> 01:22:06,989
Chef Jong-won finished cooking first.

2173
01:22:07,055 --> 01:22:09,925
-Make Gizzard Shad for His Majesty!
-His Majesty!

2174
01:22:09,992 --> 01:22:11,727
PEAR AND WHITE KIMCHI

2175
01:22:11,794 --> 01:22:14,062
GRILLED GIZZARD SHAD,
SEASONED GIZZARD SHAD

2176
01:22:15,898 --> 01:22:17,533
GEGAMJEONG

2177
01:22:18,500 --> 01:22:20,235
STONE POT RICE WITH MUSHROOM

2178
01:22:22,337 --> 01:22:25,274
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S
MAKE GIZZARD SHAD FOR HIS MAJESTY!

2179
01:22:25,340 --> 01:22:27,676
<i>Make Gizzard Shad!</i>

2180
01:22:28,310 --> 01:22:29,912
SERVE TO HIS MAJESTY

2181
01:22:29,978 --> 01:22:32,181
-He should…
-Tread backward.

2182
01:22:32,247 --> 01:22:33,649
-Don't show your bums.
-No?

2183
01:22:33,715 --> 01:22:35,884
-When you walk away.
-When I leave.

2184
01:22:37,152 --> 01:22:38,187
That's it.

2185
01:22:39,888 --> 01:22:41,356
IMPERTINENCE!

2186
01:22:41,423 --> 01:22:42,524
You almost killed me.

2187
01:22:44,393 --> 01:22:45,294
Thank you.

2188
01:22:45,360 --> 01:22:48,130
That kimchi
is stuffed inside a piece of pear.

2189
01:22:48,197 --> 01:22:50,165
-Let me make it easier to eat.
-In pear?

2190
01:22:50,232 --> 01:22:51,533
WHITE KIMCHI STUFFED INSIDE PEAR

2191
01:22:51,600 --> 01:22:54,069
<i>He cut up a piece of pear</i>
<i>and stuffed white kimchi inside.</i>

2192
01:22:54,136 --> 01:22:55,437
<i>Pears get sweeter in fall.</i>

2193
01:22:56,605 --> 01:22:59,608
I guess King Jeongjo liked white kimchi.

2194
01:22:59,675 --> 01:23:00,809
Let me open the pot for you.

2195
01:23:00,876 --> 01:23:02,845
The rice with mushrooms.

2196
01:23:02,911 --> 01:23:04,179
-Wow.
-Wow.

2197
01:23:04,246 --> 01:23:05,581
-Does it look good?
-It does.

2198
01:23:05,647 --> 01:23:08,283
-It smells so good.
-It smells wonderful.

2199
01:23:08,350 --> 01:23:12,120
You can serve yourself with a rice paddle.

2200
01:23:14,223 --> 01:23:16,692
This is like a royal meal for the king.

2201
01:23:16,758 --> 01:23:17,960
He said he loved fresh rice.

2202
01:23:20,128 --> 01:23:22,297
Rice with mushrooms and bracken.

2203
01:23:22,364 --> 01:23:23,532
-It's the real deal.
-Yes.

2204
01:23:23,599 --> 01:23:27,436
His grandmother often cooked
with mushrooms and bracken.

2205
01:23:27,503 --> 01:23:29,071
-It looks so good.
-It looks glossy

2206
01:23:29,137 --> 01:23:31,874
and has good consistency, thanks to kelp.

2207
01:23:31,940 --> 01:23:32,875
-Gosh.
-All right.

2208
01:23:32,941 --> 01:23:33,842
SNIFF

2209
01:23:33,909 --> 01:23:34,743
AMAZED

2210
01:23:35,143 --> 01:23:37,513
-Does it smell good? So good?
-It smells so good.

2211
01:23:37,579 --> 01:23:40,282
And now, please try it with <i>gegamjeong.</i>

2212
01:23:40,349 --> 01:23:41,783
-Thank you.
-Sure.

2213
01:23:42,217 --> 01:23:43,151
FIRST, GEGAMJEONG

2214
01:23:43,218 --> 01:23:45,954
<i>-Gegamjeong.</i>
<i>-Gegamjeong. </i>What's the soup like?

2215
01:23:48,390 --> 01:23:49,258
Gosh.

2216
01:23:50,926 --> 01:23:53,128
-Gosh.
-"Gosh."

2217
01:23:53,195 --> 01:23:54,997
It's so refreshing.

2218
01:23:56,198 --> 01:23:58,267
-And clean.
-And clean.

2219
01:23:58,333 --> 01:24:00,435
-And spicy. It's great.
-And spicy.

2220
01:24:00,502 --> 01:24:01,837
-I see.
-I would like that too.

2221
01:24:01,904 --> 01:24:03,472
And stone pot rice with mushrooms.

2222
01:24:04,139 --> 01:24:06,441
-So there's no sauce for the rice.
-No.

2223
01:24:07,175 --> 01:24:09,044
-It's hot.
-It's still hot.

2224
01:24:09,111 --> 01:24:10,546
-It's hot.
-The stone pot rice.

2225
01:24:10,612 --> 01:24:12,347
-Give him that thing.
-Liquid nitrogen.

2226
01:24:12,414 --> 01:24:14,416
-Liquid nitrogen.
-Liquid nitrogen.

2227
01:24:14,483 --> 01:24:15,417
Help him cool down.

2228
01:24:15,484 --> 01:24:16,418
Do you want it?

2229
01:24:17,452 --> 01:24:18,720
-Cool it down.
-Do you want it?

2230
01:24:18,787 --> 01:24:20,589
-No?
-He almost cried.

2231
01:24:20,656 --> 01:24:23,325
-We had to cool it down.
-It'd freeze the rice in an instant.

2232
01:24:23,392 --> 01:24:26,094
-It's minus 196°C.
-Cool it down.

2233
01:24:26,161 --> 01:24:27,329
All right. Take your time.

2234
01:24:28,530 --> 01:24:30,098
A sip of <i>gegamjeong</i> soup.

2235
01:24:31,199 --> 01:24:35,804
He filled the shell, grilled it,
and then put it in the soup, right?

2236
01:24:35,871 --> 01:24:39,007
-Crab meat, beef, tofu, and bean sprouts.
-Try that too.

2237
01:24:39,074 --> 01:24:40,976
First of all, the soup is so clean.

2238
01:24:41,643 --> 01:24:42,945
Is it something you like?

2239
01:24:43,011 --> 01:24:46,348
I just thought,
"I should've gotten drunk last night."

2240
01:24:46,915 --> 01:24:48,116
TASTES LIKE A HANGOVER CURE

2241
01:24:48,183 --> 01:24:49,084
That means he's serious.

2242
01:24:49,151 --> 01:24:50,686
And the filling.

2243
01:24:50,752 --> 01:24:53,188
-It was inside the shell.
-Yes.

2244
01:24:53,922 --> 01:24:56,024
Bean sprouts, tofu, and beef.

2245
01:24:56,091 --> 01:24:57,025
THE FROWN OF TRUTH

2246
01:24:57,092 --> 01:24:58,026
Mmm.

2247
01:24:59,695 --> 01:25:00,996
It's so good.

2248
01:25:01,063 --> 01:25:02,497
It's that good?

2249
01:25:02,564 --> 01:25:05,467
It tastes similar to a dumpling.

2250
01:25:05,534 --> 01:25:06,768
<i>It's made with bean sprouts, beef,</i>

2251
01:25:06,835 --> 01:25:08,136
<i>-and tofu.</i>
<i>-It's a bit…</i>

2252
01:25:08,203 --> 01:25:09,137
<i>Like North Korean dumplings.</i>

2253
01:25:09,204 --> 01:25:10,706
-The dumpling filling?
-Like that.

2254
01:25:10,772 --> 01:25:12,407
-Kimchi.
-White kimchi.

2255
01:25:12,474 --> 01:25:13,909
With some pear.

2256
01:25:14,476 --> 01:25:16,011
YUM

2257
01:25:18,480 --> 01:25:19,815
THAT HAS TO BE GOOD…

2258
01:25:19,881 --> 01:25:22,184
It tastes sweeter with pear.

2259
01:25:22,250 --> 01:25:24,686
-It's refreshing and sweet.
-Refreshing and sweet.

2260
01:25:26,355 --> 01:25:27,422
It's good.

2261
01:25:27,489 --> 01:25:28,457
Your favorite, gizzard shad.

2262
01:25:28,523 --> 01:25:30,392
-How will you eat it?
-This is…

2263
01:25:30,459 --> 01:25:31,893
-Eat it whole.
-Whole?

2264
01:25:32,594 --> 01:25:35,197
He put lots of work into grilling them.

2265
01:25:35,263 --> 01:25:37,199
And those come with bones.

2266
01:25:38,867 --> 01:25:40,802
That's how you eat it.

2267
01:25:40,869 --> 01:25:42,504
EATS HALF AT ONCE

2268
01:25:43,071 --> 01:25:43,972
Wow.

2269
01:25:44,706 --> 01:25:46,541
AUTOMATICALLY AMAZED

2270
01:25:46,608 --> 01:25:47,943
Of the sounds he made,

2271
01:25:48,010 --> 01:25:50,245
-that one sounded the most excited.
-You cut off the gills too, right?

2272
01:25:50,312 --> 01:25:52,047
<i>-So neatly.</i>
<i>-It was bound to happen.</i>

2273
01:25:52,114 --> 01:25:55,083
Sang-uk said he really loves
eating gizzard shad in fall.

2274
01:25:55,150 --> 01:25:56,451
It's so fatty.

2275
01:25:58,420 --> 01:26:00,022
Very rich.

2276
01:26:00,088 --> 01:26:01,890
It's a lump of omega-3.

2277
01:26:01,957 --> 01:26:04,393
-And it tastes very savory.
-I see.

2278
01:26:04,459 --> 01:26:06,962
I also like chewing on the bones.

2279
01:26:08,063 --> 01:26:09,398
Is there one more? I want one too.

2280
01:26:09,464 --> 01:26:13,268
He eats it whole and takes a bite.

2281
01:26:13,335 --> 01:26:15,904
Top the the rice
with seasoned fish and have it together.

2282
01:26:15,971 --> 01:26:16,905
-This one?
-Yes.

2283
01:26:16,972 --> 01:26:18,507
That one looks good.

2284
01:26:18,573 --> 01:26:19,775
Put it on top like a side dish.

2285
01:26:19,841 --> 01:26:21,143
EAT IT WITH RICE!

2286
01:26:21,943 --> 01:26:23,712
He did this in 15 minutes?

2287
01:26:23,779 --> 01:26:26,748
So Chef Do-hee
really does have quick hands.

2288
01:26:26,815 --> 01:26:28,316
He did a great job at chopping.

2289
01:26:28,383 --> 01:26:30,452
The best swordsman in Joseon.

2290
01:26:31,119 --> 01:26:34,056
-It's all about chopping.
-Great job with your knife.

2291
01:26:35,223 --> 01:26:36,758
-It smells so good.
-It does?

2292
01:26:37,626 --> 01:26:38,460
Wow.

2293
01:26:38,527 --> 01:26:40,262
A BIG BITE

2294
01:26:42,831 --> 01:26:44,499
It's all about how it's cut.

2295
01:26:44,566 --> 01:26:47,102
THE FROWN OF TRUTH COMES OUT RIGHT AWAY

2296
01:26:47,169 --> 01:26:48,537
The frown of truth.

2297
01:26:48,603 --> 01:26:49,838
TWO THUMBS UP

2298
01:26:49,905 --> 01:26:52,407
-It's so delicious. I mean it.
-It's that good?

2299
01:26:52,474 --> 01:26:54,643
-He eats well.
-This is really good.

2300
01:26:54,710 --> 01:26:57,813
-He did a great job slicing it.
-How are you so good?

2301
01:26:57,879 --> 01:26:59,314
He's so good with his knife.

2302
01:26:59,381 --> 01:27:00,949
The best swordsman in Joseon.

2303
01:27:01,016 --> 01:27:03,585
The best swordsman in Joseon, Woo Do-hee!

2304
01:27:03,652 --> 01:27:05,821
Poong might use him in the next episode.

2305
01:27:05,887 --> 01:27:07,456
In the next episode,

2306
01:27:07,522 --> 01:27:10,992
-I want to use him too!
-Not his mom, but Woo Do-hee!

2307
01:27:11,059 --> 01:27:12,494
I didn't expect much

2308
01:27:12,561 --> 01:27:15,030
-from the cold gizzard shad.
-Yes, the seasoned gizzard shad.

2309
01:27:15,097 --> 01:27:17,532
But it's really savory.

2310
01:27:17,599 --> 01:27:19,401
-It's the perilla powder.
-And he used perilla oil.

2311
01:27:19,468 --> 01:27:20,635
<i>-Perilla powder.</i>
<i>-Perilla powder.</i>

2312
01:27:20,702 --> 01:27:23,271
<i>He has built a good sense of taste.</i>

2313
01:27:23,338 --> 01:27:27,109
-This is so good.
-If he can taste the savoriness…

2314
01:27:27,175 --> 01:27:30,445
He removed the bones from the flesh.

2315
01:27:30,512 --> 01:27:33,315
<i>-There are no bones?</i>
<i>-Right, I can't feel it.</i>

2316
01:27:33,381 --> 01:27:34,549
-Really?
-That's why I…

2317
01:27:34,616 --> 01:27:36,251
The best swordsmanship in Joseon.

2318
01:27:37,486 --> 01:27:40,989
-I want to use him too.
-That secret weapon worked.

2319
01:27:41,056 --> 01:27:42,924
Can you stick around
until the next episode?

2320
01:27:42,991 --> 01:27:45,861
HE'LL HAVE TO STAY FOR ANOTHER EPISODE

2321
01:27:45,927 --> 01:27:47,028
This mix

2322
01:27:48,563 --> 01:27:51,399
-is perfect.
-It must be the best.

2323
01:27:51,466 --> 01:27:53,902
This is already good,
but it's even better with this one.

2324
01:27:55,470 --> 01:27:56,772
DRINKING BY THE BOWL

2325
01:27:57,606 --> 01:27:58,974
A PERFECT FINISH

2326
01:27:59,040 --> 01:28:00,041
All right.

2327
01:28:00,108 --> 01:28:03,378
Next, please try Chef Hyun-suk's dish.

2328
01:28:03,845 --> 01:28:07,315
SCALLOP APPETIZER

2329
01:28:07,382 --> 01:28:10,252
AND STEAMED CUTLASSFISH
WITH BELATED ASPARAGUS PIECES

2330
01:28:12,354 --> 01:28:14,723
ALSO, NITROGEN SAUCE(?) WITHOUT NITROGEN

2331
01:28:15,223 --> 01:28:18,293
CHEF CHOI HYUN-SUK'S
MOLECULAR GASTRONOMY IS EASY, ISN'T IT?

2332
01:28:18,360 --> 01:28:21,096
It was
Molecular Gastronomy Is Easy, Isn't It?

2333
01:28:21,163 --> 01:28:24,266
-It was hard.
-Molecular gastronomy is hard.

2334
01:28:24,332 --> 01:28:25,467
It's so hard.

2335
01:28:25,534 --> 01:28:27,636
Molecular gastronomy is really tough.

2336
01:28:27,702 --> 01:28:29,971
-It is.
-He brought something with him.

2337
01:28:30,038 --> 01:28:33,942
A man who brought glory to our nation
is here, so I'll finish the dish too.

2338
01:28:34,009 --> 01:28:35,443
It's for the scallops.

2339
01:28:35,510 --> 01:28:37,779
POWDERED SAUCE
FOR HIM TO EAT WITH THE APPETIZER

2340
01:28:38,113 --> 01:28:40,148
It's a molecular gastronomy recipe.

2341
01:28:40,215 --> 01:28:42,851
<i>-Yes.</i>
<i>-He used liquid nitrogen.</i>

2342
01:28:42,918 --> 01:28:45,720
<i>So it turned solid.</i>

2343
01:28:45,787 --> 01:28:49,925
He could make the powdered sauce
thanks to liquid nitrogen.

2344
01:28:49,991 --> 01:28:54,863
-Espuma. Strawberry jam and cream.
-That's strawberry jam and cream.

2345
01:28:54,930 --> 01:28:58,033
Let me serve the pine nut espuma.

2346
01:28:58,099 --> 01:28:59,768
It should've been powdery too.

2347
01:28:59,835 --> 01:29:03,572
-No, it would've been foam.
-It should've turned into foam,

2348
01:29:03,638 --> 01:29:04,940
so I whipped it myself.

2349
01:29:06,441 --> 01:29:07,475
Whoa. Your thigh.

2350
01:29:08,043 --> 01:29:09,110
-Goodness.
-I'm fine.

2351
01:29:09,177 --> 01:29:11,079
How can you drop this on his thigh?

2352
01:29:11,146 --> 01:29:13,715
-Your Majesty!
-I apologize!

2353
01:29:13,782 --> 01:29:16,551
Goodness, how did this happen?

2354
01:29:16,618 --> 01:29:19,955
-Chef Hyun-suk makes lots of mistakes.
-Mistakes.

2355
01:29:20,021 --> 01:29:21,022
I'll kill you.

2356
01:29:21,089 --> 01:29:24,125
-You made the mistake.
-He's already sensitive.

2357
01:29:24,192 --> 01:29:25,994
-He's dying inside.
-Why would you kill me?

2358
01:29:26,061 --> 01:29:28,096
-He's sensitive today.
-Come on.

2359
01:29:28,163 --> 01:29:30,999
He dropped sauce on his thigh.

2360
01:29:31,066 --> 01:29:32,200
What should he eat first?

2361
01:29:32,267 --> 01:29:34,836
Use a spoon to scoop the powdered sauce

2362
01:29:34,903 --> 01:29:36,471
and eat it with the scallops.

2363
01:29:36,538 --> 01:29:38,940
-And the eggplant.
-Eggplant too.

2364
01:29:40,208 --> 01:29:42,510
-It's just…
-It just looks like powder.

2365
01:29:43,278 --> 01:29:45,280
A HEARTY BITE

2366
01:29:50,619 --> 01:29:51,620
Nice try.

2367
01:29:51,686 --> 01:29:54,723
Press the button, Your Majesty.

2368
01:29:54,789 --> 01:29:56,258
Nice try.

2369
01:29:57,058 --> 01:29:58,193
It's done.

2370
01:29:58,260 --> 01:30:00,495
I've never had anything like this.

2371
01:30:00,562 --> 01:30:02,964
I don't know how to explain this.

2372
01:30:03,031 --> 01:30:04,766
What's the sauce like?

2373
01:30:04,833 --> 01:30:07,535
It's similar to yogurt.

2374
01:30:07,602 --> 01:30:09,304
-Yes, with the cream.
-Yogurt.

2375
01:30:09,371 --> 01:30:13,141
It's a mix of cream, strawberry jam,
and lemon juice, so it can taste like it.

2376
01:30:13,208 --> 01:30:14,476
It has sourness and sweetness.

2377
01:30:14,542 --> 01:30:15,744
UNDERSTOOD

2378
01:30:16,077 --> 01:30:17,212
AND NOW, SHALL I BEGIN?

2379
01:30:19,481 --> 01:30:20,482
He's never had it before.

2380
01:30:20,548 --> 01:30:21,950
It's the world of fine dining.

2381
01:30:22,417 --> 01:30:23,251
THE LEFTOVER SAUCE

2382
01:30:23,318 --> 01:30:25,587
He finished everything already.

2383
01:30:25,654 --> 01:30:28,623
You asked us, "Is it safe to eat that?"

2384
01:30:29,791 --> 01:30:32,060
It's perfectly edible.

2385
01:30:32,127 --> 01:30:33,795
-It's good.
-It is?

2386
01:30:33,862 --> 01:30:35,030
-But it's a first.
-This one…

2387
01:30:35,096 --> 01:30:36,998
-…is the appetizer, right?
-Right.

2388
01:30:37,065 --> 01:30:39,668
-It's the appetizer.
-You're exactly right. It's the appetizer.

2389
01:30:39,734 --> 01:30:43,538
It's a new experience for Sang-uk.
Next, cutlassfish.

2390
01:30:43,605 --> 01:30:47,042
-Eat it with the espuma and the soy sauce.
-All at once.

2391
01:30:47,108 --> 01:30:51,012
And the belated asparagus.

2392
01:30:51,079 --> 01:30:54,349
-He turned it into a fillet, zero bones.
-It's a fillet.

2393
01:30:54,416 --> 01:30:57,485
-He loves seafood, cutlassfish.
-That's the main dish.

2394
01:30:57,552 --> 01:30:59,587
This is the main dish.

2395
01:31:00,922 --> 01:31:02,957
A BIG BITE!

2396
01:31:03,024 --> 01:31:04,059
Sang-uk the big eater.

2397
01:31:06,461 --> 01:31:07,696
GASP

2398
01:31:09,297 --> 01:31:11,299
You didn't need liquid nitrogen to cook.

2399
01:31:11,366 --> 01:31:13,068
It's so good as is.

2400
01:31:14,536 --> 01:31:17,172
You could've served it like that.

2401
01:31:17,238 --> 01:31:19,107
He stopped breathing for a moment.

2402
01:31:19,407 --> 01:31:21,276
CONTINUES TO EAT

2403
01:31:21,343 --> 01:31:22,944
-It's good.
-The sauce looks good.

2404
01:31:23,011 --> 01:31:26,014
-The sauce must be good.
-The sauce must be good. And nutty.

2405
01:31:26,081 --> 01:31:28,149
And the saltiness from the soy sauce.
It looks good.

2406
01:31:28,216 --> 01:31:32,220
It's a mix of nutty pine nuts
and the sweet and salty soy sauce.

2407
01:31:32,287 --> 01:31:33,355
<i>Sweet and salty.</i>

2408
01:31:34,155 --> 01:31:35,623
YUM♥

2409
01:31:35,690 --> 01:31:38,526
It looks like he'll finish everything.

2410
01:31:38,593 --> 01:31:39,894
Yes, that's his last bite.

2411
01:31:41,429 --> 01:31:42,997
-He puts everything up.
-It's good.

2412
01:31:45,633 --> 01:31:46,668
-It looks good.
-Whoa.

2413
01:31:46,735 --> 01:31:48,403
IN ONE BITE!

2414
01:31:48,803 --> 01:31:50,839
GASP…

2415
01:31:51,573 --> 01:31:52,874
ONLY TOOK TWO BITES

2416
01:31:52,941 --> 01:31:55,210
It was this big,
and he finished it in two bites.

2417
01:31:55,276 --> 01:31:56,878
We just gave it to him.

2418
01:31:56,945 --> 01:31:58,213
Was it cooked well?

2419
01:31:58,279 --> 01:31:59,714
-Very well.
-Very well.

2420
01:31:59,781 --> 01:32:01,883
-And no bones?
-No bones.

2421
01:32:03,118 --> 01:32:05,487
This has a hint of Japanese cuisine.

2422
01:32:05,553 --> 01:32:06,588
Yes.

2423
01:32:06,654 --> 01:32:08,757
But at the same time,
I can taste pine nuts,

2424
01:32:08,823 --> 01:32:12,794
-so it feels like a fusion dish.
-Like fusion Korean cuisine.

2425
01:32:12,861 --> 01:32:16,297
And that big bite of cutlassfish

2426
01:32:17,499 --> 01:32:19,434
hit me with abundant flavors.

2427
01:32:19,501 --> 01:32:21,036
-I love it.
-I see.

2428
01:32:21,102 --> 01:32:24,839
It's the battle
between the familiar and the novel.

2429
01:32:24,906 --> 01:32:27,242
That soy sauce.

2430
01:32:27,308 --> 01:32:28,676
It must be good.

2431
01:32:28,743 --> 01:32:30,578
Until the last drop.

2432
01:32:30,645 --> 01:32:32,781
All right, now.

2433
01:32:32,847 --> 01:32:34,983
-Yes.
-Sang-uk.

2434
01:32:35,050 --> 01:32:37,318
-But they were both so good.
-Please get ready.

2435
01:32:37,385 --> 01:32:39,287
<i>Press one for Jong-won,</i>

2436
01:32:39,354 --> 01:32:40,522
<i>and two for Hyun-suk.</i>

2437
01:32:41,423 --> 01:32:43,224
Press the button!

2438
01:32:43,291 --> 01:32:45,260
-Did you?
-I did.

2439
01:32:45,326 --> 01:32:47,228
Let's find out the result.

2440
01:32:48,263 --> 01:32:49,531
Who did Sang-uk choose?

2441
01:32:49,597 --> 01:32:51,399
-Seasonal SeaVengers.
-Seasonal SeaVengers.

2442
01:32:51,466 --> 01:32:54,002
Will Hyun-suk of the 190 Club win?

2443
01:32:54,068 --> 01:32:58,907
Or will Jong-won of the 180 Club
take Hyun-suk down?

2444
01:32:58,973 --> 01:33:02,077
Who did Sang-uk choose?

2445
01:33:02,143 --> 01:33:04,846
Who's the winner?

2446
01:33:06,147 --> 01:33:07,682
SON JONG-WON WINS

2447
01:33:07,749 --> 01:33:09,150
Son Jong-won wins.

2448
01:33:09,517 --> 01:33:10,518
MAKE GIZZARD SHAD FOR HIS MAJESTY! WINS

2449
01:33:10,585 --> 01:33:13,655
Jong-won wins.

2450
01:33:14,422 --> 01:33:17,092
That was just…

2451
01:33:17,158 --> 01:33:21,095
He abandoned the 190 Club
and chose Chef Jong-won.

2452
01:33:22,697 --> 01:33:25,767
It already looked delicious.

2453
01:33:25,834 --> 01:33:29,571
Rice, soup, fish, and kimchi

2454
01:33:29,637 --> 01:33:31,906
already looked very delicious.

2455
01:33:31,973 --> 01:33:33,575
And the gizzard shad

2456
01:33:34,142 --> 01:33:35,577
tasted richer as I kept chewing on it.

2457
01:33:36,244 --> 01:33:37,445
<i>I loved that.</i>

2458
01:33:37,512 --> 01:33:38,713
<i>And this seasoned fish.</i>

2459
01:33:39,414 --> 01:33:41,883
It went perfectly with rice.

2460
01:33:41,950 --> 01:33:44,619
-Chef Do-hee chopped everything.
-Korean cuisine.

2461
01:33:44,686 --> 01:33:48,256
The best swordsman in Joseon, Woo Do-hee.

2462
01:33:48,323 --> 01:33:50,158
I'm glad he sharpened the knife.

2463
01:33:50,758 --> 01:33:51,960
I see.

2464
01:33:52,026 --> 01:33:53,328
Any thoughts on today?

2465
01:33:53,394 --> 01:33:55,530
It was nice to be here
without any pressure.

2466
01:33:55,597 --> 01:33:57,131
I had fun watching everything

2467
01:33:57,198 --> 01:33:58,800
and helping out a little.

2468
01:33:58,867 --> 01:34:00,702
It was a great experience.

2469
01:34:00,768 --> 01:34:04,205
Was the gas tank not recoverable?

2470
01:34:04,272 --> 01:34:06,374
There was no nitrogen.

2471
01:34:07,242 --> 01:34:09,511
"Cannitrogen lose" meant
that he couldn't use nitrogen.

2472
01:34:09,577 --> 01:34:11,713
It wasn't "cannot lose."

2473
01:34:11,779 --> 01:34:13,248
I cannitrogen lose.

2474
01:34:13,314 --> 01:34:15,650
-He cannitrogen lose.
-Cannitrogen lose.

2475
01:34:15,717 --> 01:34:18,219
-Couldn't use nitrogen.
-No nitrogen.

2476
01:34:18,286 --> 01:34:21,022
The secret weapon was the decisive point.

2477
01:34:21,089 --> 01:34:22,190
It was my fault.

2478
01:34:22,257 --> 01:34:25,894
Let me invite you to my restaurant
so I can serve you molecular gastronomy.

2479
01:34:25,960 --> 01:34:27,328
-I see.
-Thank you.

2480
01:34:27,395 --> 01:34:28,563
Just in case,

2481
01:34:28,630 --> 01:34:32,267
after this round, you should check
if the gas can has a canine mark.

2482
01:34:32,333 --> 01:34:34,169
-Yes.
-The canine incidents.

2483
01:34:34,235 --> 01:34:37,171
His staff must've done something to it.

2484
01:34:37,238 --> 01:34:38,273
DO NOT ENTER

2485
01:34:38,473 --> 01:34:40,742
We're here with Mi-mi and Daniel.

2486
01:34:40,808 --> 01:34:42,177
That foul play was why I won.

2487
01:34:42,243 --> 01:34:43,745
So is your arm okay?

2488
01:34:43,811 --> 01:34:45,146
I was way too excited.

2489
01:34:45,213 --> 01:34:46,514
THAT KIND OF PAIN WAS NOTHING

2490
01:34:46,581 --> 01:34:48,816
You probably received
lots of calls after the game.

2491
01:34:49,050 --> 01:34:50,218
HIS BUDDY, JONATHAN!

2492
01:34:50,285 --> 01:34:53,121
Daniel and I grew up together
before I went to Gwangju.

2493
01:34:53,188 --> 01:34:54,022
Were you a good runner?

2494
01:34:54,088 --> 01:34:56,024
We were about the same.

2495
01:34:56,090 --> 01:34:58,226
-Was he really?
-Do I have to respond?

2496
01:34:58,426 --> 01:34:59,928
TUNE IN TO WATCH THE CONGOLESE CHEMISTRY!

2497
01:34:59,994 --> 01:35:01,429
I often went to a homestyle diner.

2498
01:35:01,496 --> 01:35:03,565
I loved <i>doenjang-jjigae.</i>

2499
01:35:03,631 --> 01:35:05,099
I like to dip it in grain syrup.

2500
01:35:05,433 --> 01:35:07,669
DEFINITELY INTO KOREAN CUISINE

2501
01:35:07,902 --> 01:35:09,037
MI-MI DOESN'T LIKE VEGETABLES?

2502
01:35:09,370 --> 01:35:11,906
ABSOLUTELY NO TO GREEN FOOD!

2503
01:35:11,973 --> 01:35:13,141
I don't even eat watermelon.

2504
01:35:13,207 --> 01:35:14,742
-Do you only eat gold kiwi?
-Yes!

2505
01:35:15,009 --> 01:35:16,177
FOR MI-MI, THE GREEN FOOD HATER,

2506
01:35:16,244 --> 01:35:18,580
THE CHEFS MAKE A PERFECT DISH

2507
01:35:18,646 --> 01:35:20,148
Isn't that green?

2508
01:35:20,215 --> 01:35:21,149
I'm using just these two.

2509
01:35:21,215 --> 01:35:22,217
The cutting board is green.

2510
01:35:22,417 --> 01:35:23,818
THROWING THE BOARD OUT

2511
01:35:24,052 --> 01:35:25,486
NO GREEN ZONE(?)

2512
01:35:25,653 --> 01:35:26,988
ONE MATCH TO PLEASE HER

2513
01:35:27,155 --> 01:35:28,723
He says <i>tteok</i> is his life.

2514
01:35:28,990 --> 01:35:29,824
AND HIS SECRET WEAPON?

2515
01:35:30,091 --> 01:35:30,925
HE BROUGHT THIS?

2516
01:35:31,192 --> 01:35:32,627
AND KIM POONG'S LEGENDARY WEAPON

2517
01:35:32,894 --> 01:35:34,495
BEST SWORDSMAN IN JOSEON, WOO DO-HEE

2518
01:35:34,562 --> 01:35:36,698
-Why are you back?
-He didn't leave?

2519
01:35:36,898 --> 01:35:38,399
THE CHEFS ARE READY FOR DANIEL

2520
01:35:38,466 --> 01:35:40,969
Wait, that's too tiny.

2521
01:35:41,035 --> 01:35:43,037
How are you so good
at making such thin slices?

2522
01:35:43,238 --> 01:35:45,006
SCOLDED FOR DOING A GOOD JOB(?)

2523
01:35:45,240 --> 01:35:47,742
POONG MAKES YET ANOTHER DARK DISH

2524
01:35:48,176 --> 01:35:49,577
WHAT IS THIS?

2525
01:35:49,811 --> 01:35:51,613
A NEW POONGPHOBIA PATIENT!

2526
01:35:51,813 --> 01:35:53,581
WILL KIM POONG WORK HIS MAGIC AGAIN?

2527
01:35:53,748 --> 01:35:55,550
<i>Will Poong show his magic?</i>

2528
01:35:57,485 --> 01:35:58,319
I'll leave.

2529
01:35:58,386 --> 01:36:01,756
Wait, no. Don't go. I feel nervous!

2530
01:36:11,099 --> 01:36:15,870
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
